RECORD	Aerosol Optics, Sky Models, LWC & Twilight — Technical Reference	Aerosol Optics, Sky Models, LWC & Twilight — Technical Reference
RECORD	Internal technical note Page 1	Internal technical note Page 1
RECORD	para 3	Aerosol Optical Properties, Sky Radiance Models,
RECORD	Cloud LWC Profiles & Twilight Illuminance	Cloud LWC Profiles & Twilight Illuminance
RECORD	para 5	Technical reference for coupling turbidity, visibility, Preetham sky domain, adiabatic liquid-water content, and civil-twilight ambient light
RECORD	para 7	1. Aerosol Optical Properties and Turbidity
RECORD	para 8	1.1 Aerosol Optical Depth (AOD / AOT)
DEFINE	para 9	Aerosol optical depth τa(λ) is the vertical integral of the aerosol extinction coefficient σext(z,λ) from the surface to the top of the atmosphere: τa(λ) = ∫0 ∞ σext(z,λ) dz
RECORD	para 10	It is dimensionless and wavelength-dependent. Typical mid-visible (550 nm) values range from <0.05 (pristine maritime / polar) through 0.1–0.3 (clean continental) to >0.5–1.0 (urban, biomass-burning or dust outbreaks). AERONET sun-photometer networks report AOD at several discrete wavelengths; satellite products (MODIS, VIIRS) provide global maps at 550 nm.
RECORD	para 11	1.2 Ångström Exponent
RECORD	para 12	Ångström (1929) observed that aerosol optical thickness follows a power law over the visible–near-IR:
DEFINE	para 14	where β is the Ångström turbidity coefficient (AOD extrapolated to 1 µm) and α is the Ångström exponent. From two wavelengths: α = − ln(τ λ1 / τλ2) / ln(λ1 / λ2)
RECORD	para 15	α is inversely related to particle size: fine-mode combustion aerosols give α ≈ 1.5–2.0; coarse dust or sea-salt give α ≈ 0–0.5; cloud droplets (very large) give α ≈ 0 (hence clouds appear white/grey). Typical continental averages lie near α ≈ 1.3. Because the power-law is only approximate, α itself varies with wavelength (“curvature”); multi-wavelength fits or second-order spectral derivatives improve size-mode separation.
RECORD	para 16	1.3 Preetham / Linke Turbidity Factor T
RECORD	para 17	The Preetham (1999) analytic sky model parameterises the atmosphere by a single scalar T (turbidity). Conceptually T is the ratio of the optical thickness of a hazy atmosphere to that of a clean Rayleigh atmosphere at the same air mass:
RECORD	Rayleigh + τaerosol) / τRayleigh	Rayleigh + τaerosol) / τRayleigh A perfectly clean atmosphere has T = 1; observed “exceptionally clear” values are ≈1.25, “very clear” ≈1.6, typical rural 2–3, urban/hazy 4–6, and heavy haze T ≥ 10. Preetham’s analytic fit was performed on data for T ∈ [2, 6]; outside that interval the model is numerically and physically unreliable (see §2).
RECORD	Internal technical note Page 2	Internal technical note Page 2
RECORD	para 22	A practical conversion from mid-visible AOD (τ550) often used in graphics and solar-energy literature is approximately: T ≈ 1 + 16 · τ550 (order-of-magnitude; see also Bodhaine 1999, Kasten–Young)
RECORD	para 23	More accurate mappings incorporate the Ångström exponent and the relative optical air mass. The Linke turbidity factor TL is a closely related quantity used in solar radiation models; linear regressions between TL and Ångström β exist for different climate regimes.
RECORD	para 24	1.4 Visibility / Meteorological Optical Range
WHEN	para 25	Koschmieder’s law relates daytime horizontal visibility V (meteorological optical range) to the horizontal extinction coefficient σext: V = (1 / σext) · ln(1 / ε) ≈ 3.912 / σext where the contrast threshold ε is conventionally taken as 0.02 (sometimes 0.05). If an exponential scale height H for aerosol extinction is assumed, the column AOD can be recovered from surface visibility:
REQUIRE	para 26	τa ≈ (3.912 / V) · H − τRayleigh Typical aerosol scale heights are 1–2 km. Visibility data are widely archived by weather services and can therefore serve as a proxy for historical AOD, although water vapour, fog and precipitation must be filtered. This relationship is the physical basis for any “visibility → turbidity” conversion used in a renderer.
RECORD	para 27	1.5 Scattering vs Extinction; Coupling to Wet Turbidity
RECORD	para 28	Extinction is the sum of scattering and absorption: σext = σsca + σabs. For most continental aerosols the single-scattering albedo ω = σsca/σext lies between 0.85 and 0.95 in the visible; strongly absorbing smoke or black-carbon plumes can drop ω below 0.7. Cloud droplets are essentially pure scatterers (ω ≈ 1) with Ångström exponent near zero.
RECORD	para 30	2. Sky Radiance Models — Validity Domain of Preetham
RECORD	para 31	2.1 What Preetham Models
RECORD	para 33	2.2 Known Limitations and Numerical Domain
RECORD	para 34	Independent critical reviews (Zotti, Wilkie & Purgathofer 2007; later quantitative comparisons) establish the following practical bounds:
RECORD	para 35	• Turbidity range. The underlying fit used T ∈ [2, 6]. For T ■ 1.9 the model produces an unrealistically bright horizon glow and can yield negative zenith luminance. For T ■ 10 the formulae become numerically unstable and the physical premise of a “clear-sky” atmosphere ceases to hold.
DEFINE	para 36	• Solar elevation. Results near sunrise/sunset (θs ■ 85°) are problematic, especially at high turbidity; absolute luminances can become negative or diverge.
RECORD	Internal technical note Page 3	Internal technical note Page 3
DEFINE	para 39	• Horizon and colour. Even inside the nominal range the horizon is often too bright and too pink compared with CIE Standard General Sky measurements and real clear-sky observations.
RECORD	para 40	• Ground-only observer. The analytic functions and the accompanying aerial-perspective term assume a ground-level observer and a flat-Earth geometry.
DEFINE	para 41	• No clouds. The model contains no cloud optical depth, no multiple scattering inside cloud layers, and no broken-cloud geometry. It is strictly a clear-sky (or at most thin-haze) model.
ALLOW	para 42	Consequently, any production renderer that drives Preetham with a turbidity that has been artificially inflated to simulate clouds is operating outside the model’s validity domain. The visual result may be plausible for light haze, but it is not a physically justified representation of cloud scattering.
DEFINE	para 43	2.3 Clouds Are Not Aerosol — Recommended Separation
RECORD	para 44	Cloud droplets are typically 5–20 µm in radius — two orders of magnitude larger than accumulation-mode aerosol. Their scattering is essentially wavelength-independent (α ≈ 0), their single-scattering albedo is ≈1, and a cloud of only a few hundred metres optical depth already reaches τ ■ 10. Treating cloud water as an additive term inside the Preetham turbidity parameter therefore:
RECORD	para 45	• violates the spectral assumptions of the Ångström / Preetham formulation;
RECORD	para 46	• pushes T far outside the [2, 6] fit range;
RECORD	para 47	• cannot reproduce the sharp spatial and angular structure of real cloud fields;
RECORD	para 48	• cannot capture the transition from single-scattering haze to multiple-scattering white cloud.
RECORD	para 49	A cleaner architecture keeps:
RECORD	para 50	1. Clear-sky radiance — Preetham (or the improved Hosek–Wilkie / Hosek–Wilkie spectral models) driven by a true aerosol turbidity T derived from AOD or visibility;
RECORD	para 51	2. Cloud optical properties — separately computed from liquid-water content profiles (or a microphysical parameterisation) and composited via a multiple-scattering approximation, a precomputed cloud lookup, or a path-traced cloud layer;
RECORD	para 52	3. Aerial perspective / extinction — applied consistently to both the clear sky and the cloud-scattered light using the same aerosol optical depth.
REQUIRE	para 53	Practical note: if a legacy “wet turbidity gain” must be retained for artistic control, document it explicitly as a stylisation that leaves the physical clear-sky path untouched, and clamp the effective T to the interval [1.8, 8] to avoid numerical breakdown.
RECORD	para 54	3. Adiabatic Liquid-Water Content and Liquid-Water Path
RECORD	para 56	In a closed air parcel rising from cloud base without entrainment, water vapour condenses at a rate set by the difference between the dry and saturated adiabatic lapse rates. The specific liquid-water mass ql satisfies
RECORD	para 57	dql = (cp / Lv) (Γd − Γs) dz
RECORD	para 58	where Γd = g/cp is the dry adiabatic lapse rate and Γ s is the saturated moist-adiabatic lapse rate. For shallow clouds (depth ■ 500 m) the condensation rate cq is nearly constant and equal to its value at cloud base, yielding the linear profile ql(z) ≈ cq(T 0, p0) · (z − zbase)
RECORD	para 59	3.2 Liquid-Water Content (LWC)
DEFINE	para 60	Liquid-water content is the mass of liquid water per unit volume:
RECORD	LWC(z) = ρ(z) · ql(z)	LWC(z) = ρ(z) · ql(z)
DEFINE	para 62	Under the constant-rate approximation this is also linear in height above base:
RECORD	LWC(z) ≈ cLWC · (z − zbase), cLWC = ρ0 cq(T0, p0)	LWC(z) ≈ cLWC · (z − zbase), cLWC = ρ0 cq(T0, p0)
RECORD	Internal technical note Page 4	Internal technical note Page 4
RECORD	para 66	Typical adiabatic gradients at mid-latitude temperatures are of order 1–2 g m−3 km−1 (i.e. 0.001–0.002 g m−3 m−1). A 300 m deep adiabatic cloud therefore reaches peak LWC ≈ 0.3–0.6 g m−3 near cloud top.
RECORD	para 67	3.3 Liquid-Water Path (LWP)
DEFINE	para 68	The liquid-water path is the vertical integral of LWC:
RECORD	LWP = ∫z_basez_top LWC(z) dz	LWP = ∫z_basez_top LWC(z) dz
RECORD	para 70	For a linear (adiabatic) profile LWP = ½ · LWCtop · ∆z. Observationally, microwave radiometers measure LWP directly; adiabatic LWP computed from cloud-base temperature/pressure and cloud depth usually overestimates the measured value because real clouds are sub-adiabatic.
RECORD	LWC(z) = fad · cLWC · (z − zbase)	LWC(z) = fad · cLWC · (z − zbase)
RECORD	para 74	with fad prescribed from cloud depth or left as a tunable parameter. This supplies a physically sourced water-path reference that can replace a purely declared WATER_PATH_REFERENCE_M in a wet-turbidity or cloud-optical-depth ramp.
RECORD	para 75	Table 1. Illustrative adiabatic LWC gradients and LWP for selected cloud-base conditions (approximate).
RECORD	Tbase (°C) pbase (hPa) cLWC (g m	Tbase (°C) pbase (hPa) cLWC (g m −3 km−1) ∆z = 200 m LWP (g m −2) ∆z = 500 m LWP (g m−2)
RECORD	para 80	4. Twilight / Civil-Twilight Illuminance
DEFINE	para 82	Twilight is conventionally divided by solar elevation angle h■ (centre of the solar disk, refraction-corrected):
RECORD	para 83	• Civil twilight: 0° ≥ h■ ≥ −6°. Terrestrial objects remain distinguishable; outdoor activities possible without artificial light.
RECORD	para 84	• Nautical twilight: −6° ≥ h■ ≥ −12°. Horizon still visible for navigation; most stars appear.
DEFINE	para 85	• Astronomical twilight: −12° ≥ h■ ≥ −18°. Sky still measurably brighter than pure night; after −18° the contribution of sunlight is negligible.
RECORD	para 86	4.2 Illuminance versus Solar Elevation
RECORD	para 87	Clear-sky horizontal illuminance falls by roughly five orders of magnitude between sunset and the end of astronomical twilight. Representative values (open horizon, no light pollution, mid-latitude) are:
RECORD	para 88	Table 2. Approximate clear-sky horizontal illuminance (lux) versus solar elevation. Compiled from multiple observational compilations (Brown, Jones & Condit, and modern photometric fits).
RECORD	Solar elevation h	Solar elevation h ■ Illuminance (lux) Notes
RECORD	para 90	+5° ≈ 3 500 – 5 000 late afternoon / early morning
RECORD	Internal technical note Page 5	Internal technical note Page 5
RECORD	para 94	0° (sunset) ≈ 400 – 800 upper limb on horizon ≈ −0.3°
RECORD	para 100	−6° (end civil) ≈ 1 – 3.5 civil twilight ends
RECORD	para 102	−12° (end nautical) ≈ 0.01 – 0.03 nautical twilight ends
RECORD	para 104	−18° (end astron.) ≈ 0.0005 – 0.001 astronomical twilight ends
RECORD	para 105	night (no Moon) ≈ 0.0001 – 0.001 starlight + airglow
RECORD	para 106	full Moon ≈ 0.05 – 0.3 depends on elevation & phase
RECORD	para 107	4.3 Analytic Approximations for the Ambient Step
RECORD	para 108	For real-time or offline lighting the rapid change near the horizon can be captured by a smooth function of solar elevation. A convenient practical form used in many graphics and illumination models is a logistic or exponential decay fitted to the civil-twilight interval:
RECORD	E(h) = E0 · 10k·h (h in degrees, h ≤ 0)	E(h) = E0 · 10k·h (h in degrees, h ≤ 0)
WHEN	para 111	When clouds are present the twilight curve is further modified: a broken cloud field can both obscure the last direct rays and reflect residual sunlight, producing a more abrupt or a more prolonged transition depending on cloud cover and solar azimuth relative to the observer. A full treatment again requires separating the clear-sky illuminance from the cloud-scattered contribution.
RECORD	para 112	5. Summary Recommendations for Implementation
RECORD	para 113	1. Turbidity source. Derive T from measured or climatological AOD (or from visibility via Koschmieder + scale height). Keep T inside [1.8, 8] for Preetham; outside that range switch to a different sky model (Hosek–Wilkie, CIE Standard General Sky, or a path-traced atmosphere).
DEFINE	para 114	2. Do not fold cloud water into T. Cloud optical depth belongs in a separate cloud-radiance term. A wet-turbidity gain is a stylisation; if retained, document and clamp it.
RECORD	para 115	3. LWC / LWP. Prefer an adiabatic (or sub-adiabatic) profile driven by cloud-base temperature/pressure and geometric thickness. The resulting LWP can directly feed a cloud optical-depth parameterisation, replacing a hard-coded WATER_PATH_REFERENCE_M.
RECORD	para 116	4. Twilight ambient. Drive the open-horizon ambient step with a tabulated or analytic illuminance-versus-elevation curve that is continuous with the daytime Preetham zenith luminance at h ≈ 0°. Scale the curve by the same turbidity used for the daytime sky.
REQUIRE	para 117	5. Spectral consistency. Aerosol extinction carries an Ångström exponent; cloud extinction does not. Any wavelength-dependent path (spectral rendering, aerial perspective) must treat the two components separately.
RECORD	Internal technical note Page 6	Internal technical note Page 6
DEFINE	para 122	End of technical note. Equations are given in a form suitable for direct implementation; numerical coefficients should be validated against the specific atmospheric column and spectral band used by the renderer.
RECORD	Atmospheric Optical Coupling	Atmospheric Optical Coupling
