RECORD	para 56	The most useful organizing principle is to avoid forcing one scalar to stand for all atmospheric obscuration. Aerosols, molecules, clouds, and twilight all affect light, but they do so through different state variables and at different spatial scales.
RECORD	Core state separation	Core state separation Aerosol loading Column aerosol optical depth τa(λ); spectral slope α; local extinction profile σext(z, λ).
RECORD	Hygroscopic response Relative-humidity growth factor f (RH) that modifies hydrat	Hygroscopic response Relative-humidity growth factor f (RH) that modifies hydrated aerosol optical coefficients.
RECORD	Cloud water Cloud-base/top, in-cloud LWC(z), liquid water path LWP, droplet	Cloud water Cloud-base/top, in-cloud LWC(z), liquid water path LWP, droplet size and cloud optical thickness τc. Sky model Sun position plus a clear-sky parameter such as Preetham turbidity; use a separate cloud representation for clouds.
RECORD	Twilight Solar depression angle, with a dedicated transition in both intensity	Twilight Solar depression angle, with a dedicated transition in both intensity and spectrum once the Sun approaches and passes the horizon.
RECORD	A practical renderer can therefore follow the chain	A practical renderer can therefore follow the chain
RECORD	para 62	aerosol mass/size/composition → AOD(λ), α, ω0, g → σext(z, λ) → visibility and aerial perspective,
RECORD	para 63	while clouds follow
RECORD	para 64	cloud thermodynamics → LWC(z), LWP, reff → τc → cloud scattering/attenuation.
RECORD	para 65	Preetham turbidity is especially important because it is neither a modern synonym for AOD nor a cloud parameter. In the original model it is a heuristic ratio between total and molecular optical thickness, evaluated around 550 nm, and the original fitted clear-sky model is intended for a limited turbidity range.
DEFINE	The paper also explicitly notes that cloudy and partially cloudy density assumpt	The paper also explicitly notes that cloudy and partially cloudy density assumptions are outside the clear-sky formulation. [1, 2]
RECORD	para 67	2 Aerosol optical properties
RECORD	para 68	2.1 Extinction, scattering, and absorption
DEFINE	para 69	The bulk aerosol extinction coefficient is the sum of scattering and absorption:
RECORD	para 70	σext(λ) = σsca(λ) + σabs(λ).
DEFINE	para 71	It has units of inverse length. In radiative-transfer work, this is the local quantity that determines how quickly a beam loses direct intensity through the Beer-Lambert law,
RECORD	I(s) = I0 exp [	I(s) = I0 exp [ − ∫ s
RECORD	para 73	0 σext(l) dl] .
RECORD	The single-scattering albedo is	The single-scattering albedo is ω0(λ) = σsca(λ) σsca(λ) + σabs(λ) ,
RECORD	para 77	2.2 Aerosol optical depth
DEFINE	para 78	Aerosol optical depth is the vertical integral of aerosol extinction from the surface to the top of atmosphere:
RECORD	para 80	z0 σext, a(z, λ) dz.
DEFINE	para 81	AOD is dimensionless because it integrates an inverse-length coefficient over length. It is therefore a column property, while visibility is primarily a near-surface/path property.
RECORD	para 82	AOD at 550 nm is a standard reference quantity in atmospheric observation products. NASA’s aerosol documentation describes AOD as the vertically integrated aerosol extinction and uses 550 nm as a common reference wavelength. [7, 8]
REQUIRE	para 83	This distinction is fundamental for implementation: an AOD of 0.3 does not uniquely determine a ground-level extinction coefficient. One must also know, or assume, the aerosol vertical distribution.
RECORD	para 84	2.3 The Angstrom exponent
RECORD	The classical Angstrom power law is	The classical Angstrom power law is τa(λ) = τa(λ0) ( λ λ0 )−α ,
DEFINE	para 86	where α is dimensionless and describes the spectral slope of optical depth. Equivalently, for two wavelengths,
RECORD	para 87	α = − ln[τa(λ1)/τa(λ2)] ln(λ1/λ2) .
RECORD	para 88	Large positive α generally indicates stronger short-wavelength weighting associated with smaller/finer aerosol, while values near zero are associated with optically important coarse particles. NASA’s Dark Target documentation gives the same qualitative interpretation and notes that typical operational retrievals use visible or near-infrared wavelength pairs. [2, 8] 4
RECORD	para 90	Figure 1: Angstrom spectral scaling normalized to AOD at 550 nm. The slope changes the color weighting of aerosol attenuation and scattering without changing the reference AOD itself.
DEFINE	para 91	2.4 Why one AOD value is not enough for appearance
WHEN	para 92	Two atmospheres can have the same AOD550 but noticeably different sky colors if their α differs. Two atmospheres can also have similar AOD and α but different brightness patterns because their single- scattering albedo, phase function, humidity response, or vertical distribution differs. A renderer that only stores one scalar “haze amount” is therefore collapsing several physical degrees of freedom.
RECORD	For a compact real-time representation, a particularly useful minimum is	For a compact real-time representation, a particularly useful minimum is
RECORD	para 94	{AOD550, α, ω0, g, Ha, RH},
DEFINE	para 95	where Ha is an aerosol scale-height parameter used to distribute column optical depth vertically.
RECORD	para 96	3 Visibility and its relationship to extinction
DEFINE	For a dark object viewed against a uniform horizon background under the assumpti	For a dark object viewed against a uniform horizon background under the assumptions of classical meteorological visibility theory, the visual range is approximately
WHEN	para 100	when σext is expressed in inverse kilometers and V in kilometers. The constant is associated with a 2 percent contrast threshold, since − ln(0.02) ≈ 3.912. The simple relationship is only valid under restricted conditions; Horvath showed how non-uniform illumination, spatially varying extinction, object reflectance, and observer threshold alter the result. [10, 11] 5
RECORD	para 102	3.2 AOD-to-visibility requires a vertical profile
DEFINE	para 103	Suppose the aerosol extinction profile is approximated as exponential:
RECORD	para 104	σext, a(z, 550) = σext, a, 0(550) exp(−z/Ha).
RECORD	para 106	0 σext, a(z, 550) dz = σext, a, 0(550)Ha,
RECORD	para 107	so σext, a, 0(550) ≈ AOD550 Ha .
RECORD	para 108	Ignoring molecular and gaseous extinction for the moment,
RECORD	para 109	V, a ≈ 3.912 Ha AOD550 .
WHEN	para 110	This is not a universal law; it is a transparent illustration of why a column measurement needs a vertical scale to become a surface visibility estimate. Satellite AOD products and visibility retrievals make the same point: aerosol vertical distribution is a major source of ambiguity when converting column optical depth into surface extinction. [12]
DEFINE	para 111	Figure 2: First-order 550 nm turbidity/AOD crosswalk with a separate visibility example using a 1.5 km aerosol scale height. The right axis is illustrative rather than a universal mapping.
FORBID	para 112	3.3 Molecular extinction must not be silently folded into aerosol AOD
DEFINE	para 116	Thus a useful decomposition is τtotal(λ) = τR(λ) + τa(λ) + τg(λ),
WHEN	para 117	where τg covers absorbers such as ozone and other gases when needed.
RECORD	para 118	4 From AOD and Angstrom exponent to Preetham turbidity
RECORD	para 119	4.1 What Preetham turbidity means
RECORD	para 120	Preetham et al. define turbidity as the ratio of the optical thickness of the total haze-plus-molecular atmosphere to the optical thickness of the molecular atmosphere. In compact notation,
RECORD	T = τR + τa	T = τR + τa τR = 1 + τa τR ,
RECORD	para 122	with the original optical application evaluating turbidity at approximately 550 nm. The paper uses turbidity as a practical heuristic tied to atmospheric haze and visibility rather than as a complete aerosol retrieval. [1]
RECORD	para 123	The original daylight model uses Angstrom’s aerosol transmittance law in its direct-light attenuation component and adopts a fixed wavelength exponent α = 1.3 for that part of the model. This is important: the original Preetham implementation does not expose modern measurement-grade α as an independent control. [1]
RECORD	para 124	4.2 A first-order crosswalk
WHEN	If a renderer has measured or modeled AOD550, a physically intelligible first-or	If a renderer has measured or modeled AOD550, a physically intelligible first-order mapping is
RECORD	T ≈ 1 + AOD550	T ≈ 1 + AOD550 τR,550 .
RECORD	para 127	Using a representative τR,550 = 0.097, this gives:
RECORD	para 128	Table 1: Approximate Preetham turbidity to AOD crosswalk at 550 nm.
RECORD	para 129	T Approx. AOD550 Interpretation
RECORD	para 130	2 0.097 Very clear / low aerosol optical load 3 0.194 Clear temperate-type loading 4 0.291 Noticeably hazy but still daylight-model regime 5 0.388 Strong haze / warm-moist appearance 6 0.485 Upper end of original fitted range
RECORD	para 131	The values should be read as a crosswalk, not as a claim that all real atmospheres at AOD 0.29 look exactly like Preetham T = 4. The mapping inherits the molecular optical-depth reference, the assumed aerosol vertical profile, wavelength convention, and Preetham’s simplified aerosol spectral law.
RECORD	para 132	4.3 Preetham’s original validity domain
DEFINE	para 133	The original fitted Preetham sky model is commonly used over a turbidity range of about T = 2 to 6. Later sky-model literature identifies the Preetham model’s weak points as high-turbidity configurations
RECORD	para 136	and sunsets. Hosek and Wilkie developed a successor model specifically to improve those regimes while retaining a similar analytic structure and to add ground albedo and independent spectral treatment. [1, 23]
RECORD	para 137	Implementation rule: use Preetham turbidity as the clear-sky aerosol/haze control. Do not increase turbidity until a cloud becomes visually thick. A cloud is a separate volumetric water/ice scattering medium. The same physical scene can have low aerosol turbidity and optically thick cloud.
RECORD	para 138	5 Humidity and wet aerosol optics
RECORD	para 139	5.1 Why water changes aerosol extinction
RECORD	para 140	Hygroscopic aerosol takes up water as relative humidity rises. Particle size and refractive index change, which can substantially increase scattering and therefore extinction. A common measurement-side quantity is the scattering enhancement factor fsca(RH) = σsca(RH) σsca(RHref ) .
RECORD	para 141	The literature shows that f (RH) is strongly dependent on aerosol chemistry, size distribution, and the reference humidity. In measurements and models, it is often represented by an empirical power law or by more physically based hygroscopic-growth formulations. [14, 15, 16, 17]
RECORD	A simple engineering form is	A simple engineering form is fsca(RH) = ( 1 − RHref 1 − RH )γ ,
RECORD	para 143	with RH expressed as a fraction and γ fitted to a specified aerosol type. This should be treated as a parameterization, not a universal atmospheric constant. [14]
RECORD	para 144	5.2 The renderer-friendly separation
DEFINE	For a compact model, it is better to write	For a compact model, it is better to write
RECORD	para 146	σext, wet ≈ σabs, dry + fsca(RH) σsca, dry,
RECORD	para 147	than to multiply all cloud or atmospheric optical density by an arbitrary “wetness” factor. This preserves the idea that humidity changes aerosol optical behavior, while a cloud water path is a different state variable.
RECORD	para 148	This is the physical interpretation most directly relevant to a parameter such as WET TURBIDITY GAIN: that parameter can be understood as an intentionally simplified proxy for f (RH), provided its scope is explicitly restricted to aerosol haze rather than cloud water.
RECORD	para 149	6 Cloud liquid water content and liquid water path
RECORD	para 150	6.1 Adiabatic in-cloud liquid water
DEFINE	For an idealized warm liquid cloud, rising air cools, saturation is maintained,	For an idealized warm liquid cloud, rising air cools, saturation is maintained, and condensed liquid accumulates above cloud base. In this document, LWC denotes liquid-water mass concentration in kg
DEFINE	para 154	m−3; meteorological literature also uses qL for a mixing ratio, so the units should be kept explicit. In the adiabatic approximation, in-cloud liquid water content is approximately linear with height:
RECORD	para 155	LWC(z) ≈ fad Γad(z − zb), zb ≤ z ≤ zt,
RECORD	para 156	where Γad is the adiabatic condensation gradient and fad is an optional sub-adiabatic factor. Ideal adiabatic behavior corresponds to fad = 1; real clouds are often sub-adiabatic because of entrainment, mixing, precipitation, and other processes. Observational and model studies routinely use the near-linear vertical LWC behavior as an idealized cloud model. [20, 18, 19, 21]
DEFINE	para 157	Figure 3: Idealized linear adiabatic LWC profile. The area under the profile is the liquid water path.
RECORD	para 158	6.2 Liquid water path
DEFINE	Liquid water path is the vertical integral	Liquid water path is the vertical integral
RECORD	para 161	zb LWC(z) dz.
RECORD	para 162	With the linear idealization above, and cloud depth H = zt − zb,
RECORD	LWP = 1	LWP = 1 2 fad ΓadH 2 .
DEFINE	para 164	The result is especially useful in a renderer because it creates a direct path from cloud thermodynamics and geometry to an integrated optical mass without inventing an arbitrary ramp.
RECORD	para 165	Modern cloud-profile datasets can calculate adiabatic LWC from cloud-base temperature and pressure, then scale the profile to observed LWP. This is exactly the kind of structure that can be used to replace a declared ramp with a sourced thermodynamic relationship. [22, 21]
RECORD	para 168	6.3 From LWP to cloud optical thickness
RECORD	para 169	For droplets with effective radius reff , a general geometric-optics expression with extinction efficiency near 2 is τc = ∫ zt
RECORD	LWC(z)	LWC(z) reff (z) dz.
RECORD	For a vertically constant LWC and reff , this reduces to	For a vertically constant LWC and reff , this reduces to
RECORD	para 173	τc ≈ 3LWP 2ρwreff .
RECORD	para 174	The sub-adiabatic cloud treatment of Barlakas et al. also gives τc = 9
WHEN	5 LWP	5 LWP ρwreff when its corresponding vertically integrated effective-radius construction is used. The coefficient is therefore model-dependent rather than universal. Cloud opacity depends jointly on water mass and droplet size: aerosol can affect droplet number and effective radius through cloud microphysics, while total cloud water mass is represented by LWP. [21]
RECORD	para 176	6.4 Why LWP should not be substituted for aerosol turbidity
RECORD	A good implementation keeps	A good implementation keeps
RECORD	para 179	{AOD, α, f (RH)} and {LWC, LWP, reff , τc}
RECORD	para 180	separate, while allowing both to contribute to the final radiative transfer.
RECORD	para 181	7 Sky radiance models and their validity domain
DEFINE	para 182	7.1 What Preetham is good at
RECORD	Preetham, Shirley and Smits provide an inexpensive analytic approximation to day	Preetham, Shirley and Smits provide an inexpensive analytic approximation to daylight sky spectral radiance and aerial perspective, parameterized by sun position and atmospheric conditions. Their model is a fitted representation of atmospheric-scattering calculations rather than a full on-line radiative-transfer solver. [1]
WHEN	para 184	The model is therefore excellent as a real-time clear-sky baseline, particularly when the renderer needs a single fast analytic evaluation instead of a multi-layer radiative-transfer calculation. The same paper also discusses the established overcast-sky luminance formulation; that is a distinct sky-state model rather than a volumetric cloud solution.
RECORD	para 185	7.2 What it does not solve
RECORD	para 186	The original paper states that its analytic formulas cover clear and overcast skies, while partially cloudy skies were not presented as a solved case. The simulated atmosphere uses uniform or exponential-like particle-density assumptions, and the authors explicitly note that those assumptions do not hold for
RECORD	para 189	cloudy or partly cloudy skies. A renderer therefore still needs a separate cloud field and cloud optical model for partially cloudy scenes. [1]
RECORD	para 190	This gives a clean architectural boundary:
RECORD	para 191	clear sky model + explicit cloud field + aerosol extinction/aerial perspective.
RECORD	para 192	Do not treat a cloud as merely a very large Preetham turbidity value.
RECORD	para 193	7.3 Hosek-Wilkie as a useful upgrade path
RECORD	para 194	Hosek and Wilkie’s 2012 model preserves the analytic character of Preetham while improving high-turbidity and sunset behavior, accounting for ground albedo, and treating spectral components independently. The authors describe it as a drop-in replacement in many mathematical contexts. [23]
RECORD	para 195	This suggests a pragmatic path: keep the physical state model independent of the sky evaluator. The renderer can use Preetham now, while later substituting Hosek-Wilkie or a more modern sky-dome model without changing how aerosol AOD, humidity, cloud LWP, and twilight are represented.
RECORD	para 196	8 Twilight and the open-horizon ambient step
RECORD	para 198	Civil twilight is conventionally the interval during which the geometric solar center moves from the horizon to 6 degrees below it. Nautical twilight continues to 12 degrees, and astronomical twilight to 18 degrees. The American Meteorological Society glossary also reports clear-evening horizontal illuminance decreasing from roughly 585–410 lux at the beginning of civil twilight to about 3.5–2 lux at its end. [26]
DEFINE	para 199	8.2 Twilight is not just a dimmed daytime shader
RECORD	para 200	Measurements show that twilight changes both intensity and spectrum with solar elevation. Spitschan et al. measured visible irradiance through the civil, nautical, and astronomical regimes and found systematic spectral changes, not merely a scalar decrease in brightness. More detailed radiative-transfer studies model twilight as solar illumination scattered from a spherical atmosphere with altitude-dependent molecular and aerosol structure. [25, 24] 11
RECORD	para 202	Figure 4: An engineering civil-twilight curve. The endpoints are the midpoints of the AMS clear-sky illuminance ranges; the interior is a log-linear interpolation chosen for renderer stability, not claimed as a universal empirical law.
RECORD	para 203	8.3 A simple implementable curve
RECORD	E(d) = E0	E(d) = E0 ( E6 E0 )d/6 , 0 ≤ d ≤ 6.
RECORD	para 206	This is intentionally labeled an engineering curve. Actual twilight illuminance depends on aerosol loading, altitude, latitude, season, solar azimuth, cloud cover, ground reflectance, and light pollution. The measurement literature supports using solar elevation as the principal coordinate but also demonstrates substantial environmental variation. [25]
RECORD	para 207	8.4 Practical renderer behavior
RECORD	para 208	A robust open-horizon transition can use:
RECORD	para 211	Eday(h, sky model) h ≥ 0, E(−h, twilight state) −6◦ < h < 0, Enight(moon, stars, airglow) h ≤ −6◦,
RECORD	para 212	where h is solar elevation. For a visually convincing result, intensity and sky chromaticity should transition together. A clear-sky Preetham evaluator can provide the daylight side down to its practical limit, while a separate twilight layer takes over as the Sun leaves the direct daylight geometry.
