RECORD	Open standard does not imply identical renderer implementation 2	Open standard does not imply identical renderer implementation 2
RECORD	How to use this document	How to use this document
REQUIRE	para 17	Energy conservation is about the whole light budget, not a single highlight. For each amount of incident light, the model must account for what is reflected, transmitted or scattered onward, and absorbed. The same photon cannot independently fund a full-strength diffuse lobe, a full-strength specular lobe, and a full-strength clear coat.
RECORD	The three checks that matter	The three checks that matter
RECORD	Question Meaning Typical diagnostic	Question Meaning Typical diagnostic
FORBID	Can the material create	Can the material create light? For non-emissive response, integrated reflected plus transmitted energy must not exceed incident energy. BRDF / BSDF energy integral.
RECORD	Does a lossless white	Does a lossless white material lose light in the shader?
RECORD	In ideal lossless test configurations, reflected/transmitted energy	In ideal lossless test configurations, reflected/transmitted energy should be preserved rather than disappearing because of a lobe approximation.
RECORD	White-furnace test with enough	White-furnace test with enough bounces.
RECORD	Does the artist-facing	Does the artist-facing material behave consistently? Parameters should have stable meaning across lights, environments, scales and supported renderers. Versioned parameter tests and reference renders.
RECORD	para 26	Physics first: Sections 3-7 introduce radiometry, BRDFs, energy integrals and Fresnel. Model mechanics: Sections 8-12 cover diffuse/specular sharing, roughness, multiple scattering, layers and transmission. Engineering: Sections 13-17 provide white-furnace testing, debugging, implementation guidance and a compact checklist. The final pages contain a glossary and source bibliography.
RECORD	para 27	Source status: technical claims below are attributed to the OpenPBR specification, MaterialX physically based shading specification, or foundational rendering literature. Illustrative diagrams are explanatory, not measured material data.
RECORD	Open standard does not imply identical renderer implementation 3	Open standard does not imply identical renderer implementation 3
RECORD	para 30	1. What OpenPBR describes
DEFINE	para 33	Figure 1. Conceptual OpenPBR material stack. Each upper interface changes the fraction of light available to the substrate; a coat is not simply another highlight added at full strength. [1]
RECORD	Major physical components	Major physical components
