RECORD	para 59	3. The rendering equation and the scattering function
RECORD	para 60	At a surface point x, the outgoing radiance in direction ωₒ consists of emitted radiance plus incident radiance reflected into that direction from all incoming directions. This is the foundational rendering equation introduced to computer graphics in its modern form by Kajiya and developed in the broader rendering literature. [3]
RECORD	Lₒ(x, ωₒ) = Lₑ(x, ωₒ) + ∫H fᵣ(x, ωᵢ, ωₒ) Lᵢ(x, ωᵢ) cos θᵢ dωᵢ	Lₒ(x, ωₒ) = Lₑ(x, ωₒ) + ∫H fᵣ(x, ωᵢ, ωₒ) Lᵢ(x, ωᵢ) cos θᵢ dωᵢ
RECORD	para 62	Here H is the incident hemisphere, fᵣ is the BRDF, and cos θᵢ accounts for projected area. The equation is recursive: Lᵢ may itself come from other surfaces that reflected or emitted light. Path tracing estimates this integral by sampling paths; rasterization and real-time pipelines approximate it with direct-light evaluation, image-based lighting, prefiltered environments and other methods.
RECORD	BRDF, BTDF, BSDF and BSSRDF	BRDF, BTDF, BSDF and BSSRDF
RECORD	Term Role Direction relation	Term Role Direction relation
RECORD	para 65	BRDF Bidirectional reflectance distribution function: surface reflection. Incoming and outgoing directions on the same side.
RECORD	para 66	BTDF Bidirectional transmittance distribution function: surface transmission/refraction. Incoming and outgoing directions on opposite sides.
ALLOW	para 67	BSDF Umbrella term for reflection and transmission distributions. May include reflective and transmissive lobes.
RECORD	para 68	BSSRDF Subsurface transport between different points on a material. Light enters at one location and exits at another.
RECORD	para 69	EDF / emission Describes light emitted by a surface. A source term, not a reflection lobe.
DEFINE	Diffuse, glossy and specular are about directionality	Diffuse, glossy and specular are about directionality
RECORD	para 71	“Diffuse” means energy is distributed broadly; “specular” is concentrated around ideal reflection or refraction; “glossy” lies between. These categories describe angular shape rather than energy amount. A rough mirror and a smooth mirror may reflect similar total energy while producing radically different-looking highlights. [2, 4]
REQUIRE	Implementation consequence	Implementation consequence A shader should not calculate arbitrary “brightness” for each lobe independently and add the values without a budget. Lobe evaluation, mixture weights, sampling PDFs and any attenuation between layers must agree with the material model.
RECORD	Open standard does not imply identical renderer implementation 6	Open standard does not imply identical renderer implementation 6
RECORD	para 75	4. Energy conservation: the integral condition
FORBID	para 76	A common surface-level definition uses the directional albedo (also called directional reflectance): integrate the BRDF over all incident directions, weighted by the projected solid angle. For a passive reflective lobe, this result must not exceed one for any fixed outgoing direction. MaterialX makes this condition explicit for physically plausible BSDFs. [1, 2]
RECORD	Eₒ(ωₒ) = ∫H fᵣ(ωᵢ, ωₒ) cos θᵢ dωᵢ ≤ 1	Eₒ(ωₒ) = ∫H fᵣ(ωᵢ, ωₒ) cos θᵢ dωᵢ ≤ 1
RECORD	Conservation versus preservation	Conservation versus preservation
RECORD	Property Mathematical idea What failure looks like	Property Mathematical idea What failure looks like
DEFINE	para 81	Energy conserving Returned energy is no greater than energy arriving. Under a white environment, material adds excess brightness or diverges as layers are added.
DEFINE	Energy preserving In an ideal lossless configuration, energy that should	Energy preserving In an ideal lossless configuration, energy that should return is not lost by an approximation. A white, lossless rough material appears too dark or has roughness-dependent brightness loss.
RECORD	para 83	Physically plausible Conservation plus appropriate scattering behavior; reciprocity and plausible Fresnel/volume behavior are also expected.
ALLOW	A model may conserve energy yet have implausible	A model may conserve energy yet have implausible color, directionality or refraction.
DEFINE	Why conservation alone is not enough	Why conservation alone is not enough
REQUIRE	para 86	A shader that returns zero radiance can be “energy conserving” in the trivial inequality sense, but it is not a useful model of a white lossless material. Conversely, a material with real absorption should not be brightened artificially to pass a white-furnace test. The test configuration must describe an idealized lossless material before energy preservation is expected. [1]
RECORD	para 87	Do not compare only the highlight pixel. Evaluate the integrated outgoing distribution or run a white-furnace test. A narrow lobe can be very bright at its peak and still conserve energy; a broad lobe can have a modest peak and carry substantial integrated energy.
RECORD	Open standard does not imply identical renderer implementation 7	Open standard does not imply identical renderer implementation 7
RECORD	para 90	5. Fresnel reflection, dielectrics and metals
RECORD	para 91	Fresnel equations describe the angle-dependent division between reflection and transmission at an interface. At normal incidence, a non-absorbing dielectric interface has reflectance determined by the refractive indices. For air-to-glass with IOR around 1.5, this is roughly 4%. As the angle approaches grazing, reflectance approaches one. [1, 4, 5]
RECORD	para 92	F₀ = ((n₁ − n₂) / (n₁ + n₂))² ; IOR 1.5 gives F₀ ≈ 0.04
RECORD	para 93	Figure 3. Exact unpolarized dielectric Fresnel curve for an air-to-IOR-1.5 interface. A value near four percent at normal incidence does not mean that only four percent of light can ever be reflected at all angles.
RECORD	OpenPBR controls to know	OpenPBR controls to know specular_ior sets the dielectric IOR; specular_roughness controls the interface microfacet width; base_metalness interpolates between dielectric and metal base models; base_color participates differently in dielectric base color and conductor Fresnel parametrization. Consult the exact model version before transferring settings between tools.
RECORD	Open standard does not imply identical renderer implementation 8	Open standard does not imply identical renderer implementation 8
REQUIRE	para 101	6. Why diffuse and specular must share a budget
RECORD	para 103	f_dielectric ≈ f_specular + (1 − E[f_specular]) · f_substrate
ALLOW	para 104	This is a conceptual form: E[f_specular] is an integrated directional reflectance, not simply the Fresnel value at the view angle nor a scalar copied from a specular_weight knob. A production implementation may require angular integration, compensation, and layer-specific details.
RECORD	The visible result	The visible result
RECORD	What metallic does to the balance	What metallic does to the balance
RECORD	para 108	As the OpenPBR base moves toward metalness, the model transitions toward conductor reflection. The familiar diffuse-like base contribution is reduced or removed because light is not treated as entering an opaque dielectric body and diffusely returning. A half-metal setting is artistically useful, but it represents a mixing model—not a universal physical substance.
RECORD	Incorrect construction Why it fails Better construction	Incorrect construction Why it fails Better construction
ALLOW	Diffuse = albedo / π plus	Diffuse = albedo / π plus independently tuned specular. May double-count energy, especially at grazing angles. Fresnel-aware balancing; integrated-lobe checks.
RECORD	Use a colored diffuse term for	Use a colored diffuse term for metal and also colored metal Fresnel.
RECORD	Duplicates color in two separate mechanisms	Duplicates color in two separate mechanisms and can make metals too bright or too saturated.
RECORD	Use the metallic response model and calibrate it	Use the metallic response model and calibrate it against a known material / reference.
RECORD	Reduce diffuse texture	Reduce diffuse texture brightness until one render looks right.
RECORD	Bakes light/environment assumptions into	Bakes light/environment assumptions into material data. Fix the shader’s energy balance, then retune the physical base color.
WHEN	Practical diagnostic	Practical diagnostic Rotate a non-metal sphere under a stable environment. The material should not suddenly gain a second independent energy source when a bright environment edge enters the specular lobe.
RECORD	Open standard does not imply identical renderer implementation 9	Open standard does not imply identical renderer implementation 9
RECORD	para 119	7. Microfacets and the role of roughness
RECORD	para 120	Microfacet models treat a rough surface as a distribution of tiny, locally specular facets. A common microfacet BRDF combines a normal-distribution function D, a Fresnel term F, and masking-shadowing factor G. One standard reflection form is shown below. [2, 4, 5, 7]
RECORD	para 121	fᵣ(ωᵢ, ωₒ) = D(ωₕ) F(ωᵢ, ωₕ) G₂(ωᵢ, ωₒ) / (4 cos θᵢ cos θₒ)
RECORD	para 122	The half-vector ωₕ is derived from the incident and outgoing directions. D describes how likely microfacet normals are oriented; F determines the Fresnel response at each microfacet; G accounts for microfacets hidden from the incident or outgoing direction. OpenPBR / MaterialX uses a GGX-family microfacet description for its common dielectric and conductor specular interfaces. [1, 2]
RECORD	para 123	Figure 4. Illustration only: the same normalized energy distribution can be narrow or broad. A shader whose roughness changes the lobe width should avoid inadvertently changing total reflectance, except where the model’s intended physical behavior or approximations specify otherwise.
DEFINE	Roughness is not a brightness slider	Roughness is not a brightness slider
RECORD	para 125	For an ideal energy-normalized model, increasing roughness broadens the reflection while broadly preserving its total integrated energy. The peak drops because that energy is spread across more directions. In a finite renderer, measured brightness can still change due to sampling variance, environment-map filtering, limited path depth, denoising, clamping or missing multiple-scattering energy.
RECORD	Geometry and normal maps	Geometry and normal maps
RECORD	para 127	The masking-shadowing term matters: facets can hide one another. Incorrect geometric attenuation can produce bad grazing highlights and incorrect normalization. Heitz’s analysis derives constraints on masking functions from visible microsurface area and explains why choosing G is tied to a microsurface assumption. Normal maps are not automatically a license to ignore geometric occlusion or change material energy accounting. [7]
RECORD	para 128	Test across roughness, not at just one value. Sweep specular_roughness from near zero to very rough while holding the environment and all other parameters fixed. Look for changes in integrated output, not merely the highlight shape.
RECORD	Open standard does not imply identical renderer implementation 10	Open standard does not imply identical renderer implementation 10
RECORD	para 131	8. Missing energy in single-scattering microfacet models
DEFINE	What energy compensation is trying to fix	What energy compensation is trying to fix
RECORD	para 135	In this schematic form, Eₛₛ denotes a directional albedo for a unit-Fresnel single-scattering microfacet model and F̄ represents a suitable averaged Fresnel factor. The exact normalization, edge cases, sampling, and implementation details matter; do not paste this shorthand into a shader without following the source specification.
RECORD	Symptoms of a missing or broken correction	Symptoms of a missing or broken correction
RECORD	Observation Likely cause to investigate	Observation Likely cause to investigate
RECORD	White rough metal or dielectric gets darker as	White rough metal or dielectric gets darker as roughness increases. Single-scatter energy loss; compensation disabled or misapplied.
RECORD	para 139	The correction creates bright overshoot. Albedo approximation wrong, roughness remapping mismatch, double application of correction, or unstable denominator.
ALLOW	Direct lighting seems correct but image-based	Direct lighting seems correct but image-based lighting is dark. Prefiltered environment, BRDF integration LUT, and runtime BRDF may not use compatible roughness/normalization.
RECORD	para 141	One renderer passes and another fails. Different BSDF variants, versions, lobe weights, bounce depth, clamps or color management.
RECORD	para 142	Separate physical attenuation from approximation loss. A rough clear coat can reflect into more directions and can legitimately attenuate the base. That is not the same as a single-scattering approximation losing energy inside one rough interface.
RECORD	Open standard does not imply identical renderer implementation 11	Open standard does not imply identical renderer implementation 11
RECORD	para 145	9. Layering: coat, fuzz and substrate
WHEN	para 146	OpenPBR models materials as a stack of interacting structures. If an upper layer reflects some light, the underlying layer must receive less. If the upper layer absorbs light, that absorbed portion does not reach the base. If it transmits light, the transmitted part is attenuated on its way through. A naive sum of independent lobes ignores this path budget. [1, 2]
RECORD	para 147	Figure 5. Conceptual layer stack. Real implementations need to account for direction, Fresnel reflectance, absorption and the approximations used to represent multiple passes.
RECORD	A useful layering abstraction	A useful layering abstraction
RECORD	para 149	f_layered ≈ f_top + T_top · f_base
WHEN	para 154	Fuzz represents a fiber-like scattering layer. It also changes the light available to the structure beneath it. Treat fuzz as a model component with its own directional response and attenuation, not merely an emissive rim term. If a custom fur/fuzz effect intentionally violates the model, document that departure.
ALLOW	para 155	Watch for double attenuation as well as missing attenuation. If the top lobe is attenuated once inside the OpenPBR BSDF and again by a renderer-specific layer node, the base may become too dark. One layer stack should have one well-defined energy bookkeeping convention.
RECORD	Open standard does not imply identical renderer implementation 12	Open standard does not imply identical renderer implementation 12
RECORD	para 158	10. Transmission, absorption and subsurface transport
RECORD	Beer-Lambert attenuation	Beer-Lambert attenuation
WHEN	para 161	A homogeneous absorbing medium attenuates light exponentially with path length. OpenPBR’s artist-facing transmission color/depth pair is one way to derive an extinction coefficient per color channel when transmission depth is positive. The color indicates the surviving fraction at the specified distance, not a free bonus tint independent of physical path length. [1]
RECORD	T(d) = exp(−σₜ d) and σₜ = −ln(T_ref) / d_ref	T(d) = exp(−σₜ d) and σₜ = −ln(T_ref) / d_ref
RECORD	para 163	T(d) is transmittance over distance d; σₜ is extinction (per-channel in an RGB approximation); T_ref is the transmission color at reference distance d_ref. Scattering can redirect the surviving energy; absorption removes energy from the light field. Extinction combines both effects along the original ray.
DEFINE	Subsurface is not the same as surface diffuse	Subsurface is not the same as surface diffuse
RECORD	para 165	Diffuse reflection is an efficient approximation for light that enters and exits near a surface point after many unresolved scattering events. Subsurface scattering carries spatial displacement and material-scale dependence. The color and mean-free-path controls affect appearance, but conservation still requires the amount of energy that emerges to be compatible with energy entering and leaving the object.
RECORD	Thin-walled materials	Thin-walled materials
ALLOW	para 167	Leaves, paper and thin films may be better represented with a thin-walled model than a thick closed volume. It changes how transmission and internal travel are approximated. Do not use the thin-walled option just to remove inconvenient refraction artifacts on a physically thick object unless the visual compromise is intended.
WHEN	para 168	Keep units and scale coherent. A transmission_depth of 1 means a distance in the scene’s declared length units. If one asset uses centimeters and another interprets the same numeric depth as meters, the color attenuation will be wildly different. Document units and conversions in the pipeline.
RECORD	Open standard does not imply identical renderer implementation 13	Open standard does not imply identical renderer implementation 13
RECORD	para 171	11. The white-furnace test
DEFINE	para 173	Figure 6. Conceptual white-furnace arrangement. A visually plausible single frame is not enough; the test should use a stable, uniform environment and enough bounces for the target model.
RECORD	A reproducible test protocol	A reproducible test protocol
RECORD	para 176	1. Environment Use a uniform white environment with a known linear radiance or unit intensity. Disable contrasty HDRI maps.
RECORD	para 177	2. Geometry Use a closed sphere or another well-behaved primitive. Keep normals and geometry fixed during tests.
RECORD	para 178	3. Ideal material Start with white, lossless values and turn off emission. Use an OpenPBR configuration named in the current white-furnace section.
RECORD	para 179	4. Transport Use enough bounces / converged samples. Fix exposure, view transform, denoiser and clamping.
RECORD	para 180	5. Sweep Test low-to-high specular and coat roughness; then add coat, fuzz, thin film, anisotropy and supported normal maps.
RECORD	para 181	6. Measure Compare linear render values or integrated energy against the environment, not only tone-mapped screenshots.
RECORD	para 182	7. Repeat Run multiple seeds and renderer backends; record software version and material parameters.
RECORD	Open standard does not imply identical renderer implementation 14	Open standard does not imply identical renderer implementation 14
RECORD	para 187	12. Implementation pitfalls and cross-renderer differences
ALLOW	para 188	OpenPBR gives tools a common model, not a guarantee of bit-identical renders. Applications may differ in specification version, parameter remapping, sampled versus analytic BRDF integration, multiple-scattering treatment, volume implementation, sampling, denoising, tone mapping and unsupported features. The model definition and the renderer’s implementation both need to be recorded. [1, 2, 10, 11]
RECORD	Pitfall Why it causes problems Mitigation	Pitfall Why it causes problems Mitigation
RECORD	Adding all lobes	Adding all lobes independently Diffuse, specular, coat and fuzz can each consume the same incident energy. Use the model’s mixture/layer equations and white-furnace tests.
RECORD	Mixing sRGB color values	Mixing sRGB color values into linear math The energy calculations see distorted values. Decode texture/color inputs into the intended linear working space before BSDF evaluation.
RECORD	Using roughness in a BRDF	Using roughness in a BRDF and LUT differently Environment prefilter and BRDF response do not match. Use consistent roughness remapping, NDF, Fresnel and compensation in precompute and runtime.
RECORD	Tuning materials with tone	Tuning materials with tone mapping on A display transform can hide over/under-energy or create highlight clipping. Inspect linear output and run unit tests in a controlled scene.
RECORD	Treating AO as energy	Treating AO as energy attenuation everywhere AO can darken areas without corresponding transport and is often multiplied into direct light incorrectly. Reserve AO for its intended approximation; do not substitute it for BRDF/layer conservation.
WHEN	Using normals as if they	Using normals as if they changed the true energy budget
RECORD	Shading normals change the frame and can create	Shading normals change the frame and can create reciprocity/normalization artifacts. Validate geometric/shading-normal handling and test normal-mapped white materials.
RECORD	Assuming every OpenPBR	Assuming every OpenPBR input is physically measured Some controls are art-directable, e.g. reflectivity weights or color tints. Distinguish physical quantities (IOR, depth) from artistic controls in tools and documentation.
ALLOW	Assuming transmission color	Assuming transmission color is a surface albedo Color may be path-length-based volume attenuation or a specific interface tint in a defined edge case. Follow the specification’s depth=0 and depth>0 semantics.
WHEN	para 199	Avoid hidden clamping as a substitute for a correct model. Clamps can stabilize a production render, but if they activate for valid material inputs they can conceal a non-conserving BSDF or a broken importance-sampling path. Log the clamp rate and test normalized values before relying on it.
RECORD	Open standard does not imply identical renderer implementation 15	Open standard does not imply identical renderer implementation 15
RECORD	para 202	13. Practical renderer integration checklist
RECORD	Material-evaluation layer	Material-evaluation layer
RECORD	para 204	□ Choose and pin an OpenPBR specification version. □ Keep shader parameter names and units consistent with the specification. □ Implement color management at the input/output boundaries, not as hidden BRDF math. □ Separate surface reflection/transmission (BSDF), subsurface/volume transport (BSSRDF/VDF) and emission (EDF). □ Keep direct lighting, environment lighting and path tracing on compatible scattering definitions.
RECORD	Transport and sampling	Transport and sampling
REQUIRE	para 206	□ A sample’s throughput weight must match the BSDF value, cosine / projection measure and PDF used to generate the sample. □ Include transmission when evaluating the whole energy budget. □ Use a layer transmission convention shared by each layer node. □ If a multiple-scattering correction exists, align evaluation, sampling and PDF accounting. □ Record path depth and termination thresholds for tests.
RECORD	Texture and coordinate conventions	Texture and coordinate conventions
RECORD	para 208	□ Define linear working color space and channel interpretation. □ Confirm roughness remapping end-to-end. □ State whether texture values are perceptual roughness or microfacet alpha. □ Validate tangent frames / anisotropy axes. □ Test geometric normals and shading normals. □ Specify scene units for subsurface radius and transmission depth.
RECORD	Test Purpose Hold constant	Test Purpose Hold constant
RECORD	para 211	White furnace Check energy preservation of ideal lossless configs. Environment radiance, exposure, bounces, color transform.
RECORD	para 212	Roughness sweep Catch roughness-correlated energy loss or overshoot. Material weights, normal, view, environment.
RECORD	para 213	Grazing-angle sweep Check Fresnel and diffuse/base attenuation. IOR, roughness, albedo, lighting.
RECORD	para 214	Layer addition Verify coat/fuzz reduces base response consistently. Base material, scene, top-layer coverage.
RECORD	Transmission depth	Transmission depth sweep Check Beer-Lambert distance response. Scene scale, reference depth, IOR, light.
RECORD	Metal/dielectric	Metal/dielectric endpoints Check that base modes have distinct expected behavior. Environment, exposure, base color.
RECORD	Cross-renderer	Cross-renderer reference Find unsupported features and parameter mapping drift. Asset version, units, texture decode, view transform.
RECORD	para 218	Make the test machine-readable. For each render, save the shader parameter block, renderer version, model version, scene units, environment identifier, sample / bounce settings, linear image, and a numeric summary of output radiance. A screenshot alone is not sufficient for regression testing.
RECORD	Open standard does not imply identical renderer implementation 16	Open standard does not imply identical renderer implementation 16
RECORD	para 221	14. Material recipes as energy hypotheses
RECORD	para 222	The recipes below are starting hypotheses for building a test scene, not universal presets. The point is to isolate the underlying energy path before artistic tuning. Use current OpenPBR defaults and its parameter reference as the schema of record. [1, 10, 11]
RECORD	Target Start with Check before artistic tuning	Target Start with Check before artistic tuning
RECORD	para 224	Painted concrete / wall base_metalness = 0; nonzero base_weight; neutral dielectric specular; moderate base_color; low-to-moderate specular roughness.
DEFINE	Diffuse/base response is attenuated by	Diffuse/base response is attenuated by dielectric specular; energy behaves at grazing angles.
RECORD	para 226	Clear-coated paint Dielectric or metallic base as appropriate; add coat_weight; set coat_ior and coat_roughness deliberately. Adding coat changes the base’s visible contribution instead of adding a second full energy budget.
RECORD	para 227	Gold / copper-like conductor base_metalness near 1; color selected for conductor response; tune specular_roughness. No independent full-strength diffuse dielectric term; colored reflectance is direction-dependent.
RECORD	para 228	Glass / colored liquid Use transmission_weight and physical IOR; encode interior attenuation via transmission_color + depth where supported. Object thickness and scene units affect absorption; check reflected and transmitted transport.
RECORD	Skin / wax / fruit Dielectric base with subsurface_weight and radius scale appro	Skin / wax / fruit Dielectric base with subsurface_weight and radius scale appropriate to object dimensions. Check energy across entry/exit; avoid using subsurface as a free brightness boost.
RECORD	para 230	Cloth / peach fuzz Enable fuzz gently above a stable base. Fuzz response and base attenuation remain consistent under changing view/light direction.
DEFINE	para 231	Paper / leaf Consider thin-walled behavior if geometry is physically a sheet; choose surface and transmission controls together. Front/back lighting, opacity and transmission should not each spend full incident energy.
RECORD	A disciplined tuning order	A disciplined tuning order
WHEN	para 234	Treat every extra lobe as a hypothesis. When a new parameter is enabled, ask: what physical path does this represent, what existing energy path does it occlude or replace, and what test would reveal an incorrect implementation?
