RECORD	para 254	16. Limits: physical principles versus a production model
DEFINE	Energy conservation is necessary, not sufficient	Energy conservation is necessary, not sufficient
RECORD	para 256	A material can conserve energy and still be the wrong material. Energy conservation alone does not guarantee the correct Fresnel curve, wavelength-dependent metal color, subsurface profile, refraction, anisotropy, polarization behavior or thin-film interference. It also does not define the artistic meaning of every control. Physically based models are approximations with chosen degrees of freedom. [1, 2, 4, 8]
DEFINE	RGB is a rendering approximation	RGB is a rendering approximation
RECORD	Reciprocity and transport conventions	Reciprocity and transport conventions
DEFINE	Art direction is not banned	Art direction is not banned
ALLOW	para 264	The OpenPBR website and MaterialX library are living documents. Current MaterialX specification material documents the OpenPBR Surface Shader Definition v1.1.1, while applications may advertise support for earlier revisions or a subset. Before comparing results, pin the specification and renderer version and record unsupported parameters. A shared model name alone is not enough. [1, 2, 10, 11]
RECORD	The practical standard	The practical standard A trustworthy OpenPBR implementation should explain its energy accounting, produce stable material behavior under changing illumination, and come with tests that reveal where it approximates or omits the standard.
RECORD	Open standard does not imply identical renderer implementation 19	Open standard does not imply identical renderer implementation 19
RECORD	para 270	Albedo / directional albedo Fraction of incident energy reflected by a scattering function under a defined directional integral; depends on convention and configuration.
RECORD	para 271	Absorption Conversion of optical energy to internal energy; removed from the rendered light field.
RECORD	para 272	BSDF Bidirectional scattering distribution function; umbrella for reflection and transmission.
RECORD	para 273	BRDF / BTDF Reflectance / transmittance distributions for light scattered from a surface.
RECORD	para 274	BSSRDF Subsurface distribution connecting incoming and outgoing light at potentially different surface points.
DEFINE	para 275	Dielectric Non-conducting optical material such as glass, water or plastic; interface reflection is governed by refractive index / Fresnel.
RECORD	para 276	Conductor Conductive material, typically a metal, modeled with complex-IOR / conductor Fresnel behavior.
RECORD	para 277	Fresnel Angle- and polarization-dependent reflection/transmission behavior at an interface.
RECORD	para 278	IOR (index of refraction) Dimensionless ratio that governs phase propagation and refraction; it affects dielectric Fresnel reflection.
RECORD	para 279	Microfacet A statistical model of rough surfaces as many tiny idealized facets.
RECORD	para 280	NDF / NDF roughness Normal distribution function describing microfacet orientations; roughness controls its width / shape.
RECORD	para 281	Masking-shadowing G Geometric attenuation term accounting for microfacets hidden along incident or outgoing directions.
RECORD	para 282	Multiple scattering Two or more scattering events within a rough microsurface or volume before light exits.
RECORD	para 283	Energy compensation A correction or model extension that restores physically appropriate energy omitted by a simplified scattering approximation.
RECORD	para 284	White furnace Uniform white environment test for energy preservation in designated lossless material configurations.
RECORD	para 285	OpenPBR Surface A standardized, artist-facing material model, maintained as part of the ASWF / MaterialX ecosystem.
RECORD	para 286	Beer-Lambert law Exponential attenuation of a beam along path length in a medium.
RECORD	para 287	Emission / EDF Light emitted by a material, treated separately from reflected incident illumination.
WHEN	para 288	Tone mapping A display transform that maps scene-linear radiance to a display range; it can obscure energy bugs if used for diagnosis.
RECORD	Five principles to remember	Five principles to remember
RECORD	para 290	1. Conserve the whole incident-light budget. 2. Couple diffuse and specular response through Fresnel / layer transmission. 3. Roughness changes distribution and can reveal missing microfacet multiple scattering. 4. Layering attenuates the base; it is not unweighted addition. 5. Validate with white-furnace and controlled linear-output tests before art-directing materials.
RECORD	Open standard does not imply identical renderer implementation 20	Open standard does not imply identical renderer implementation 20
RECORD	References and further reading	References and further reading
RECORD	para 294	Primary references are listed first because they define the model and its intended implementation. Accessed 9 October 2026. Links are included for direct verification. OpenPBR / MaterialX sources can change; pin release tags or repository commits for production validation.
RECORD	para 295	[1] Andersson, M. et al. OpenPBR Surface Specification. Academy Software Foundation, OpenPBR subproject of MaterialX. The specification covers the material structure, component BRDFs, energy accounting, layering and white-furnace testing. OpenPBR specification and parameter reference.
RECORD	para 296	[2] Harrysson, N.; Smythe, D.; Stone, J. et al. MaterialX Physically Based Shading Nodes, Version 1.39. Academy Software Foundation, 5 June 2026. Includes the scattering framework, microfacet reflectance model, directional albedo, energy compensation, vertical-layering transmittance and OpenPBR Surface shader definition (v1.1.1). Specification source.
RECORD	para 297	[3] Kajiya, J. T. (1986). “The Rendering Equation.” Proceedings of SIGGRAPH ’86, pp. 143–150. DOI: 10.1145/15922.15902. Foundational integral equation for light transport.
RECORD	para 298	[4] Cook, R. L.; Torrance, K. E. (1982). “A Reflectance Model for Computer Graphics.” ACM Transactions on Graphics 1(1), 7–24. DOI: 10.1145/357290.357293. Foundational physically motivated specular reflectance model.
RECORD	para 299	[5] Walter, B.; Marschner, S. R.; Li, H.; Torrance, K. E. (2007). “Microfacet Models for Refraction through Rough Surfaces.” EGSR 2007. Paper and supplemental material. Microfacet transmission and refraction, including rough dielectric surfaces.
RECORD	para 300	[6] Heitz, E.; Hanika, J.; d’Eon, E.; Dachsbacher, C. (2016). “Multiple-Scattering Microfacet BSDFs with the Smith Model.” ACM Transactions on Graphics 35(4). DOI: 10.1145/2897824.2925943. Derives multiple-scattering components for Smith-model microfacet BSDFs.
RECORD	para 301	[7] Heitz, E. (2014). “Understanding the Masking-Shadowing Function in Microfacet-Based BRDFs.” Journal of Computer Graphics Techniques 3(2), 48–107. Open-access article. Explains masking functions, visible microfacet normals and normalization constraints.
RECORD	para 302	[8] Burley, B. (2012). “Physically-Based Shading at Disney.” SIGGRAPH course notes. Publication page and PDF. Production experience in designing an artist-facing physically based BRDF.
RECORD	para 303	[9] Pharr, M.; Jakob, W.; Humphreys, G. Physically Based Rendering: From Theory to Implementation, 4th ed. 2023. Online book and implementation resources: pbrt.org; FAQ on energy conservation: pbrt FAQ.
RECORD	para 304	[10] Autodesk. “OpenPBR Surface.” Maya Lighting & Shading documentation, 2026. Summarizes material composition, energy conservation, layer behavior and white-furnace expectations in Maya. Documentation.
RECORD	para 305	[11] Adobe Substance 3D. “OpenPBR overview.” Updated 31 July 2026. Artist-facing overview of common parameters including roughness, IOR, specular response, metalness and transmission. Documentation.
RECORD	para 307	This guide paraphrases the sources above and adds explanatory diagrams and implementation-oriented checks. Equations are presented in simplified forms where labeled; the authoritative specifications define exact parameter semantics, remapping, edge cases and implementation details. Use the current OpenPBR source and MaterialX reference graph when writing a renderer, converting assets or establishing a conformance suite.
RECORD	End of document	End of document
RECORD	OpenPBR Rendering & Energy Conservation • Prepared 9 October 2026	OpenPBR Rendering & Energy Conservation • Prepared 9 October 2026
