RECORD	para 1	# Android 4.x Holo Theme Schema
RECORD	para 2	**Schema ID:** `android-holo-4x`
RECORD	para 3	**Target:** Android 4.0 Ice Cream Sandwich through Android 4.4 KitKat (API 14–19). The core Holo theme family began in Android 3.0/API 11; `Theme.Holo.Light.DarkActionBar` was introduced in API 14.
RECORD	para 4	**Purpose:** A practical, source-backed design and implementation schema for recreating the native Android Holo visual language in an application, prototype, shell, or component library.
RECORD	para 5	**Evidence convention:** - **AOSP value** means the value/name is taken from Android Open Source Project framework resources. - **Platform guidance** means it is documented by Android’s original developer/design material. - **Schema recommendation** means a practical consistency rule derived for this spec; it should not be mistaken for a mandatory Google-published design token.
RECORD	para 7	## 1. Design target and identity
DEFINE	para 8	Holo is the dark/light system theme family introduced in Android 3.x and refined/standardized in Android 4.0. Android 4.0 defined three main Holo themes:
RECORD	para 9	| Theme | Intended appearance | Availability | |---|---|---| | `@android:style/Theme.Holo` | Dark surfaces, light foregrounds, cyan-blue interactive accent | API 11+ | | `@android:style/Theme.Holo.Light` | Light gray surfaces, dark foregrounds, cyan-blue interactive accent | API 11+ | | `@android:style/Theme.Holo.Light.DarkActionBar` | Light content area with a dark action bar | API 14+ |
WHEN	para 10	**Use the named Holo theme explicitly** when matching the historical platform appearance. Android 4.0's `Theme.DeviceDefault` was allowed to reflect a device manufacturer's design; it is not a reliable synonym for the fixed Holo look. See [Holo Everywhere](https://android-developers.googleblog.com/2012/01/holo-everywhere.html).
DEFINE	para 14	The framework source defines the colors below. Hex values use `#AARRGGBB` when eight digits are shown; opaque equivalents are also listed for convenience.
WHEN	para 17	**Canonical accent rule:** Use `#33B5E5` as the first-choice Holo accent when custom controls need a signature Holo blue. Do not recolor every element blue: surfaces and text remain neutral, and green/orange/red are reserved for meaning or state.
RECORD	para 18	### 2.2 Color state rules
RECORD	para 20	Keep theme-specific text colors separate: dark-theme widgets sit on black/dark surfaces and light-theme widgets sit on `#F3F3F3`. Do not apply a single foreground color to both variants.
WHEN	para 22	**Typeface:** Roboto is the defining Ice Cream Sandwich-era type family. Use Roboto rather than a modern rounded geometric font when visual fidelity matters. Android framework font fallback remains acceptable on systems where Roboto is not available.
RECORD	para 23	The platform's base text appearance resources define these common size levels:
RECORD	para 25	For application-authored text, use **sp** so user font scaling works. The Action Bar entries above document the original framework resource definitions rather than recommending dp for new text styles.
RECORD	para 28	## 4. Layout and dimensions
DEFINE	para 29	These are implementation baselines, not a universal grid guarantee for every Android 4.x layout.
RECORD	para 34	### 5.1 App chrome and Action Bar
RECORD	para 38	### 5.3 Text fields and search
RECORD	para 39	- Use Holo EditText/search widgets or custom equivalents that preserve the native field outline/underline, selection handles, hint treatment, and focus indication. - Keep hint text visually secondary to entered text. - Show validation text near the relevant field and avoid color-only error indication. - Ensure selection, cursor, and focus remain visible on both dark and light variants.
RECORD	para 40	### 5.4 Checkboxes, radio buttons, switches, seek bars, and progress
RECORD	para 42	### 5.5 Lists and preference screens
RECORD	para 44	### 5.6 Dialogs, menus, and popups
RECORD	para 48	## 6. Interaction and motion
RECORD	para 49	Every interactive element should support the following applicable states:
RECORD	para 50	```text normal pressed focused selected_or_activated checked disabled error_or_invalid loading_or_progress ```
RECORD	para 52	## 7. Implementation: native Android XML
RECORD	para 53	### 7.1 Select the historical theme explicitly
RECORD	para 54	For a native Android 4.x application, use the framework theme directly:
RECORD	para 55	```xml <application android:theme="@android:style/Theme.Holo" ... > </application> ```
RECORD	para 56	For the light variant:
RECORD	para 57	```xml <application android:theme="@android:style/Theme.Holo.Light" ... > </application> ```
RECORD	para 58	For light content with the dark Action Bar (API 14+):
RECORD	para 59	```xml <application android:theme="@android:style/Theme.Holo.Light.DarkActionBar" ... > </application> ```
RECORD	para 61	Prefer deriving app-specific styles from framework Holo styles and changing only the necessary values. The original Android framework guidance explicitly identifies `Widget.Holo` styles as stable parents for incremental customization on compatible Android versions.
RECORD	para 62	Example theme inheritance:
RECORD	para 63	```xml <resources> <style name="AppTheme" parent="@android:style/Theme.Holo"> <!-- Add only intentional app-specific overrides here. --> </style>
RECORD	para 64	<style name="AppActionButton" parent="@android:style/Widget.Holo.Button"> <!-- Add only necessary widget-level overrides here. --> </style> </resources> ```
WHEN	para 65	Do not copy a modern Material theme into an Android 4.x project and assume that it reproduces Holo. Also avoid setting attributes introduced after API 19 directly in resources loaded on Android 4.x unless version-qualified resources or a compatibility library handles them. In particular, do not treat modern Material `colorPrimary`/`colorAccent` tokens as the original Holo contract.
RECORD	para 66	### 7.3 Theme-aware dimensions and selectors
REQUIRE	para 67	When custom widgets must match the system, prefer framework-provided theme attributes for selectable backgrounds, preferred list-item dimensions, padding, and text appearances. The 2012 Holo guidance gives examples such as `?android:attr/selectableItemBackground`, `?android:attr/listPreferredItemHeight`, `?android:attr/listPreferredItemPaddingLeft`, and `?android:attr/textAppearanceListItem`.
RECORD	para 68	## 8. Custom UI / non-Android renderers
WHEN	para 69	If implementing Holo in a custom shell, game UI, desktop UI, or a web renderer instead of native Android widgets, map each native concept to a named token rather than copying colors ad hoc.
DEFINE	para 71	This YAML is a **portable schema proposal**, not an Android resource file and not a verbatim Google data format. Native Android resource values and theme inheritance remain authoritative for native implementations.
DEFINE	para 73	A recreation is Holo-faithful when all applicable items pass:
DEFINE	para 76	The sources below are intended to be directly checkable. The AOSP files are primary implementation evidence; Android's archived design/developer guidance explains the intended family and usage.
RECORD	para 77	1. **Android Developers Blog — “Holo Everywhere” (3 January 2012).** Defines the Holo family, its cross-device compatibility intent, explicit theme selection, `DeviceDefault` distinction, theme metrics, and theme-aware widget examples. https://android-developers.googleblog.com/2012/01/holo-everywhere.html
RECORD	para 78	2. **Android Developers — Android 4.0 / Ice Cream Sandwich highlights.** Describes the refined unified phone/tablet UI, visibility of common actions, interaction feedback, and the typeface update. https://developer.android.com/about/versions/android-4.0-highlights
RECORD	para 79	3. **Android Open Source Project — `colors_holo.xml`.** Primary source for Holo palette names and exact color values, including `holo_blue_light`, `holo_blue_dark`, Holo green/red/orange/purple, and Holo surfaces. https://android.googlesource.com/platform/frameworks/base/+/6d2c0e5/core/res/res/values/colors_holo.xml
