RECORD	para 86	### Attribution and interpretation note
RECORD	para 87	Android framework source files are published under the Apache License 2.0. This Markdown document is a new specification assembled from the cited references; the portable YAML block, layout recommendations, and fidelity checklist are schema guidance, not official Google-authored standards. For strict pixel-perfect recreation, compare against an Android 4.0–4.4 emulator and the matching framework resources for the target API level.
RECORD	CLOUD TYPES, FRONTS, AND ALTITUDES | Field reference Page 1	CLOUD TYPES, FRONTS, AND ALTITUDES | Field reference Page 1
RECORD	CLOUDS OF THE ATMOSPHERE	CLOUDS OF THE ATMOSPHERE
RECORD	A field guide to cloud types, altitude, warm fronts, cold fronts, and the struct	A field guide to cloud types, altitude, warm fronts, cold fronts, and the structures between
RECORD	para 12	16Approximate altitude (km above ground)
RECORD	Ci Cs Cc	Ci Cs Cc
RECORD	Ns Sc St Cu Cb	Ns Sc St Cu Cb
RECORD	para 16	Typical simplified envelopes, not hard boundaries. Cloud bases and tops shift with latitude, season, weather, and terrain.
RECORD	para 17	What this guide does: catalogues the ten internationally recognized cloud genera, their common species and notable visual features; explains approximate cloud-base and cloud-top heights; and connects cloud structure to frontal weather systems. It is designed to be useful for observing the real sky, not only memorizing names.
RECORD	para 18	One important rule: a cloud name describes what is observed. It does not, by itself, prove which weather system produced it. Height, shape, precipitation, motion, wind, pressure trend, and the wider synoptic pattern should be read together.
RECORD	para 19	Reference basis: World Meteorological Organization (WMO) International Cloud Atlas; NOAA/National Weather Service educational material; Met Office cloud guides; and peer-reviewed research on frontal cloud and precipitation organization.
RECORD	CLOUD TYPES, FRONTS, AND ALTITUDES | Field reference Page 2	CLOUD TYPES, FRONTS, AND ALTITUDES | Field reference Page 2
DEFINE	para 21	1. How cloud height is described
RECORD	para 22	A cloud has a base (its lowest visible level) and a top (its highest visible level). Cloud classification by level usually refers mainly to where the cloud occurs or where its base lies, not to a hard ceiling. A shallow cloud can be low; a deep convective cloud can cross low, middle, and high levels.
RECORD	Level /	Level / description Approximate range in mid-latitudes Common genera Interpretation
RECORD	High Base often above about 5 km	High Base often above about 5 km (16,500 ft); commonly 6-13 km Cirrus, Cirrostratus, Cirrocumulus Mostly ice crystals; often thin, fibrous, veil-like, or tiny ripples
RECORD	para 26	Middle Often 2-7 km (6,500-23,000 ft) Altocumulus, Altostratus;
RECORD	Nimbostratus often spans levels Water droplets, ice, or a mix depending on tempe	Nimbostratus often spans levels Water droplets, ice, or a mix depending on temperature and depth
DEFINE	Low Surface to about 2 km (0-6,500	Low Surface to about 2 km (0-6,500 ft) Stratus, Stratocumulus, Cumulus bases, Cumulonimbus bases Liquid droplets are common; can obscure terrain or form fog
ALLOW	para 29	Deep / vertical Base may be low; top may reach 8-16 km or the tropopause Cumulus congestus, Cumulonimbus Strong vertical motion; can cross multiple altitude bands
ALLOW	para 30	Use feet and kilometres as estimates, not rigid definitions. Pilot reports, ceilometers, radiosondes, lidar, and satellite retrievals can give more precise heights. The same named cloud may sit at different altitudes in different climates.
RECORD	CLOUD TYPES, FRONTS, AND ALTITUDES | Field reference Page 3	CLOUD TYPES, FRONTS, AND ALTITUDES | Field reference Page 3
RECORD	para 32	2. The naming system: genera, species, varieties, features
RECORD	para 33	The international system uses ten main genera. A genus is the broad category: for example, Cirrus or Cumulonimbus. More detailed labels describe shape, arrangement, transparency, or attached features. They are not all separate genera, and not every combination is allowed.
RECORD	Name layer What it tells you Examples	Name layer What it tells you Examples
RECORD	Genus Main cloud family Cirrus (Ci), Altocumulus (Ac), Cumulonimbus (Cb)	Genus Main cloud family Cirrus (Ci), Altocumulus (Ac), Cumulonimbus (Cb)
RECORD	para 36	Species Shape or internal structure Cirrus uncinus; Cumulus congestus; Cumulonimbus capillatus
RECORD	para 37	Variety Transparency, layering, or pattern across the sky undulatus (wave pattern), radiatus (bands), opacus (opaque)
RECORD	Supplementary feature A recognizable structure attached to or merged with a	Supplementary feature A recognizable structure attached to or merged with a cloud incus (anvil), mamma (pouches), asperitas (rough waves), virga (fall streaks that evaporate)
RECORD	para 39	Accessory cloud A smaller associated cloud nearby or attached pileus (cap), velum (veil), pannus (ragged low cloud), flumen (beaver tail)
RECORD	Special /	Special / human-influenced forms A cloud linked to a specific local source or modification contrail cloud (homogenitus), wildfire-generated cloud (flammagenitus)
WHEN	para 41	Useful word roots: cirro- / cirrus = high, wispy; alto- = middle-level in cloud names; strato- = layered; cumulo- = heaped or puffy; nimbo- / -nimbus = precipitation-bearing. The prefix alto- can be misleading if read as ordinary English: it generally signals a middle cloud, not the highest cloud.
ALLOW	para 42	Cloud names may be combined in a formal observation, such as Cumulonimbus capillatus incus: a cumulonimbus of the fibrous-topped species, bearing an anvil feature. The formal system has many more combinations than the ten basic genera.
RECORD	CLOUD TYPES, FRONTS, AND ALTITUDES | Field reference Page 4	CLOUD TYPES, FRONTS, AND ALTITUDES | Field reference Page 4
RECORD	para 44	3. High clouds: Cirrus, Cirrostratus, Cirrocumulus
ALLOW	para 45	High clouds are commonly composed mainly of ice crystals because temperatures are very cold. They may look bright white in daylight and can appear yellow, orange, or red near sunrise and sunset. They are often distant from the surface weather that eventually reaches an observer.
RECORD	CIRRUS (Ci) - fibres, wisps, hooks	CIRRUS (Ci) - fibres, wisps, hooks
RECORD	para 48	Common species: fibratus (fine threads, mostly straight or gently curved); uncinus (hooks or comma-like heads, often with a trailing brush); spissatus (dense, thick patches); castellanus (turreted tops indicating vertical structure); floccus (small tufts with ragged bases). Look for changes in coverage and direction over time.
RECORD	CIRROSTRATUS (Cs) - high veil	CIRROSTRATUS (Cs) - high veil
DEFINE	para 51	Common species: fibratus, a fibrous veil with threads; nebulosus, a more uniform, nebulous sheet. Cirrostratus can be so thin that a halo is more obvious than the cloud itself.
RECORD	CIRROCUMULUS (Cc) - very small ripples or grains	CIRROCUMULUS (Cc) - very small ripples or grains
RECORD	para 53	Typical height: about 6-12 km (20,000-40,000 ft). Appearance: tiny white cloudlets, grains, or ripples, usually with little or no shading. It can form a “mackerel sky” texture. Common species: stratiformis (widespread sheet with elements), lenticularis (lens-shaped), castellanus (small turrets), floccus (tufted elements). Weather clues: indicates structure or waves aloft, but is not a stand-alone forecast of rain.
RECORD	para 54	High-cloud observation tip: note whether the cover is increasing, whether cloud elements are spreading into a veil, the direction of upper-level motion, and whether a halo forms. A trend over several hours is more informative than a single cloud photograph.
RECORD	CLOUD TYPES, FRONTS, AND ALTITUDES | Field reference Page 5	CLOUD TYPES, FRONTS, AND ALTITUDES | Field reference Page 5
RECORD	para 56	4. Middle clouds: Altocumulus, Altostratus, Nimbostratus
RECORD	ALTOCUMULUS (Ac) - rolls, patches, or rounded elements	ALTOCUMULUS (Ac) - rolls, patches, or rounded elements
ALLOW	para 58	Typical layer: roughly 2-7 km (6,500-23,000 ft), with substantial variation. Appearance: rounded patches or rolls, often larger and more shaded than cirrocumulus. Elements may be arranged in sheets or parallel bands. Composition: liquid droplets, ice crystals, or both, depending on temperature and vertical depth.
WHEN	para 60	Weather clues: altocumulus castellanus in the morning can signal instability aloft and a possible risk of later showers or thunderstorms if moisture and lift are sufficient. Lenticular altocumulus points to wave motion, not automatically to approaching precipitation.
RECORD	ALTOSTRATUS (As) - broad grey-blue sheet	ALTOSTRATUS (As) - broad grey-blue sheet
RECORD	NIMBOSTRATUS (Ns) - thick precipitation layer	NIMBOSTRATUS (Ns) - thick precipitation layer
ALLOW	para 65	Important distinction: Nimbostratus tends to produce widespread, relatively continuous precipitation. Cumulonimbus produces convective showers or storms with stronger local variability, and may bring lightning, hail, or damaging wind.
RECORD	CLOUD TYPES, FRONTS, AND ALTITUDES | Field reference Page 6	CLOUD TYPES, FRONTS, AND ALTITUDES | Field reference Page 6
RECORD	para 67	5. Low clouds: Stratus and Stratocumulus
RECORD	STRATUS (St) - low, flat, grey layer	STRATUS (St) - low, flat, grey layer
RECORD	para 70	Common species: nebulosus (uniform fog-like layer); fractus (ragged shreds, often under precipitation clouds and sometimes called scud in informal weather discussion). Stratus near the ground can reduce visibility substantially.
RECORD	STRATOCUMULUS (Sc) - low rolls and cloud cells	STRATOCUMULUS (Sc) - low rolls and cloud cells
ALLOW	para 72	Typical base: surface to about 2 km (0-6,500 ft). Appearance: low lumpy masses with broad elements, often in rows or sheets; dark gaps or shading distinguish it from the more delicate high cloudlets. Formation: commonly the top of a moist boundary layer cooled beneath an inversion; it may develop from a broken-up stratus deck, shallow convection, or air flowing over terrain.
ALLOW	para 73	Species: stratiformis (sheet); lenticularis (lens-shaped); castellanus (turrets); floccus (tufts); volutus (roll). Varieties/patterns: undulatus (waves), radiatus (bands that appear to converge or diverge in perspective), perlucidus (gaps between elements), opacus (opaque sheet). Asperitas and fluctus may add distinctive underside or wave structures.
ALLOW	para 74	What low clouds tell you: a persistent low deck often signals moisture trapped under a stable layer. A clearing trend after a front may begin with broken stratocumulus, while renewed moist advection can rebuild the deck. Surface wind, temperature, dew point, and visibility are especially useful because low clouds are close to the boundary layer.
RECORD	CLOUD TYPES, FRONTS, AND ALTITUDES | Field reference Page 7	CLOUD TYPES, FRONTS, AND ALTITUDES | Field reference Page 7
RECORD	para 77	6. Vertically developed clouds: Cumulus and
RECORD	CUMULUS (Cu) - detached heaps with a relatively flat base	CUMULUS (Cu) - detached heaps with a relatively flat base
ALLOW	para 80	Typical base: often 0.5-2.5 km, depending on moisture and surface heating. Appearance: cauliflower-like towers, bright sunlit tops, and a flatter base near the level where rising air first reaches saturation. Mechanism: buoyant thermals rise from heated surfaces or are lifted by terrain or convergence. If the air is stable or dry above, growth may stop.
WHEN	para 81	Species and practical meaning: fractus - ragged, changing fragments, often in turbulent or precipitating environments; humilis - small fair-weather puffs with limited vertical depth; mediocris - moderate vertical growth; congestus - tall cauliflower towers with vigorous updrafts and potential for showers. Cumulus congestus can grow into cumulonimbus if the updraft and moisture sustain deeper development.
RECORD	CUMULONIMBUS (Cb) - thunderstorm cloud	CUMULONIMBUS (Cb) - thunderstorm cloud
ALLOW	para 83	Typical base: often around 0.5-2 km, but lower or higher bases occur. Top: may reach 8-16 km and often spreads near the tropopause; strong storms can briefly overshoot it. The vertical extent is more important than a single “cloud height.”
ALLOW	para 84	Appearance and physics: deep convective tower with strong updrafts and downdrafts. Liquid droplets form in lower warm regions; supercooled water, ice crystals, graupel, and hail may coexist in colder regions. The glaciated upper cloud can spread into an anvil (incus) in strong upper-level flow. Precipitation loading and evaporation can help generate downdrafts and gust fronts.
RECORD	para 86	Safety note: an approaching cumulonimbus can produce lightning, hail, severe gusts, flash flooding, and tornadoes in some storms. Do not use cloud appearance alone to judge safe distance; thunder means lightning is close enough to be dangerous.
RECORD	CLOUD TYPES, FRONTS, AND ALTITUDES | Field reference Page 8	CLOUD TYPES, FRONTS, AND ALTITUDES | Field reference Page 8
RECORD	para 88	7. A broader cloud atlas: species, varieties, and special forms
RECORD	para 89	The table below lists the major species commonly taught from the WMO system. Some are subtle in the field and several only occur with particular genera. The WMO atlas is the controlling reference for exact formal combinations.
RECORD	Genus Species (common formal set) How to recognize it	Genus Species (common formal set) How to recognize it
RECORD	para 91	Cirrus fibratus; uncinus; spissatus; castellanus; floccus Threads, hooks, dense fibrous patches, turrets, or tufted ends
RECORD	para 92	Cirrostratus fibratus; nebulosus Fibrous or uniform transparent ice-crystal veil
RECORD	para 93	Cirrocumulus stratiformis; lenticularis; castellanus; floccus Fine sheets of tiny grains, lenses, turrets, or tufts
RECORD	para 94	Altocumulus stratiformis; lenticularis; castellanus; floccus; volutus Patches or rolls, wave lenses, turrets, tufts, or rolls
RECORD	para 95	Altostratus No species routinely assigned; varieties include translucidus and opacus Sun weakly visible through thinner sheet, or obscured by opaque layer
RECORD	para 96	Nimbostratus No species routinely assigned in the WMO genus table Deep, widespread precipitating layer
RECORD	para 97	Stratocumulus stratiformis; lenticularis; castellanus; floccus; volutus Low lumps, cells, waves, lenses, tufts, or rolls
RECORD	para 98	Stratus nebulosus; fractus Uniform low layer or ragged shreds
RECORD	para 99	Cumulus fractus; humilis; mediocris; congestus Ragged fragments through progressively taller towers
RECORD	para 100	Cumulonimbus calvus; capillatus Smooth swollen top transitioning to fibrous/glaciated upper cloud
ALLOW	para 103	Accessory clouds: pileus is a smooth cap over a rising tower; velum is a veil intersecting a convective cloud; pannus consists of ragged shreds often under a precipitating deck; flumen is an inflow band linked to some supercells. These may coexist with a parent cloud and are not separate genera.
RECORD	CLOUD TYPES, FRONTS, AND ALTITUDES | Field reference Page 9	CLOUD TYPES, FRONTS, AND ALTITUDES | Field reference Page 9
RECORD	para 105	8. Special clouds and clouds with non-weather origins
RECORD	Name / type Typical appearance Origin or process	Name / type Typical appearance Origin or process
RECORD	para 107	Contrail cloud (homogenitus) Narrow streaks behind aircraft; can widen into ice-cloud sheets Aircraft exhaust adds water and particles; persistence depends strongly on upper-level humidity and temperature
RECORD	para 108	Homomutatus An initially narrow contrail evolves into a wider, natural-looking cirrus-like veil Wind shear spreads ice crystals and the cloud field changes form
RECORD	para 109	Flammagenitus Cloud plume over intense fire or wildfire Fire-driven buoyancy and moisture; large pyroconvective events can reach great heights
RECORD	para 110	Cataractagenitus Local cloud or fog near a major waterfall Waterfall spray and local cooling/moistening
RECORD	para 111	Silvagenitus Localized cloud in very moist air above forest canopy Evapotranspiration contributes moisture, with local lifting and cooling
RECORD	para 112	Lenticular clouds Smooth lens or stacked saucer shapes, often over mountains Standing mountain waves; cloud forms in ascent and evaporates in descent while air flows through
RECORD	para 113	Roll cloud / volutus Long horizontal tube that appears to roll Often tied to outflow boundaries or particular wave/boundary-layer structures; mechanism depends on setting
RECORD	Hole-punch / cavum Circular or linear hole in a thin supercooled cloud with ice	Hole-punch / cavum Circular or linear hole in a thin supercooled cloud with ice streaks nearby Ice formation triggers glaciation and precipitation that removes droplets in the hole
RECORD	para 115	Mammatus Rounded pouches hanging from a cloud base, often an anvil Cloud-scale mixing, evaporation, and hydrometeor processes; not by itself proof of a tornado
RECORD	para 116	Kelvin-Helmholtz / fluctus Breaking wave crests resembling ocean waves Strong vertical wind shear at a stable interface between layers
DEFINE	para 117	Asperitas Chaotic, wave-textured underside Gravity-wave and turbulent cloud-layer structure; visual pattern alone is not a severe-weather warning
RECORD	para 118	The WMO system uses terms ending in -genitus for a locally generated cloud and -mutatus for a cloud form that has changed through a particular process. These categories do not replace the ordinary genus; they add information about origin or transformation. Human-created clouds can still participate in ordinary atmospheric physics, including ice growth, radiation, and spreading by wind.
RECORD	para 119	Not every dramatic cloud has a single distinctive weather meaning. A visual feature can reveal local airflow or microphysics but often cannot identify the full storm environment without radar, satellite, surface observations, and vertical profiles.
RECORD	CLOUD TYPES, FRONTS, AND ALTITUDES | Field reference Page 10	CLOUD TYPES, FRONTS, AND ALTITUDES | Field reference Page 10
RECORD	para 121	9. Warm fronts: broad ascent and layered cloud shields
WHEN	para 122	A warm front advances when a warmer air mass replaces colder air at the surface. Because cold air is denser, the advancing warm air usually cannot simply bulldoze underneath it; instead it glides up and over the shallow cold air along a gently sloping frontal surface. The ascent can extend hundreds of kilometres ahead of the surface front.
RECORD	WARM AIR RISING GRADUALLY	WARM AIR RISING GRADUALLY
RECORD	COLD AIR AT SURFACE	COLD AIR AT SURFACE
RECORD	para 128	Schematic only - actual frontal slopes and cloud bands vary.
ALLOW	para 129	Why the cloud layers form: as moist air ascends, pressure falls and the air expands. Expansion cools it; once it reaches saturation, droplets and/or ice crystals form. Because uplift is broad and comparatively gradual, cloud tends to occupy a large shield and may appear as successive layers at different heights.
RECORD	para 130	A textbook approach sequence (not a promise):
RECORD	High ice wisps appear	High ice wisps appear far ahead 2. Cirrostratus
ALLOW	para 133	A veil spreads; halo may form 3. Altostratus
RECORD	para 134	Sky turns grey; Sun looks dim 4. Nimbostratus
RECORD	para 135	Deep layer; widespread rain or snow 5. Low cloud
ALLOW	Stratus or fragments	Stratus or fragments may follow
RECORD	para 137	Precipitation geography: the broad area of ascent and precipitation is commonly ahead of the surface warm front, on its cold side, though exact placement depends on the cyclone, front slope, dry slots, and temperature profile. Precipitation can begin aloft and evaporate in a dry lower layer, producing virga or a sub-cloud layer that gradually moistens.
ALLOW	para 138	What changes at passage: temperatures and dew points often rise, winds shift, and the air mass becomes warmer or more humid. Cloud cover may break in the warm sector, but stratus, stratocumulus, fog, or continued rain can persist if moisture and stability remain.
WHEN	para 139	Warm-front pattern to remember: high cloud often arrives first; the deck thickens and lowers; steady precipitation becomes more likely as the cloud deepens. This sequence is a useful pattern, not a fixed timetable. Some warm fronts have little cloud or precipitation if the air is dry, ascent is weak, or the vertical structure differs.
RECORD	CLOUD TYPES, FRONTS, AND ALTITUDES | Field reference Page 11	CLOUD TYPES, FRONTS, AND ALTITUDES | Field reference Page 11
RECORD	para 141	10. Cold fronts: sharper lifting and convective clouds
WHEN	para 142	A cold front is the leading boundary of colder air displacing warmer air. The dense cold air tends to wedge underneath the warm air. The resulting lift is often more abrupt than along a warm front, especially when the front is strong and the warm sector is moist and unstable.
RECORD	COLD AIR WEDGES UNDER WARM MOIST AIR Cumulonimbus / tower	COLD AIR WEDGES UNDER WARM MOIST AIR Cumulonimbus / tower
ALLOW	Anvil may spread downwind	Anvil may spread downwind
RECORD	COLD AIR WARM AIR	COLD AIR WARM AIR
RECORD	para 146	Schematic only - cold fronts can also be shallow, diffuse, or mostly layered.
ALLOW	para 147	Common cloud development: cumulus may deepen into congestus and cumulonimbus along or just ahead of the front. Where instability is limited, the cloud band can be shallow or mostly stratiform. A cold front does not automatically mean thunderstorms; moisture, instability, lift, and wind shear determine what develops.
RECORD	para 148	A common cold-front passage pattern:
RECORD	para 150	Warm, moist air; Cu or Ac may grow 2. Front
DEFINE	Towering Cu / Cb or a rain	Towering Cu / Cb or a rain band if lift is strong 3. Passage
RECORD	para 152	Showers, gusts, wind shift, temperature drop 4. Behind
RECORD	para 153	Cooler air; broken Cu/Sc, then possible clearing
ALLOW	para 155	Post-frontal clouds: cold air moving over a relatively warm surface can create shallow convection, cloud streets, cumulus, or stratocumulus. If the air mass is dry or subsidence is strong, the sky may clear rapidly. Over water, cold-air outbreaks can organize striking bands of clouds and snow showers.
RECORD	para 156	Cold-front pattern to remember: look for a boundary, a change in wind and temperature, and a narrow or broken band of stronger cloud growth near the leading edge. The most intense cloud is not necessarily exactly on the surface front; upper-level forcing, terrain, and storm outflow can shift it.
RECORD	CLOUD TYPES, FRONTS, AND ALTITUDES | Field reference Page 12	CLOUD TYPES, FRONTS, AND ALTITUDES | Field reference Page 12
RECORD	para 158	11. Fronts compared: stationary, occluded, and mixed cases
RECORD	Front / boundary Lift and cloud pattern Typical precipitation / caveats	Front / boundary Lift and cloud pattern Typical precipitation / caveats
RECORD	para 160	Warm front Warm air glides over cooler surface air along a gentle slope; often broad, layered cloud shield Can produce long-duration steady rain/snow ahead of the surface front; classic
DEFINE	Ci -> Cs -> As -> Ns sequence is common but not universal	Ci -> Cs -> As -> Ns sequence is common but not universal
ALLOW	para 162	Cold front Colder air wedges beneath warmer air; steeper lift can deepen clouds rapidly Often showers or thunderstorms in a relatively narrow band, but may be dry, shallow, or stratiform
RECORD	para 163	Stationary front Boundary moves little; persistent convergence and lift may repeatedly form clouds Cloud and rain can linger over one region for many hours or days; local waves can intensify rain
DEFINE	Occluded front Cold front catches a warm front, lifting much of the warm sector	Occluded front Cold front catches a warm front, lifting much of the warm sector off the ground Cloud and precipitation can wrap around the low; character depends on which air mass is colder at the surface
ALLOW	Dryline (not a temperature	Dryline (not a temperature front) Boundary between moist and dry air, often in the Great Plains; convergence can lift moist air Can trigger severe storms when instability, cap erosion, and upper-level forcing align; cloud may be limited on the dry side
WHEN	para 166	Outflow boundary Cool downdraft air spreads outward from a storm Can create a new line of cumulus or storms when it meets warm moist air
ALLOW	para 167	Occlusion: in a mature mid-latitude cyclone, the faster-moving cold front may catch up with the warm front. The warm air is lifted above the surface, and the surface pattern becomes an occluded front. The cloud field is often extensive and layered, but embedded convection is possible.
ALLOW	para 168	Stationary fronts: repeated lift over a nearly fixed boundary can maintain cloudiness or precipitation. If waves develop along the boundary, one section may intensify even while another weakens. A stationary front is a motion description, not a cloud type.
RECORD	para 169	Frontal cloud systems in research: real cyclones contain multiple scales of cloud organization: broad frontal shields, narrow rainbands, embedded convective cells, and post-frontal bands. The exact arrangement varies with vertical wind shear, stability, moisture, topography, and the cyclone stage.
RECORD	CLOUD TYPES, FRONTS, AND ALTITUDES | Field reference Page 13	CLOUD TYPES, FRONTS, AND ALTITUDES | Field reference Page 13
RECORD	para 171	12. Why the same front can produce different cloud heights
DEFINE	para 172	Cloud height is the result of thermodynamics and dynamics, not a fixed altitude assigned to a weather front. Several variables can radically change the observed cloud deck.
RECORD	Control Effect on cloud height or shape Practical sign	Control Effect on cloud height or shape Practical sign
RECORD	para 174	Moisture profile A saturated layer starting low makes low cloud; a moist layer aloft can form Altocumulus or Altostratus without a low deck Compare dew point with temperature at the surface and, if available, a sounding
RECORD	para 175	Stability / inversion An inversion caps vertical growth and favours stratus or stratocumulus; instability allows cumulus to deepen Flat tops suggest a cap; growing towers suggest stronger buoyancy
RECORD	para 176	Lifting strength Gentle, widespread ascent favours layered clouds; concentrated strong ascent can build towers Broad solid deck versus localized cauliflower growth
RECORD	Temperature and freezing	Temperature and freezing level Cloud phase changes from mostly liquid to mixed phase and ice as temperature drops; the level varies with air mass High cloud can be ice-dominated; thunderstorms contain supercooled water and ice aloft
RECORD	Wind shear Tilts towers, spreads anvils, shapes bands, and can create waves at l	Wind shear Tilts towers, spreads anvils, shapes bands, and can create waves at layer boundaries Anvil displaced downwind; repeating wave-like cloud features
ALLOW	para 179	Terrain Air forced up windward slopes may cool and saturate; lee waves form lenticular clouds Cloud hugging a ridge or standing in a smooth lens downwind
DEFINE	para 180	Latitude and tropopause The upper limit of the troposphere is higher in the tropics and lower near the poles Tropical anvils can be higher than comparable mid-latitude anvils
RECORD	para 181	Time of day / surface heating Heating creates thermals and can deepen cumulus; nighttime cooling can create stratus or fog Clouds build afternoon, or low cloud/fog forms overnight
RECORD	para 183	Cloud top is often tied to a stable layer: a capping inversion can flatten cumulus; a tropopause can stop or spread thunderstorm outflow into an anvil; strong updrafts can penetrate briefly into the lower stratosphere. These are common patterns, not hard geometric boundaries.
RECORD	CLOUD TYPES, FRONTS, AND ALTITUDES | Field reference Page 14	CLOUD TYPES, FRONTS, AND ALTITUDES | Field reference Page 14
RECORD	para 185	13. How clouds produce rain, snow, virga, and ice
WHEN	para 186	Clouds are not simply water vapour made visible. Visible cloud is made of liquid droplets, ice crystals, or both. Water vapour is invisible. A cloud forms when air reaches saturation and water condenses or deposits onto microscopic particles; later growth processes determine whether precipitation reaches the ground.
RECORD	para 187	Warm-cloud growth: in clouds above freezing, droplets collide and coalesce. Turbulence and the size distribution of droplets influence how quickly some grow into drizzle or rain drops. Larger drops fall relative to smaller ones and can collect them.
RECORD	para 188	Mixed-phase and cold-cloud growth: below 0 C, liquid droplets can remain supercooled. In many clouds, ice crystals grow at the expense of supercooled droplets because saturation vapour pressure over ice is lower than over liquid water. Ice crystals may aggregate, and riming can accrete supercooled droplets to form graupel or contribute to hail in strong updrafts.
ALLOW	para 189	Virga: precipitation falls out of a cloud but evaporates or sublimates before reaching the surface. A dry sub-cloud layer can therefore show visible streaks under Altocumulus, Cumulus, or other clouds without rain measured on the ground. As precipitation moistens the lower air, the streaks may eventually reach the surface.
ALLOW	para 191	Frontal precipitation: warm fronts often have large regions of layered cloud and broad ascent, making sustained precipitation likely. Cold fronts can contain convective rainbands where ascent is concentrated. Both may have embedded cells and both can produce rain or snow, depending on the atmospheric column.
RECORD	para 192	Three observations to separate cloud from precipitation: (1) cloud form and vertical extent, (2) whether streaks reach the ground, and (3) the temperature profile between cloud and surface. A dark base alone does not identify precipitation type or intensity.
RECORD	CLOUD TYPES, FRONTS, AND ALTITUDES | Field reference Page 15	CLOUD TYPES, FRONTS, AND ALTITUDES | Field reference Page 15
RECORD	para 194	14. Field method: identify what you see and infer cautiously
DEFINE	para 195	Use the following order when looking at the sky. It is deliberately practical: first record visible evidence, then infer a process, then check whether nearby observations support that inference.
RECORD	Step Question Useful evidence	Step Question Useful evidence
DEFINE	para 197	1. Level Where is the base? Is the cloud shallow or vertically deep? Terrain reference, aircraft reports, ceilometer, nearby cloud layers; remember perspective can mislead
RECORD	para 198	2. Shape Fibrous, veil, rounded elements, flat deck, tower, anvil, wave, or ragged fragments? Assign a likely genus before trying a species or feature
DEFINE	para 199	3. Motion Is the cloud approaching, spreading, lowering, or growing? Is upper cloud moving differently from low cloud? Track the same feature for 10-30 minutes; note motion at more than one level
DEFINE	para 200	4. Water Are droplets only visible as cloud, are there virga streaks, or is precipitation reaching the surface? Look for fall streaks, surface wetting, rain/snow observations, radar
DEFINE	Environment Is a front, trough, mountain wave, sea breeze, or	Environment Is a front, trough, mountain wave, sea breeze, or thunderstorm outflow nearby? Surface pressure, temperature, dew point, wind shift, radar and satellite
DEFINE	para 203	6. Trend Is the deck thinning, thickening, lowering, or becoming more cellular? Repeated observations matter more than a single snapshot
RECORD	Fast recognition cues	Fast recognition cues
ALLOW	para 205	- High wisps spreading into a veil with a halo: possible cirrus-to-cirrostratus progression; a frontal shield may be approaching.
RECORD	para 206	- A grey sheet that gradually hides the Sun and deepens into steady precipitation: Altostratus progressing toward Nimbostratus is one possible pattern.
WHEN	para 207	- Growing towers with sharp cauliflower edges: strong vertical development; if they glaciate and spread an anvil, Cumulonimbus is likely.
ALLOW	para 208	- Smooth lens clouds near mountains: likely standing-wave clouds; they can indicate strong winds aloft, but the cloud may remain stationary relative to terrain.
RECORD	para 209	- Low, widespread grey cloud with poor visibility: Stratus or fog; check surface temperature, dew point, wind, and terrain.
RECORD	para 210	- A ragged low cloud under a storm base: could be pannus or scud; it should not automatically be interpreted as a funnel or tornado.
WHEN	para 211	Do not overclaim: clouds can suggest processes, but a cloud photograph rarely proves a front location, storm severity, exact altitude, or precipitation type. Use a current surface map, radar, satellite loop, and sounding when those distinctions matter.
RECORD	CLOUD TYPES, FRONTS, AND ALTITUDES | Field reference Page 16	CLOUD TYPES, FRONTS, AND ALTITUDES | Field reference Page 16
RECORD	para 213	15. Scientific and technical references
RECORD	para 214	The World Meteorological Organization atlas is the definitive source for formal cloud names, species, varieties, supplementary features, and accessory clouds. NOAA and Met Office educational pages are useful for interpreting frontal structure and typical heights. The research papers below provide deeper treatment of cyclone cloud structure and precipitation.
RECORD	Classification and altitude	Classification and altitude
RECORD	para 216	1. World Meteorological Organization (WMO), International Cloud Atlas - official cloud definitions, genera, species, varieties, supplementary features, accessory clouds, and classification summary. https://cloudatlas.wmo.int/en/home.html
RECORD	para 217	2. WMO, Definitions of clouds - ten recognized genera and genus definitions. https://cloudatlas.wmo.int/en/definitions-of-clouds.html
RECORD	para 218	3. WMO, Approximate levels and heights - regional ranges for high, middle, and low cloud groups. https://cloudatlas.wmo.int/en/clouds-definitions.html
RECORD	para 219	4. WMO, Supplementary features and accessory clouds - formal definitions for incus, mamma, virga, asperitas, cavum, fluctus, arcus, murus, cauda, tuba, pileus, velum, pannus, and flumen. Official WMO page
RECORD	para 220	5. Met Office, High clouds - cirrus, cirrostratus, cirrocumulus, typical height and morphology. Met Office high clouds
RECORD	para 221	6. Met Office, Mid-level clouds - altocumulus, altostratus, nimbostratus. Met Office mid-level clouds
RECORD	para 222	7. Met Office, Low-level clouds - stratus, stratocumulus, cumulus and cumulonimbus. Met Office low-level clouds
RECORD	para 223	8. NOAA, How to read surface weather maps: fronts - practical characteristics of warm and cold fronts and their passage. NOAA JetStream: weather maps
RECORD	para 224	9. NOAA, Air masses - frontal slopes and why warm fronts commonly support broad layered clouds while cold fronts can produce sharper lifting. NOAA JetStream: air masses
RECORD	Peer-reviewed research	Peer-reviewed research
RECORD	para 226	10. Hobbs, P. V. (1978). “Organization and structure of clouds and precipitation on the mesoscale and microscale in cyclonic storms.” Reviews of Geophysics, 16(4), 741-755. DOI: 10.1029/RG016i004p00741. A classic review of frontal, warm-sector, cold-frontal, and post-frontal precipitation bands.
RECORD	para 227	11. Browning, K. A. (1990). “Organization of clouds and precipitation in extratropical cyclones.” In Extratropical Cyclones: The Erik Palmén Memorial Volume, pp. 129-153. DOI: 10.1007/978-1-944970-33-8_8. A key synthesis of cloud and precipitation organization in mid-latitude cyclones.
RECORD	para 228	12. Catto, J. L., Jakob, C., Berry, G., and Nicholls, N. (2012). “Relating global precipitation to atmospheric fronts.” Geophysical Research Letters, 39, L10805. DOI: 10.1029/2012GL051736. Open-access research connecting precipitation patterns to warm, cold, and quasi-stationary fronts.
RECORD	para 229	13. Houze, R. A. Jr. (2014). Cloud Dynamics, 2nd ed. Academic Press. A major textbook on cloud-scale dynamics, cloud formation, and precipitation processes. Publisher page.
RECORD	para 230	14. Markowski, P. and Richardson, Y. (2010). Mesoscale Meteorology in Midlatitudes. Wiley-Blackwell. Background on fronts, cyclones, instability, and mesoscale convective organization. DOI / publisher record.
ALLOW	para 231	Use links as starting points. Some references are open access; others may require library access. Scientific papers describe distributions and mechanisms, while the official WMO atlas controls formal cloud terminology.
RECORD	CLOUD TYPES, FRONTS, AND ALTITUDES | Field reference Page 17	CLOUD TYPES, FRONTS, AND ALTITUDES | Field reference Page 17
RECORD	para 233	16. Compact glossary and final reference card
RECORD	Advection Horizontal transport of a property such as heat or moisture by wind	Advection Horizontal transport of a property such as heat or moisture by wind
RECORD	para 236	Anvil (incus) Flattened, spreading upper part of a strong cumulonimbus, often near a stable tropopause
RECORD	Cloud base / top Lowest / highest visible boundary of a cloud	Cloud base / top Lowest / highest visible boundary of a cloud
RECORD	para 238	Condensation level Altitude where a rising air parcel first becomes saturated under the parcel's thermodynamic path
RECORD	Convection Vertical overturning driven by buoyancy and/or destabilizing processe	Convection Vertical overturning driven by buoyancy and/or destabilizing processes
RECORD	para 240	Frontal slope The three-dimensional inclined zone separating air masses; usually gentler for warm fronts than cold fronts
RECORD	para 241	Inversion A layer where temperature increases with height; often suppresses vertical mixing
RECORD	Mixed-phase cloud Cloud containing both supercooled liquid droplets and ice crys	Mixed-phase cloud Cloud containing both supercooled liquid droplets and ice crystals
RECORD	Nimbostratus Deep widespread precipitating cloud, typically layered rather than	Nimbostratus Deep widespread precipitating cloud, typically layered rather than strongly cellular
RECORD	para 244	Tropopause Boundary region between the troposphere and stratosphere; often limits or redirects cloud-top growth
RECORD	Virga Precipitation falling from cloud but evaporating or sublimating before it	Virga Precipitation falling from cloud but evaporating or sublimating before it reaches the surface
RECORD	One-minute summary	One-minute summary
RECORD	para 248	Warm fronts often feature a broad, gently ascending cloud shield and a possible progression from cirrus through cirrostratus and altostratus to nimbostratus. Cold fronts often lift air more abruptly, allowing cumulus towers and cumulonimbus where moisture and instability are available. Real cloud systems are messier: fronts can be dry, weak, stationary, occluded, or mixed with waves and convection.
RECORD	para 249	Best field habit: identify the genus, estimate the cloud base and top separately, record how the cloud is changing, then relate it to wind, temperature, dew point, pressure trend, terrain, and current radar/satellite data. Always distinguish what you can see from what you infer.
RECORD	Clouds and Atmospheric Formation - science handout 1	Clouds and Atmospheric Formation - science handout 1
RECORD	How they form, grow, transform, and produce precipitation in Earth's atmosphere	How they form, grow, transform, and produce precipitation in Earth's atmosphere
DEFINE	para 5	A visible cloud is a population of tiny droplets and/or ice crystals suspended in air.
WHEN	para 6	Why the sky is full of invisible water but only sometimes has clouds: water vapor is transparent. A cloud becomes visible when enough water exists in the condensed phase - liquid drops, ice crystals, or both - to scatter light back toward an observer.
RECORD	At a glance	At a glance
RECORD	Process What changes Typical result	Process What changes Typical result
RECORD	Evaporation Liquid water -> water vapor Moist air	Evaporation Liquid water -> water vapor Moist air
RECORD	para 10	Rising / expansion Pressure falls; parcel cools Relative humidity rises
RECORD	Condensation Vapor -> liquid on CCN Cloud droplets	Condensation Vapor -> liquid on CCN Cloud droplets
RECORD	Deposition / freezing Vapor or liquid -> ice Ice crystals	Deposition / freezing Vapor or liquid -> ice Ice crystals
RECORD	Growth Droplets collide or ice grows/collects Precipitation-sized particles	Growth Droplets collide or ice grows/collects Precipitation-sized particles
RECORD	Dissipation Evaporation, mixing, sinking, precipitationCloud weakens or disappea	Dissipation Evaporation, mixing, sinking, precipitationCloud weakens or disappears
RECORD	para 15	This handout is written for a scientifically literate general reader. It explains the physics first, then adds the microphysics and climate connections that make real clouds much more complicated than the simple "warm air rises and makes clouds" picture.
RECORD	Clouds and Atmospheric Formation - science handout 2	Clouds and Atmospheric Formation - science handout 2
RECORD	para 17	1. Where the water comes from
RECORD	para 18	Earth's atmosphere is continuously supplied with water vapor. Evaporation from oceans, lakes, soil, and other surfaces is the dominant surface-to-atmosphere pathway. Plants add vapor through transpiration, and ice and snow can also contribute through sublimation. The hydrologic cycle continually moves water between the surface, atmosphere, clouds, and precipitation. [4]
DEFINE	Water vapor is a gas, not a visible cloud	Water vapor is a gas, not a visible cloud
WHEN	para 20	Individual water molecules are far too small to scatter enough visible light for us to see them. You can have very humid air that looks perfectly clear. The visible transition begins when water molecules assemble into a condensed phase - a population of tiny liquid droplets or ice crystals. [1,2]
RECORD	Saturation and relative humidity	Saturation and relative humidity
RECORD	para 22	The atmosphere has a temperature-dependent equilibrium vapor pressure. In simplified terms, warm air can support more water vapor before reaching saturation than cold air can. Relative humidity (RH) is commonly expressed as the actual water-vapor partial pressure divided by the saturation vapor pressure at the same temperature, multiplied by 100%. RH near 100% means the air is close to saturation.
RECORD	para 23	Important nuance: "the air can only hold so much water" is a useful teaching approximation, but it is not a literal container limit. Saturation describes thermodynamic equilibrium between water vapor and a condensed phase at a given temperature and pressure. Cloud microphysics also depends on particle size, composition, curvature, surface tension, and the dynamics of the air.
RECORD	How air reaches saturation	How air reaches saturation
RECORD	Mechanism Physical picture Examples	Mechanism Physical picture Examples
RECORD	para 26	Adiabatic lifting Air rises, pressure decreases, and the parcel expands/coolsThermals, fronts, mountains
RECORD	Convergence Horizontal winds force air upward Low-pressure systems, boundaries	Convergence Horizontal winds force air upward Low-pressure systems, boundaries
RECORD	Frontal lifting Denser air undercuts or lifts warmer air Cold and warm fronts	Frontal lifting Denser air undercuts or lifts warmer air Cold and warm fronts
RECORD	Radiative / surface coolingAir near a surface loses heat until it saturates Radi	Radiative / surface coolingAir near a surface loses heat until it saturates Radiation fog, stratus
RECORD	Evaporation into cool air Moisture enters air faster than it can be mixed awaySe	Evaporation into cool air Moisture enters air faster than it can be mixed awaySea smoke, steam fog
RECORD	Clouds and Atmospheric Formation - science handout 3	Clouds and Atmospheric Formation - science handout 3
RECORD	para 32	2. Why rising air cools and makes clouds
WHEN	para 33	A very common route to cloud formation begins with upward motion. As an air parcel rises into lower pressure, it expands. Expansion costs internal energy, so the parcel cools even if it exchanges little heat with its surroundings. This is the adiabatic process. [1,5]
RECORD	The dry and moist adiabatic lapse rates	The dry and moist adiabatic lapse rates
RECORD	para 35	For unsaturated air, the dry adiabatic lapse rate is about 9.8 C per kilometer. Once condensation begins, latent heat released by condensation offsets part of the cooling, so the saturated (moist) adiabatic lapse rate is smaller and varies with temperature and pressure, commonly on the order of about 4-7 C per kilometer in the lower atmosphere.
RECORD	para 40	-10 C Dry adiabatic: ~9.8 C/km Moist adiabatic: ~4-7 C/km
RECORD	Atmospheric stability controls cloud shape	Atmospheric stability controls cloud shape
RECORD	para 44	Key idea: cloud shape is a visible record of atmospheric motion. Flat layers often indicate widespread, gentle ascent or stable stratification. Towering clouds indicate strong localized updrafts and continued vertical development.
RECORD	Clouds and Atmospheric Formation - science handout 4	Clouds and Atmospheric Formation - science handout 4
RECORD	para 46	3. Why tiny particles matter: cloud condensation nuclei
RECORD	para 47	Cloud droplets generally do not appear efficiently by simply clustering pure water molecules in the open atmosphere. Most cloud droplets form on pre-existing aerosol particles called cloud condensation nuclei (CCN). Sources include sea salt, mineral dust, sulfate and other pollution-derived particles, smoke, organic material, and biological particles. [1,6]
RECORD	Kohler theory in plain language	Kohler theory in plain language
RECORD	Droplet diameter / particle size ->Equilibrium supersaturation ->	Droplet diameter / particle size ->Equilibrium supersaturation -> critical supersaturation
RECORD	para 51	Small size: curvature makes growth difficult
RECORD	para 52	Larger activated droplet: ordinary growth becomes favorable
RECORD	para 53	This is a simplified qualitative sketch, not a numerical Kohler curve. Real activation depends on particle size, soluble mass, surface tension, chemical composition, and temperature. Modern treatments extend classical Kohler theory to mixtures, surfactants, non-ideal solutions, and temperature-dependent properties. [6,7]
DEFINE	Supersaturation is usually small - but crucial	Supersaturation is usually small - but crucial
RECORD	para 55	Inside growing clouds, supersaturation with respect to liquid water is often only a fraction of one percent. That tiny excess is nevertheless enough to activate an appropriate subset of aerosol particles. Updraft speed, temperature, aerosol properties, and turbulent mixing all influence how much supersaturation develops and how many particles activate. [6-8]
RECORD	Clouds and Atmospheric Formation - science handout 5	Clouds and Atmospheric Formation - science handout 5
RECORD	para 57	4. The major cloud families
DEFINE	para 58	The familiar cloud names encode appearance and altitude. The World Meteorological Organization uses a more detailed system of genera, species, and varieties; the broad families below are a practical introduction. [9]
RECORD	Family / examples Typical appearance What usually shapes it	Family / examples Typical appearance What usually shapes it
RECORD	para 60	Cirrus / cirro- High, thin, wispy; mostly ice Strong upper-level winds, ice growth and fall streaks
RECORD	Cumulus Puffy heaps with vertical structure Thermals, buoyancy, localized updraf	Cumulus Puffy heaps with vertical structure Thermals, buoyancy, localized updrafts
RECORD	Stratus Broad, gray or white layers Stable air, widespread weak ascent, cooling	Stratus Broad, gray or white layers Stable air, widespread weak ascent, cooling near surface
RECORD	para 63	Nimbostratus Thick layered cloud with widespread precipitationDeep, persistent ascent in large weather systems
RECORD	para 64	Cumulonimbus Towering cloud with anvil; intense precipitation possibleStrong instability, moisture, sustained deep updrafts
RECORD	para 65	Altostratus / altocumulus Middle-level sheets or patches Mid-level ascent, wave motion, fronts and instability
DEFINE	Altitude is not the whole story	Altitude is not the whole story
WHEN	para 67	Clouds are three-dimensional and can span large vertical ranges. A cloud genus does not mean every part of the cloud sits at one fixed altitude. In mountains, for example, a cloud can be classified as low-level relative to the local terrain even if its elevation above sea level is considerable.
RECORD	A useful visual rule	A useful visual rule
RECORD	para 69	Cirro- usually signals high cloud; alto- signals middle-level cloud; strato- suggests a layer; cumulo- suggests vertical heaps; and nimbo- is associated with precipitation. These name pieces are clues, not substitutes for the formal classification system.
RECORD	Clouds and Atmospheric Formation - science handout 6	Clouds and Atmospheric Formation - science handout 6
DEFINE	para 71	5. What is actually inside a cloud?
RECORD	para 72	A cloud is not a giant blob of liquid water. It is a dilute suspension of enormous numbers of tiny hydrometeors - primarily cloud droplets and/or ice crystals - moving with the air. Individual cloud droplets are typically on the order of several to a few tens of micrometers in diameter, while precipitation particles are much larger.
RECORD	para 73	Droplet growth: condensation
RECORD	para 74	Freshly activated droplets grow by diffusion of water vapor toward their surfaces. Growth is fastest early on and slows as droplets become larger because the available vapor field adjusts around each particle. Turbulence and mixing continuously modify the local temperature and humidity histories that individual droplets experience. [5]
RECORD	para 75	Droplet growth: collision and coalescence
RECORD	para 76	In warm clouds, droplets do not all have the same size or fall speed. Larger droplets fall faster, overtake smaller ones, collide, and sometimes merge. This collision-coalescence process can create droplets large enough to fall as drizzle or rain. Turbulence can enhance collision rates under some conditions. [5,10]
RECORD	Water vapor Cloud droplet Rain drop	Water vapor Cloud droplet Rain drop
RECORD	Ice nucleus /■freezing seed Ice crystal Snow / hail /■ice pellets	Ice nucleus /■freezing seed Ice crystal Snow / hail /■ice pellets
RECORD	para 81	condensation collection / coalescence
RECORD	para 82	nucleation / freezing aggregation / riming
RECORD	para 83	Cloud microphysics is a competition among processes that create, grow, transform, transport, and remove hydrometeors. Which process dominates depends strongly on temperature, humidity, vertical motion, aerosol population, cloud depth, and time.
RECORD	Clouds and Atmospheric Formation - science handout 7	Clouds and Atmospheric Formation - science handout 7
RECORD	para 85	6. From cloud droplets to precipitation
DEFINE	para 86	Cloud droplets are small enough that their terminal fall speeds are modest and upward air motions can keep them suspended. Precipitation requires a pathway that makes some particles substantially larger.
RECORD	Warm-rain pathway	Warm-rain pathway
WHEN	para 88	In liquid-only clouds, a broad droplet-size distribution allows faster-falling droplets to collide with slower droplets. If the collision leads to coalescence, the drop grows. Repetition can move water from hundreds or thousands of cloud droplets into a much smaller number of drizzle and raindrops. This is the classic collision-coalescence mechanism. [5,10]
RECORD	Cold and mixed-phase pathway	Cold and mixed-phase pathway
ALLOW	para 90	In clouds containing both supercooled liquid water and ice, ice crystals can grow rapidly through vapor deposition. They may then aggregate with other ice crystals or collect supercooled droplets by riming. The resulting particles can become snowflakes, graupel, or hail. If they fall into warmer layers, they may melt into rain.
RECORD	Why precipitation can shut the cloud down	Why precipitation can shut the cloud down
RECORD	para 92	Precipitation exports water from the cloud. Evaporation below or within the cloud can cool air and change buoyancy. Falling hydrometeors can also collide with other particles and alter the distribution of liquid and ice. Thus precipitation is not merely an output of a cloud; it can feed back on cloud dynamics and lifetime. [3,10]
DEFINE	para 93	Key distinction: condensation makes cloud water; microphysical growth makes precipitation. Those are related but not identical steps.
RECORD	Cloud droplets / ice	Cloud droplets / ice
RECORD	para 95	Warm cloud:■coalescence Ice / mixed phase:■deposition + riming
RECORD	Precipitation-sized■hydrometeor	Precipitation-sized■hydrometeor
RECORD	para 97	liquid pathway ice pathway
DEFINE	para 98	The diagram is simplified. Real storms can contain multiple microphysical pathways simultaneously, including melting layers, aggregation, secondary ice production, hail growth, and evaporation of falling precipitation.
RECORD	Clouds and Atmospheric Formation - science handout 8	Clouds and Atmospheric Formation - science handout 8
RECORD	para 100	7. Why clouds appear where they do
REQUIRE	para 101	Clouds are strongly organized by the geometry of atmospheric motion. Moisture alone does not place a cloud over a particular location; the atmosphere must also provide a way to lift, cool, or otherwise saturate air.
RECORD	Setting Lifting / cooling mechanism Common cloud expression	Setting Lifting / cooling mechanism Common cloud expression
DEFINE	para 103	Mountain slopes Air is forced upward over terrain (orographically induced ascent)Cap clouds, lenticular clouds, upslope precipitation
RECORD	Cold fronts Cold dense air lifts warmer moist air Lines of cumulus / cumulonimbu	Cold fronts Cold dense air lifts warmer moist air Lines of cumulus / cumulonimbus
RECORD	para 105	Warm fronts Warm air glides over cooler air along a shallow slope Layered stratiform cloud, broad precipitation areas
RECORD	para 106	Low-pressure centers Convergence and large-scale ascent Widespread multilayer cloud and precipitation
RECORD	Sunny land surfaces Surface heating drives thermals Fair-weather cumulus, growin	Sunny land surfaces Surface heating drives thermals Fair-weather cumulus, growing cumulus
RECORD	Nighttime cooling Surface cools the lowest air to saturation Radiation fog and l	Nighttime cooling Surface cools the lowest air to saturation Radiation fog and low stratus
RECORD	para 109	Marine boundary layers Moisture supply, cooling from the surface/air-sea exchange, and inversion structureStratus and stratocumulus
RECORD	Cloud streets and organized convection	Cloud streets and organized convection
DEFINE	para 112	A cloud is a coupled fluid + thermodynamics + microphysics problem. The visible shape is the end product of processes acting across scales from molecular collisions to kilometers of organized weather.
RECORD	Clouds and Atmospheric Formation - science handout 9	Clouds and Atmospheric Formation - science handout 9
RECORD	para 114	8. Why clouds change shape so quickly
RECORD	para 115	Clouds can look solid from a distance, yet their individual droplets are constantly forming, evaporating, growing, colliding, freezing, and falling. Air is also being entrained from outside the cloud. These processes can change the cloud's appearance on timescales of minutes.
RECORD	Entrainment and mixing	Entrainment and mixing
ALLOW	para 117	Rising cloud air mixes with environmental air that may be warmer, colder, drier, or more humid. Dry-air entrainment tends to increase evaporation and can weaken cloud water. Strong mixing can also broaden the distribution of droplet growth histories. The balance between cloud ascent and entrainment is therefore central to cloud lifetime and precipitation initiation. [5,11]
RECORD	Cloud base can evaporate while the top grows	Cloud base can evaporate while the top grows
ALLOW	para 119	A growing cumulus may add condensate in its upper regions while droplets near the base evaporate in a drier environment. What the observer sees is the combined result of these local gain and loss processes. This helps explain why clouds can look as though they are boiling, billowing, or dissolving even when the larger-scale weather changes only gradually.
DEFINE	Fog is a cloud at the surface	Fog is a cloud at the surface
WHEN	para 121	The physics does not stop at the ground. When the air immediately above the surface reaches saturation and enough condensed water becomes visible, the result is fog. Radiation fog, advection fog, upslope fog, and evaporation fog differ mainly in how the air is cooled or moistened enough to reach saturation. [2]
DEFINE	Cloud lifetime is a balance	Cloud lifetime is a balance
RECORD	para 123	A useful conceptual equation is:
RECORD	para 124	cloud water change = formation + growth + transport - evaporation - precipitation removal - mixing losses
RECORD	para 125	The terms are not independent: precipitation can alter temperature and humidity; latent heating can alter buoyancy; aerosols can modify droplet number and size; and radiation can heat or cool cloud layers. That coupling is why clouds remain an active area of atmospheric research. [3,8,12]
RECORD	Clouds and Atmospheric Formation - science handout 10	Clouds and Atmospheric Formation - science handout 10
RECORD	para 127	9. Why clouds matter for weather and climate
RECORD	Aerosol-cloud interactions	Aerosol-cloud interactions
REQUIRE	para 131	Why this is scientifically hard: cloud processes span many scales. Molecular-scale thermodynamics controls droplet activation; droplets interact through turbulence and gravity; clouds organize within weather systems; and those weather systems influence regional and global climate. Numerical models cannot directly resolve every scale, so many cloud processes must be represented through parameterizations.
RECORD	What satellites can and cannot tell us	What satellites can and cannot tell us
RECORD	para 133	Satellites can map cloud cover, top temperatures, optical thickness, phase, and other properties over large areas. But connecting those measurements to the underlying aerosol population, vertical motion, supersaturation, and individual microphysical pathways is difficult. Combining satellites, aircraft, surface observations, laboratory measurements, and models is therefore central to modern cloud research. [3,12]
RECORD	Clouds and Atmospheric Formation - science handout 11	Clouds and Atmospheric Formation - science handout 11
RECORD	para 135	10. Deeper scientific references
DEFINE	para 136	The links below are selected for deeper reading. They mix foundational explanations, review papers, and a recent peer-reviewed study. DOI links are the most stable route when available.
RECORD	para 137	1. NOAA JetStream - How Clouds Form. Official educational overview covering water, condensation nuclei, saturation, adiabatic cooling, entrainment, and cloud growth/dissipation. https://www.noaa.gov/jetstream/clouds/how-clouds-form
RECORD	para 138	2. NASA Science - How Do Clouds Form? Clear introduction to evaporation, saturation, condensation, deposition, cloud particles, fog, and why cloud science is difficult. https://science.nasa.gov/kids/earth/how-do-clouds-form/
RECORD	para 139	3. Burrows, S. M. et al. (2022), Reviews of Geophysics - Ice-Nucleating Particles That Impact Clouds and Climate: Observational and Modeling Research Needs. Open-access review of ice-nucleating particles, mixed-phase cloud processes, precipitation and climate impacts. DOI: 10.1029/2021RG000745. https://doi.org/10.1029/2021RG000745
RECORD	para 140	4. NASA Earth Observatory - The Water Cycle. Background on evaporation, transpiration, sublimation, cloud formation, and precipitation in the hydrologic cycle. https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/the-water-cycle/
RECORD	para 141	5. Grabowski et al. / Pinsky et al. (2013), Annual Review of Fluid Mechanics - Growth of Cloud Droplets in a Turbulent Environment. Review of condensational growth, collision-coalescence, turbulence, and the warm-rain bottleneck. DOI: 10.1146/annurev-fluid-011212-140750. https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-fluid-011212-140750
RECORD	para 142	6. Padró & Nenes (2007), Atmospheric Chemistry and Physics - Cloud droplet activation: solubility revisited. Paper on aerosol solubility, curvature effects, and cloud droplet activation within the framework of Kohler theory. DOI: 10.5194/acpd-7-2325-2007. https://doi.org/10.5194/acpd-7-2325-2007
RECORD	para 143	7. Ervens et al. (2021), Atmospheric Chemistry and Physics - A predictive thermodynamic framework of cloud droplet activation for chemically unresolved aerosol mixtures. Modern treatment of cloud droplet activation including surface tension, non-ideality, and bulk-surface partitioning. https://acp.copernicus.org/articles/21/16387/2021/
RECORD	para 144	8. Li et al. (2025), Atmospheric Research - A review of aerosol-cloud interactions: Mechanisms, climate effects, and observation methods. Recent review of liquid, ice, and mixed-phase aerosol-cloud interactions, precipitation effects, radiation, and observations. DOI: 10.1016/j.atmosres.2025.108267. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.atmosres.2025.108267
RECORD	para 145	9. World Meteorological Organization - International Cloud Atlas. Formal international terminology and classification of cloud genera, species, and varieties. https://cloudatlas.wmo.int/
RECORD	para 146	10. Rosenfeld et al. (2014), Reviews of Geophysics - Global observations of aerosol-cloud-precipitation-climate interactions. Large review connecting CCN/IN populations to cloud microstructure, albedo, precipitation and climate. DOI: 10.1002/2013RG000441. https://doi.org/10.1002/2013RG000441
RECORD	para 147	11. Korolev / Morrison and related mixed-phase cloud literature. Starting point for advanced study of supercooled liquid water, ice growth, WBF, riming, aggregation, and cloud glaciation. See the Burrows review above for a well-linked synthesis. https://doi.org/10.1029/2021RG000745
RECORD	para 148	12. Fan, J., Wang, Y., Rosenfeld, D., & Liu, X. (2016), Journal of the Atmospheric Sciences - Review of Aerosol-Cloud Interactions: Mechanisms, Significance, and Challenges. Detailed review of aerosol-cloud feedbacks, precipitation, radiative effects, and unresolved processes. DOI: 10.1175/JAS-D-16-0037.1. https://doi.org/10.1175/JAS-D-16-0037.1
