DEFINE	para 153	Prepared October 2026. Educational reference document; descriptions are simplified where noted so that the core physical picture remains readable without omitting important scientific caveats.
RECORD	EARTH BIOMES, SUB-BIOMES	EARTH BIOMES, SUB-BIOMES
RECORD	AND THE TERRAIN THAT SHAPES	AND THE TERRAIN THAT SHAPES
RECORD	A high-resolution conceptual atlas of climate, continentalness, relief, hydrolog	A high-resolution conceptual atlas of climate, continentalness, relief, hydrology, soils, and ecological patterning across Earth.
RECORD	para 5	Central idea: a biome is not painted onto a map by latitude alone. It emerges from long-term energy and water balance, modified by distance from the ocean, atmospheric circulation, mountain barriers, landform, substrate, fire, flooding, and ecological history.
RECORD	para 18	Use this guide as a practical, broad approximation of global biome and sub-biome diversity. It is intentionally more detailed than a school-level biome list, but it does not claim every local plant association is a separate global biome.
RECORD	WHAT THIS ATLAS COVERS	WHAT THIS ATLAS COVERS
RECORD	para 20	• Climate geography: tropical-to-polar gradients, oceanic versus continental climates, seasonality, monsoons, and moisture balance.
RECORD	para 21	• Terrain: mountain belts, windward and leeward slopes, elevation zones, plateaus, basins, valleys, floodplains, dunes, and coasts.
RECORD	para 22	• Ecological taxonomy: major terrestrial biomes plus a wide catalog of recognizable sub-biomes and special substrate- or water-controlled systems.
RECORD	para 23	• Modeling logic: a repeatable way to connect climate, elevation, slope, drainage, geology, and disturbance when classifying real landscapes or building a terrain system.
RECORD	para 24	Classification note: there is no single universally adopted global hierarchy of “biome -> sub-biome”. Different scientific products classify climate, potential vegetation, ecoregions, land cover, or ecological land units for different purposes. This document treats the hierarchy as a useful synthesis and flags where categories overlap. See references [1]-[5].
RECORD	EARTH BIOMES, SUB-BIOMES & TERRAIN FIELD REFERENCE / 2026	EARTH BIOMES, SUB-BIOMES & TERRAIN FIELD REFERENCE / 2026
DEFINE	para 26	Conceptual synthesis; local boundaries are gradual and classification-dependent. 2
RECORD	How to use the atlas	How to use the atlas
RECORD	para 28	Start with the physical controls, then use the biome catalog. The same visible vegetation can arise from different histories, and similar climates can support different vegetation where fire, soils, grazing, flooding, or geological history differ. A map label is therefore a summary of a coupled system, not a complete explanation.
