RECORD	para 44	1. The climate engine: heat, water, and seasonality
RECORD	The primary climate dimensions	The primary climate dimensions
RECORD	CONTROL WHAT TO TRACK ECOLOGICAL CONSEQUENCE	CONTROL WHAT TO TRACK ECOLOGICAL CONSEQUENCE
RECORD	para 48	Energy / temperature Mean temperature; coldest-month minimum; heat accumulation; frost-free season; freeze-thaw frequency. Tropical forest vs boreal forest; treeline position; frost-limited woody vegetation.
RECORD	Moisture supply Annual precipitation, rain/snow fraction, seasonality, storm	Moisture supply Annual precipitation, rain/snow fraction, seasonality, storm type, fog/dew inputs. Rainforest vs seasonal forest; snowbed; fog oasis; monsoon forest.
DEFINE	para 50	Atmospheric demand Potential evapotranspiration (PET), solar radiation, wind, vapor-pressure deficit. A warm place with moderate rain can still be water-limited if demand is high.
RECORD	Seasonality Wet/dry season timing, winter versus summer rain, length of	Seasonality Wet/dry season timing, winter versus summer rain, length of drought, snowpack persistence. Savanna vs evergreen forest; Mediterranean shrubland vs humid temperate forest.
RECORD	Extremes and	Extremes and disturbance Fire-return interval, flood magnitude, wind exposure, tropical cyclones, frost events. Fire-maintained grassland; floodplain forest; wind-pruned krummholz.
DEFINE	Why annual averages are insufficient	Why annual averages are insufficient
DEFINE	para 55	• Minimum winter temperature can determine whether broadleaf evergreen species survive, even if annual mean temperature is mild.
RECORD	para 56	• Rainfall seasonality can separate evergreen forest, seasonal forest, savanna, and thorn scrub at similar annual totals.
ALLOW	para 57	• Snowfall stored through winter may support spring growth; rain of the same amount at a different time has a different ecological effect.
RECORD	para 58	• Fire, grazing, and flood regimes can maintain a grassland or open woodland where climate alone might also allow more trees.
WHEN	para 59	For a defensible biome assignment, use at least: temperature seasonality, precipitation amount and seasonality, evaporative demand, elevation, and one measure of terrain-controlled moisture. Add soils and disturbance when the goal is finer than a broad climate-biome map.
DEFINE	para 61	Conceptual synthesis; local boundaries are gradual and classification-dependent. 4
RECORD	para 62	2. Continentalness: from maritime margins to inland climates
RECORD	OCEAN OCEANCONTINENTAL INTERIOR	OCEAN OCEANCONTINENTAL INTERIOR
RECORD	para 65	Maritime: smaller seasonal temperature range
RECORD	para 66	Continental: larger seasonal temperature range Conceptual pattern only; real climates also depend on latitude, circulation, currents and terrain.
RECORD	Typical tendencies (not universal laws)	Typical tendencies (not universal laws)
RECORD	SETTING COMMON PHYSICAL SIGNATURE POSSIBLE BIOME RESPONSE	SETTING COMMON PHYSICAL SIGNATURE POSSIBLE BIOME RESPONSE
ALLOW	para 69	Near ocean / maritime Smaller annual temperature range; humid air more frequently available; fog and cloud can be important; precipitation may be frequent but not necessarily heavy. Temperate rain forest, oceanic broadleaf forest, maritime heath, coastal shrubland, cloud forest on suitable slopes.
RECORD	Coastal dry-summer	Coastal dry-summer margins Ocean nearby but seasonal circulation suppresses summer rain; cold currents and upwelling can stabilize air and create fog.
RECORD	Mediterranean shrubland, coastal desert, fog desert, succulent	Mediterranean shrubland, coastal desert, fog desert, succulent shrubland.
RECORD	para 72	Mid-continent Greater seasonal temperature range; moisture can decline downwind of oceans and after mountain barriers; winter cold and summer heat become more pronounced. Temperate grassland, steppe, mixed forest, continental taiga, semi-arid shrub steppe.
RECORD	Deep interior / rain-shadow	Deep interior / rain-shadow basin Low precipitation can combine with high evaporative demand, strong temperature extremes, and limited surface water. Cold desert, salt basin, dry steppe, sagebrush-like shrubland, semi-desert.
RECORD	Mechanisms that matter	Mechanisms that matter
RECORD	para 75	• Seasonal thermal range: inland surfaces commonly experience hotter summers and colder winters than nearby maritime settings at comparable latitude and elevation.
ALLOW	para 76	• Moisture trajectory: air masses may lose moisture as they travel over land, especially when uplift and precipitation occur along intervening ranges. But storm tracks and monsoons can bring moisture far inland.
RECORD	para 77	• Coastal currents: cool eastern-boundary currents often favor stable marine layers and fog along west coasts in subtropical dry belts; warm currents can add moisture and alter storm intensity.
RECORD	para 78	• Snow and frozen ground: continental interiors can have long snow seasons or strong seasonal frost, changing growing seasons, soil drainage, and spring runoff.
RECORD	para 79	Do not infer biome from continentalness alone. A humid continental forest, a prairie, and a cold desert can all occur far from oceans because temperature, rainfall, soil storage, fire, and topography interact.
WHEN	para 80	Useful quantitative approach: calculate a continentality index from annual temperature range and latitude only if you need a comparative metric; for ecological mapping, direct climatologies (monthly temperatures, precipitation, PET, frost days, snow cover) usually preserve more interpretable information.
DEFINE	para 82	Conceptual synthesis; local boundaries are gradual and classification-dependent. 5
RECORD	para 83	3. Mountains: uplift, rain shadows, and ecological compression
RECORD	WINDWARD / MOIST LEEWARD / DRIER	WINDWARD / MOIST LEEWARD / DRIER
RECORD	para 86	Air rises, expands and cools; cloud and precipitation often increase. Descending air warms; relative humidity often falls.
RECORD	Why relief changes biome boundaries	Why relief changes biome boundaries
RECORD	para 88	• Elevation lowers temperature: temperature commonly decreases with altitude, but the local lapse rate varies with humidity, season, inversions, and time of day. Cold-air pooling can make valley floors colder than slopes at night.
ALLOW	para 89	• Cloud belts move upslope: windward slopes may intercept cloud water and fog; cloud forests can occur where frequent cloud immersion supplements rainfall.
RECORD	para 90	• Snowpack redistributes water: snow accumulates in shaded hollows and high basins, then melts later than lowland rain. Snowbeds and meltwater corridors can support plants unlike the surrounding slopes.
DEFINE	para 91	• Aspect changes radiation: equator-facing slopes tend to receive more solar energy than pole-facing slopes in mid-latitudes. They are often warmer and drier; the direction is reversed between hemispheres.
RECORD	para 92	• Terrain redistributes soil and water: ridges shed water and often have thin, exposed soils; concave hollows collect colluvium and moisture; floodplains receive sediment and groundwater.
DEFINE	Mountain effect is more than altitude	Mountain effect is more than altitude
ALLOW	para 94	The same elevation may hold closed forest on a humid windward slope, open woodland on a sunny aspect, alpine meadow on a windy ridge, peatland in a poorly drained basin, and bare scree on a steep unstable face. Bedrock affects soil depth and chemistry; glacial history affects landforms and lakes; avalanche tracks and recurring snow slides create disturbance corridors. [4, 5, 9]
RECORD	para 95	A mountain should usually be classified as a stack of ecological zones and terrain facets, not as one “mountain biome”. The FAO’s broad mountain-system classes are useful at global scale, but each range contains several climates and vegetation types.
DEFINE	para 97	Conceptual synthesis; local boundaries are gradual and classification-dependent. 6
RECORD	para 98	4. Landform, soil, drainage, and local mosaics
RECORD	SUBSTRATE PHYSICAL FILTER COMMON ECOLOGICAL RESPONSE	SUBSTRATE PHYSICAL FILTER COMMON ECOLOGICAL RESPONSE
RECORD	para 102	Ridge / summit Wind, radiation, shallow soils, rapid runoff, frost exposure. Dwarf scrub, fellfield, grassland, open woodland, alpine plants.
RECORD	para 103	Steep slope High drainage and erosion; soil depth depends on rock, stability, and colluvium. Dry scrub on hot slopes; forest on sheltered moist slopes; talus vegetation.
RECORD	Concave hollow Convergent runoff, deeper colluvium, sometimes cold-air	Concave hollow Convergent runoff, deeper colluvium, sometimes cold-air pooling. Mesic forest, fern-rich understory, snowbed, seep or small wetland.
RECORD	para 105	Footslope / alluvial fan Deposited sediment; infiltration changes with grain size; episodic debris flows. Woodland, grassland, desert scrub, riparian bands along channels.
RECORD	para 106	Floodplain / terrace Flood frequency, groundwater access, sediment renewal. Riparian forest, wet meadow, oxbow wetland, seasonally flooded grassland.
RECORD	Closed basin Poor drainage, evaporation concentration, groundwater	Closed basin Poor drainage, evaporation concentration, groundwater discharge. Salt flats, saline meadows, alkali shrubland, playa wetlands.
RECORD	para 108	Karst / limestone Rapid infiltration into fractures; alkaline substrate and thin soils in places. Calcicolous grassland, dry woodland, cave-associated habitats, spring-fed wetlands.
RECORD	para 109	Ultramafic / serpentine Often low calcium-to-magnesium ratio and elevated metals; unusual nutrient balance. Distinct edaphic shrubland/grassland with specialist plants.
RECORD	Volcanic substrate Rugged lava, porous ash, nutrient release as substrate	Volcanic substrate Rugged lava, porous ash, nutrient release as substrate weathers; uneven soil age. Lava-field pioneer communities, ash-soil forest, geothermal or volcanic wetland patches.
DEFINE	Soils are not merely a passive layer	Soils are not merely a passive layer
RECORD	para 112	Soil texture controls infiltration and water-holding capacity; depth determines rooting volume; organic matter changes aggregation and storage; salinity imposes osmotic stress; pH and mineralogy affect nutrient availability. Soil age, parent material, climate, vegetation, and topography interact through time. [10, 11] Two nearby sites can therefore support different communities despite sharing weather.
RECORD	Landscape water paths	Landscape water paths
RECORD	para 114	• Runoff-dominated: steep slopes, crusted surfaces, shallow soils, or intense storms can route water quickly into gullies and channels.
RECORD	para 115	• Infiltration-dominated: permeable soils or fractured bedrock can move water downward, sometimes sustaining springs and groundwater-dependent vegetation.
RECORD	para 116	• Storage-dominated: deep soils, peat, snowpacks, and alluvial aquifers can buffer dry periods.
RECORD	para 117	• Evapoconcentration: closed basins in arid climates accumulate salts where evaporation exceeds flushing and groundwater discharges near the surface.
DEFINE	para 119	Conceptual synthesis; local boundaries are gradual and classification-dependent. 7
RECORD	para 120	5. Global biome overview: useful levels of classification
RECORD	BROAD SYSTEM CLIMATE / CONTROL EXAMPLES OF SUB-BIOMES	BROAD SYSTEM CLIMATE / CONTROL EXAMPLES OF SUB-BIOMES
RECORD	para 123	Tropical moist forest Warm all year; very high moisture; weak thermal seasonality. Lowland rain forest, cloud forest, swamp forest, peat-swamp forest, flooded forest.
RECORD	para 124	Tropical seasonal forest Warm; dry season or monsoon seasonality. Semi-evergreen forest, moist deciduous forest, dry deciduous forest, monsoon forest.
RECORD	para 125	Savanna / open woodland Warm; seasonal moisture and disturbance often limit tree cover. Wet/dry savanna, thorn savanna, wooded grassland, gallery-forest mosaic.
RECORD	para 126	Desert / xeric shrubland Chronic water deficit; either hot or cold. Hyper-arid desert, coastal fog desert, gravel desert, dune desert, cold desert, salt basin.
RECORD	Mediterranean shrubland /	Mediterranean shrubland / woodland Mild wet season and hot dry season; fire often important. Chaparral, maquis, garrigue, matorral, fynbos, kwongan, open sclerophyll woodland.
RECORD	para 128	Temperate forest Seasonal temperatures; moisture supports tree canopy. Deciduous broadleaf, mixed forest, evergreen conifer, temperate rain forest, coastal forest.
RECORD	Temperate grassland /	Temperate grassland / steppe Semi-arid to subhumid; grasses dominate; fire and herbivory matter. Tallgrass, mixed-grass, shortgrass prairie, Eurasian steppe, pampas, veld, cold steppe.
RECORD	para 130	Boreal forest Long cold winters; short growing season; conifers often dominant. Closed taiga, open lichen woodland, peatland-forest mosaic, continental larch forest.
RECORD	para 131	Tundra / polar desert Low heat availability and short growing season; permafrost common in many settings. Arctic tundra, alpine tundra, wet sedge tundra, dry heath tundra, polar desert.
RECORD	Mountain systems Elevation, exposure, and relief compress climate	Mountain systems Elevation, exposure, and relief compress climate zones. Montane forest, subalpine woodland, alpine meadow/fellfield, nival rock and ice.
RECORD	Azonal / special systems Local hydrology, salinity, substrate, or disturbance	Azonal / special systems Local hydrology, salinity, substrate, or disturbance overrides zonal climate. Bogs, fens, marshes, mangroves, salt marshes, riparian corridors, dunes, serpentine barrens.
RECORD	para 134	“Sub-biome” is used here for a recognizable subdivision based on moisture seasonality, vegetation structure, substrate, hydrology, or elevation. In formal research, the same unit might be called an ecoregion, formation, ecosystem type, vegetation zone, or ecological land unit.
DEFINE	para 136	Conceptual synthesis; local boundaries are gradual and classification-dependent. 8
RECORD	para 137	6. Tropical and subtropical forest systems
RECORD	para 138	Tropical systems are not all rainforest. The length and reliability of the dry season, flooding regime, elevation, and substrate produce strong structural differences. Warm temperatures allow year-round growth where water and nutrients permit, but seasonal drought can favor deciduousness, deep roots, fire-resistant traits, and open canopies.
RECORD	SUB-BIOME SIGNATURE TYPICAL LANDSCAPE / REGIONS	SUB-BIOME SIGNATURE TYPICAL LANDSCAPE / REGIONS
RECORD	para 140	Lowland tropical rain forest No long severe dry season; high moisture; multi-layer evergreen canopy, lianas and epiphytes. Amazon, Congo Basin, Borneo, New Guinea, Central American wet lowlands.
RECORD	para 141	Tropical montane / cloud forest Cooler with elevation; frequent cloud immersion; steep, wet slopes. Andes, Central American ranges, East African mountains, New Guinea highlands.
RECORD	para 142	Tropical swamp / peat forest Waterlogged soils; flood or groundwater control; organic matter can accumulate. Amazonian flooded forest, Congo swamps, Southeast Asian peat swamp.
RECORD	Seasonally flooded / whitewater	Seasonally flooded / whitewater forest Annual flood pulse; sediment and nutrient inputs differ by river water source. Amazon várzea and igapó types; riverine forests in tropical basins.
RECORD	para 144	Tropical moist deciduous forest Pronounced dry season; many trees shed leaves seasonally. South and Southeast Asia, parts of tropical Africa and the Americas.
RECORD	Monsoon forest Strong wet summer and dry winter, often associated with	Monsoon forest Strong wet summer and dry winter, often associated with monsoon circulation. South Asia, mainland Southeast Asia, northern Australia pockets.
RECORD	para 146	Tropical dry forest Long dry season; open to closed canopy, drought-deciduous trees, thorny understory. Seasonally dry Central America, Caribbean, dry tropical South America, India, Madagascar.
RECORD	para 147	Thorn forest / tropical scrub More severe aridity; spinescence, small leaves, succulents and open woody cover. Caatinga, parts of the Deccan, African drylands, dry Caribbean islands.
RECORD	para 148	Savanna-forest transition Tree cover varies with rainfall, fire, soils and herbivory; grass fuels can maintain openness. Cerrado-forest edges, African miombo/savanna mosaics, northern Australian savannas.
RECORD	para 149	Subtropical humid forest Hot summers, mild winters, abundant rainfall; evergreen and deciduous species mix. Southeastern China, southeastern United States, southern Japan, parts of eastern South America.
RECORD	Subtropical evergreen broadleaf	Subtropical evergreen broadleaf forest Mild humid climate; evergreen broadleaf canopy, often with evergreen oak/laurel-type species. East Asia and other humid subtropical settings.
RECORD	Terrain modifiers in the tropics	Terrain modifiers in the tropics
RECORD	para 152	• Windward slopes can carry cloud forest above lowland rainforest, while adjacent rain shadows support montane dry forest or scrub.
RECORD	para 153	• Flood basins create swamp and seasonally flooded forest inside otherwise terra-firme rainforest regions.
RECORD	para 154	• Nutrient-poor ancient soils can support distinctive heath forest or white-sand vegetation, while young alluvium can support more productive forest.
RECORD	para 155	• In savanna mosaics, shallow soils, rock pavements, fire frequency, grazing and waterlogging can keep tree cover low despite rainfall that could support forest.
DEFINE	para 156	Tropical mountain systems deserve their own elevation and exposure layers; they are not simply a cooler copy of lowland tropical forest. [4, 5]
DEFINE	para 158	Conceptual synthesis; local boundaries are gradual and classification-dependent. 9
RECORD	para 159	7. Drylands: deserts, semi-deserts, and xeric shrublands
RECORD	para 160	Drylands occupy climates where precipitation is low or unreliable relative to evaporative demand. Their appearance depends heavily on substrate and water-routing: sandy dune fields, rocky pavements, salt basins, ephemeral washes, and mountain-front fans are very different habitats within the same arid region. In arid terrain, the soil-geomorphic template can strongly predict vegetation patterns. [13]
RECORD	SUB-BIOME PHYSICAL SIGNATURE TERRAIN EXPRESSIONS	SUB-BIOME PHYSICAL SIGNATURE TERRAIN EXPRESSIONS
RECORD	para 162	Hyper-arid desert Extremely low and unreliable rain; sparse annuals and drought specialists. Rock desert, sand sea, gravel plain, salt pan, dry mountain desert.
RECORD	para 163	Hot subtropical desert Subtropical subsidence, clear skies, high evaporative demand. Sahara, Arabian deserts, Sonoran-like hot desert sectors, Australian deserts.
RECORD	para 164	Cold desert Low precipitation plus cold winters, frost, and sometimes snow. Great Basin, Gobi, Patagonian steppe/desert transitions, Central Asian basins.
RECORD	Coastal fog desert Very low rain but regular marine fog/dew in some coastal	Coastal fog desert Very low rain but regular marine fog/dew in some coastal settings. Atacama margins, Namib coastal belt.
RECORD	Semi-desert / desert steppe More continuous grass and shrub cover than true dese	Semi-desert / desert steppe More continuous grass and shrub cover than true desert but water still limits production. Desert grassland, semi-desert scrub, cold steppe margins.
RECORD	para 167	Dune and sand-sheet systems Mobile or semi-stable sand; burial and abrasion select for specialist plants. Barchan dunes, longitudinal dunes, nebkhas, interdune flats.
ALLOW	para 168	Gravel / stony desert pavement Surface armor reduces erosion and infiltration; shallow soils; runoff may be concentrated. Reg, stony plains, desert pavement, rocky plateau.
RECORD	Badlands / clay-rich drylands Fine substrate, high runoff and erosion, low infil	Badlands / clay-rich drylands Fine substrate, high runoff and erosion, low infiltration in some crusted surfaces. Gullied marl, shale badlands, mudstone hills.
RECORD	para 170	Salt desert / playa Closed basin evaporation concentrates salts; seasonal standing water possible. Salt pan, alkali flat, saline mudflat, ephemeral lake margin.
RECORD	para 171	Xeric shrubland Woody shrubs dominate; drought, fire, soils or grazing maintain structure. Sagebrush steppe, Mediterranean-type dry scrub, thorn scrub.
RECORD	Succulent / spiny desert scrub Succulents or spinescent plants exploit episodic	Succulent / spiny desert scrub Succulents or spinescent plants exploit episodic water and reduce water loss. Nama-Karoo-like scrub, succulent Karoo, cactus scrub, arid thornbush.
RECORD	para 173	Desert oasis / springline Groundwater or spring supply locally overrides regional aridity. Riparian oasis, spring marsh, palm oasis, groundwater-dependent woodland.
ALLOW	para 174	A good dryland map should separate at least three dimensions: (1) water deficit, (2) substrate/landform, and (3) plant growth form. For example, a sandy arid basin and a rocky semiarid mountain front may receive similar rainfall but have different infiltration, runoff, rooting depth, and plant composition. [13, 14]
RECORD	para 175	Map drylands by routing water as well as by assigning rainfall classes. Ephemeral channels, alluvial fans, groundwater discharge, and shaded north-facing slopes can form narrow green corridors through an otherwise dry matrix.
DEFINE	para 177	Conceptual synthesis; local boundaries are gradual and classification-dependent. 10
RECORD	para 178	8. Temperate forests, rainforests, and Mediterranean systems
RECORD	para 179	Temperate biomes occupy many combinations of winter cold, summer heat, precipitation, and seasonality. Distance from the ocean often separates mild oceanic forest from strongly seasonal continental forest. Mediterranean climates are unusual because summers are dry while winters are the wetter season; their shrublands and woodlands frequently have long histories of nutrient limitation and fire. [15]
RECORD	SUB-BIOME SIGNATURE TERRAIN / REGION EXAMPLES	SUB-BIOME SIGNATURE TERRAIN / REGION EXAMPLES
RECORD	para 181	Temperate oceanic broadleaf forest Mild winters; cool-to-warm summers; rainfall relatively even. Western Europe, southern Chile margins, parts of New Zealand and Tasmania.
RECORD	Temperate seasonal deciduous	Temperate seasonal deciduous forest Warm growing season and cold winter; leaf fall avoids winter stress. Eastern North America, Europe, East Asia.
RECORD	para 183	Temperate mixed forest Broadleaf deciduous species mixed with conifers; transitional climate or soil conditions. Northeastern North America, East Asia, central European transitions.
RECORD	Temperate conifer forest Conifer dominance under cool, dry, nutrient-poor or	Temperate conifer forest Conifer dominance under cool, dry, nutrient-poor or disturbance regimes. Interior ranges, sandy soils, fire-adapted pine systems, some continental margins.
RECORD	para 185	Temperate rain forest Very high rainfall, often maritime; large trees, mosses, epiphytes and nurse logs. Pacific Northwest, coastal British Columbia/Alaska, southern Chile, Tasmania, New Zealand.
RECORD	Cloud forest (subtropical/temperate) Persistent or frequent cloud immersion at e	Cloud forest (subtropical/temperate) Persistent or frequent cloud immersion at elevation, often on windward slope. Mountain island slopes, subtropical uplands, selected coastal ranges.
RECORD	para 187	Mediterranean chaparral Hard-leaved shrubland; hot dry summer; fire adaptations vary by species. California and Baja California.
RECORD	para 188	Maquis / garrigue Mediterranean evergreen shrubland; garrigue often lower and more open on calcareous or degraded sites. Mediterranean Basin.
RECORD	para 189	Matorral Sclerophyll shrubland and woodland; fire and drought effects vary regionally. Central Chile.
RECORD	para 190	Fynbos Species-rich, often nutrient-poor shrubland with proteoid, ericoid and restioid components; fire-sensitive timing. Cape Floristic Region, South Africa.
RECORD	Kwongan Species-rich southwestern Australian shrubland/heath on	Kwongan Species-rich southwestern Australian shrubland/heath on nutrient-poor soils. Southwestern Australia.
RECORD	Mediterranean open woodland /	Mediterranean open woodland / savanna Oak, pine or other woodland structure combined with grass/shrub layers and recurrent fire/grazing. Dehesa/montado-like systems and other regional open woodlands.
DEFINE	Why the Mediterranean family is not just “dry forest”	Why the Mediterranean family is not just “dry forest”
REQUIRE	para 194	Its defining climatic pattern is usually wet winter and dry summer, unlike summer-rainfall savanna or many continental steppe systems. Plants must cope with summer water deficit while often exploiting winter moisture. Fire regimes, nutrient-poor soils, and regional evolutionary histories produce similar-looking but compositionally distinct shrublands: chaparral, maquis, matorral, fynbos, and kwongan are analogues, not exact ecological equivalents. [15]
DEFINE	para 196	Conceptual synthesis; local boundaries are gradual and classification-dependent. 11
RECORD	para 197	9. Grasslands, savannas, shrublands, and open woodlands
RECORD	para 198	These are not merely failed forests. Open biomes can be maintained by climate, seasonal drought, fire, herbivory, shallow or nutrient-poor soils, waterlogging, cold, or interactions among these factors. In many regions, removing fire or large herbivores changes woody cover; elsewhere rainfall and soil constraints keep forests from closing even without frequent fire.
RECORD	SUB-BIOME SIGNATURE EXAMPLES / TERRAIN	SUB-BIOME SIGNATURE EXAMPLES / TERRAIN
RECORD	para 200	Tropical savanna Warm all year; wet/dry season; grasses and scattered trees; fire often important. Wet savanna, dry savanna, thorn savanna, wooded savanna, palm savanna.
RECORD	para 201	Tropical wooded grassland A more tree-rich grassland mosaic; canopy varies with fire and soil moisture. Miombo woodland, mopane woodland, cerrado woodland/savanna mosaics.
RECORD	para 202	Montane grassland / shrubland Cooler elevation; frost, wind, fire, and shallow soils limit closed forest. Puna, páramo, tropical montane grassland, afromontane grassland.
RECORD	para 203	Temperate tallgrass prairie Subhumid; relatively productive tall grasses and deep-rooted herbaceous cover. North American tallgrass prairie; mesic prairie remnants.
RECORD	para 204	Mixed-grass prairie Intermediate moisture; mixture of taller and shorter grasses, often with shrubs. Central Great Plains transitions.
RECORD	para 205	Shortgrass steppe Semi-arid; short drought-adapted grasses and low shrubs. Western Great Plains, dry continental basins.
RECORD	para 206	Eurasian steppe Strong continentality; cold winters, warm/hot summers; little tree cover over large areas. Pontic-Caspian, Kazakh, Mongolian and Central Asian steppe types.
RECORD	Pampas / temperate grassland Humid to subhumid temperate grassland on plains and	Pampas / temperate grassland Humid to subhumid temperate grassland on plains and loess/alluvial soils. Pampas of Argentina and Uruguay.
RECORD	Veld / Highveld grassland Seasonal rainfall, frost at elevation in some sectors,	Veld / Highveld grassland Seasonal rainfall, frost at elevation in some sectors, fire and grazing. Southern African grasslands.
RECORD	para 209	Patagonian steppe Cool, windy and dry; often leeward of the Andes. Shrub steppe, bunchgrass steppe, rocky plateau.
