RECORD	para 285	13. Continental transects: North America and Eurasia
RECORD	para 286	These examples show how large-scale patterns arise and why biome boundaries curve, fragment, and interlock. They are schematic, not literal west-to-east or north-to-south transects for every latitude.
RECORD	para 287	North America: Pacific margin to continental interior
RECORD	North American mountain mosaics	North American mountain mosaics
RECORD	para 290	• Olympic and coastal ranges: maritime moisture, cloud belts, temperate rain forest, snowfields, and dry lee-side areas over short distances.
RECORD	para 291	• Rocky Mountains: foothill grassland or shrubland -> montane forest -> subalpine forest/krummholz -> alpine tundra; aspect and snowpack create wet hollows and dry ridges.
RECORD	para 292	• Great Basin ranges: basin-and-range relief isolates mountain “islands” of woodland and montane forest above desert valleys; north-facing slopes and springs can support mesic pockets.
RECORD	para 293	• Appalachians: elevation and aspect create cooler coves, rich hardwood forests, high-elevation spruce-fir patches and exposed heath balds in regional contexts.
RECORD	para 294	Eurasia: Atlantic margin to interior Asia
RECORD	para 296	Eurasia demonstrates both latitude and continentalness: moving north cools the climate, while moving inland strengthens seasonal extremes and often increases aridity; mountain arcs then create strong local exceptions.
