RECORD	para 368	16. A practical ruleset for assigning biomes to terrain
RECORD	para 369	For a GIS map, simulation, or procedural terrain system, a robust approach is hierarchical: classify broad climate first, then modify it by relief and water routing, then apply soil and disturbance overlays. Avoid using altitude as a direct biome lookup without latitude and moisture context.
RECORD	Suggested classification pipeline	Suggested classification pipeline
RECORD	para 372	Use monthly temperature and precipitation, coldest month, growing-season length, precipitation seasonality, PET or aridity index, snow fraction and frost frequency. Derive broad thermal/moisture domain.
RECORD	para 373	2. Continentalness and circulation
RECORD	para 374	Estimate maritime influence, seasonal temperature range, prevailing windward direction, storm-track exposure, monsoon seasonality and coastal-current context where relevant.
RECORD	para 376	Compute elevation, slope, aspect, curvature, roughness, topographic wetness, distance to drainage, local relief, valley depth and ridge exposure.
DEFINE	para 378	Where possible, use precipitation and temperature fields corrected for topography; distinguish windward uplift from lee-side rain shadows. Do not assume every side is simply wet/dry.
RECORD	para 379	5. Water-routing layer
RECORD	para 380	Track runoff accumulation, flood recurrence, seasonal wetness, groundwater discharge, lakes, snowmelt paths and closed basins. Apply riparian, floodplain, wetland and salt-basin overlays.
RECORD	para 381	6. Substrate / soil layer
WHEN	para 382	Use lithology, soil texture/depth, drainage class, pH/salinity, parent material and soil age if available. These modify forest/grassland/shrubland outcomes and generate edaphic specialists.
RECORD	para 383	7. Potential vegetation class
DEFINE	para 384	Assign the broad biome and candidate sub-biome that fits the climate envelope, then test whether tree cover is plausible given fire, cold, waterlogging, soil and disturbance.
RECORD	para 385	8. Disturbance and land-use state
RECORD	para 386	Apply fire regime, grazing/herbivory, storm damage, flood regime, avalanches, human land use and succession. Keep potential vegetation separate from present land cover.
RECORD	para 387	9. Ecotones and uncertainty
RECORD	para 388	Blend or probabilistically assign where climate envelopes overlap. Preserve confidence scores and multiple factors rather than forcing every transition into a hard line.
DEFINE	para 390	Conceptual synthesis; local boundaries are gradual and classification-dependent. 20
