RECORD	Potential vegetation versus current land cover	Potential vegetation versus current land cover
DEFINE	Uncertainty is structural, not just measurement error	Uncertainty is structural, not just measurement error
RECORD	para 416	• Global classifications use different variable sets and boundary rules, and their maps do not always agree. The biome-inventory literature documents divergence among global products. [12]
RECORD	para 417	• Climate-only classifications often miss substrate-controlled vegetation, wetlands, and terrain microclimates.
RECORD	para 418	• A single “mountain” category hides huge differences between windward forest, lee desert, alpine meadow, and glacier terrain.
RECORD	para 419	• Ecological communities reflect history and available species as well as current climate, so one global rule cannot predict every local assemblage.
WHEN	para 420	For high-fidelity mapping, retain both the best label and the evidence behind it: climate drivers, terrain position, soil/water factors, and confidence. When uncertainty is high, store two plausible sub-biomes or an ecotone instead of inventing false precision.
DEFINE	para 422	Conceptual synthesis; local boundaries are gradual and classification-dependent. 22
RECORD	para 423	18. Glossary and reference map
RECORD	TERM WORKING DEFINITION	TERM WORKING DEFINITION
RECORD	para 425	Biome A broad ecological formation, commonly associated with climate and dominant vegetation structure, though definitions vary.
RECORD	para 426	Sub-biome A practical subdivision of a biome; not a universally standardized formal rank.
RECORD	para 427	Ecoregion A geographically distinct area characterized by a particular assemblage of communities and species.
RECORD	para 428	Ecological land unit (ELU) An integrated unit produced by combining physical setting and land-cover information, such as bioclimate, landform, lithology and land cover.
RECORD	para 429	Continentalness Degree of inland versus maritime climate influence, often expressed by seasonal temperature range and moisture patterns.
RECORD	para 430	Orographic precipitation Precipitation caused or enhanced by air being forced to rise over topography.
RECORD	para 431	Rain shadow A region of reduced precipitation in the lee of a mountain range relative to windward exposure, under suitable circulation conditions.
WHEN	Potential evapotranspiration	Potential evapotranspiration (PET) An estimate of atmospheric demand for water from a vegetated surface if water supply were not limiting.
RECORD	para 433	Aridity index A ratio or index comparing precipitation with evaporative demand; definitions differ by product.
RECORD	para 434	Azonal ecosystem A system whose local control (such as flooding, salinity or substrate) overrides the broad zonal climate-vegetation pattern.
RECORD	para 435	Edaphic Relating to soil conditions such as texture, depth, chemistry, drainage and nutrient supply.
RECORD	para 436	Ecotone A transition area between ecological systems, sometimes gradual and sometimes sharply bounded.
RECORD	para 437	Treeline The upper or poleward limit of tree growth, varying with climate, exposure, species and disturbance.
RECORD	para 438	Krummholz Stunted or wind-shaped woody growth near treeline, often bent or shrub-like due to exposure.
RECORD	para 439	Fellfield Open alpine or arctic vegetation with much exposed ground, rock and sparse low plants.
RECORD	para 440	Potential vegetation Vegetation expected under the environmental conditions specified by a model, often excluding current human land use.
RECORD	para 441	Land cover The surface cover actually present, such as forest, grass, crop, bare ground, water or built area.
