RECORD	para 472	Use these references as complementary lenses, not as competing answers to a single taxonomy question. For broad climate geography, start with [2]; for species assemblages and conservation, [1]; for climate + landform + lithology, [3] and [16]; for mountain belts and orographic effects, [4], [5] and [7]; for classification uncertainty, [6] and [12].
DEFINE	para 473	End of guide. The most useful high-fidelity map is usually a layered model with continuous drivers and multiple categorical overlays, not one flat biome raster.
RECORD	Earth biomes, sub-biomes, and terrain - reference guide 1	Earth biomes, sub-biomes, and terrain - reference guide 1
RECORD	EARTH BIOMES, SUB-BIOMES, AND	EARTH BIOMES, SUB-BIOMES, AND
RECORD	para 4	How climate sets the broad envelope, while landform, water, soil, and geology carve that envelope into real landscapes
RECORD	para 6	A biome is not simply a place at a particular latitude. At broad scales, temperature and water availability strongly constrain vegetation. Terrain then redistributes heat, precipitation, runoff, snow, soil depth, and exposure over much smaller distances. The result is a nested landscape: broad biome -> regional ecoregion -> local habitat or sub-biome-like patch.
RECORD	para 12	uplift + shade + drainage
RECORD	Biome / sub-biome	Biome / sub-biome
RECORD	para 18	recurring ecological pattern
RECORD	para 19	Figure 1. A practical way to think about biome formation: global climate creates the outer envelope, but terrain and the soil-water system repeatedly reshape conditions at finer scales. These feedbacks make neighboring landscapes ecologically different even under the same regional climate.
RECORD	One important terminology note	One important terminology note
RECORD	Earth biomes, sub-biomes, and terrain - reference guide 2	Earth biomes, sub-biomes, and terrain - reference guide 2
RECORD	para 23	1. THE FIRST FILTER: ENERGY AND WATER
DEFINE	para 25	A useful mental model is an atmospheric "budget":
RECORD	Input What it does	Input What it does
RECORD	para 27	Solar energy Sets potential heating and growing-season energy; varies with latitude, season, cloudiness, and slope aspect.
RECORD	para 28	Rain / snow Adds water. Snow can also store water and delay its delivery into the soil until melt.
RECORD	Evaporation +	Evaporation + transpiration Removes water from soil and vegetation; demand rises with energy, wind, and dryness.
RECORD	para 30	Runoff + drainage Moves water away from a location, often making ridges and steep slopes much drier than nearby hollows.
RECORD	para 31	Storage Soil, wetlands, lakes, groundwater, snowpack, and organic layers can buffer dry periods.
RECORD	para 33	Source: Beck, Zimmermann, McVicar et al. (2018), Scientific Data, global 1-km Köppen-Geiger climate maps. https://doi.org/10.1038/sdata.2018.214
RECORD	The Whittaker-style intuition	The Whittaker-style intuition
RECORD	Polar desert / ice	Polar desert / ice
RECORD	Latitude / climate belt tendency (stylized)	Latitude / climate belt tendency (stylized)
RECORD	para 40	Real biome boundaries follow climate, circulation, ocean influence, terrain, and history - not latitude alone.
RECORD	Earth biomes, sub-biomes, and terrain - reference guide 3	Earth biomes, sub-biomes, and terrain - reference guide 3
RECORD	para 42	2. MAJOR TERRESTRIAL BIOMES - AND THEIR COMMON
RECORD	para 44	The following is a practical synthesis rather than a single universal classification. Real maps divide the Earth into many more ecoregions, and the names of intermediate categories vary. The key is the physical logic behind each group.
RECORD	Broad biome Typical environmental envelope Common subtypes / expressions Terrain	Broad biome Typical environmental envelope Common subtypes / expressions Terrain effects
RECORD	Tropical moist forest Warm year-round, high rainfall, long	Tropical moist forest Warm year-round, high rainfall, long growing season. Lowland rainforest; montane cloud forest; peat swamp forest. Mountains create cloud belts; floodplains and slopes separate wetland and upland forests.
RECORD	Tropical dry forest /	Tropical dry forest / savanna Warm; strong wet-dry seasonality or water limitation. Seasonal dry forest; wooded savanna; grass-dominated savanna; thorn scrub. Fire, drainage, shallow soil, and slope moisture change tree cover dramatically.
RECORD	Desert / xeric	Desert / xeric shrubland Low effective precipitation; high evaporative demand in many regions. Hot desert; cold desert; semi-desert shrubland; salt desert. Alluvial fans, playas, dunes, rocky slopes, and wadis create strong habitat mosaics.
RECORD	Mediterranean	Mediterranean woodland / shrubland Mild/wet season + dry summer; recurring fire common. Chaparral; maquis; matorral; fynbos-like shrub systems. Aspect, slope, bedrock, and fire history can separate woodland from shrubland and grassland.
RECORD	para 50	Temperate forest Moderate temperatures; precipitation often sufficient for trees. Deciduous broadleaf; mixed forest; temperate rainforest; dry woodland. Rain shadows, valley cold-air pooling, aspect, and soil depth alter forest structure.
RECORD	Temperate grassland /	Temperate grassland / shrubland Seasonal climate; water or disturbance can limit trees. Prairie; steppe; pampas; montane grassland; shrub-steppe. Plains promote broad grasslands; slopes and shallow soils favor shorter vegetation.
ALLOW	para 52	Boreal forest / taiga Long cold winters; short growing season. Closed conifer forest; open taiga; peatland complexes; subarctic woodland. Lowlands can be waterlogged; uplands drain faster; permafrost may reorganize drainage.
RECORD	para 53	Tundra Very short growing season; cold soils; low stature vegetation. Arctic tundra; alpine tundra; polar desert; wet sedge tundra. Elevation, wind exposure, snow cover, and permafrost determine which microhabitats persist.
RECORD	para 54	WWF-style global ecoregion maps further subdivide terrestrial environments into many ecological regions, including flooded grasslands and savannas, montane grasslands and shrublands, mangroves, and numerous forest and desert categories. The exact hierarchy differs among systems, so map legends should always be read with their stated definitions.
RECORD	Earth biomes, sub-biomes, and terrain - reference guide 4	Earth biomes, sub-biomes, and terrain - reference guide 4
DEFINE	para 56	3. TERRAIN IS A CLIMATE MODIFIER
RECORD	para 57	Terrain does not replace regional climate; it redistributes it. A mountain ridge, canyon, plateau, dune field, river terrace, or shaded slope can create local energy and water conditions that differ strongly from the surrounding landscape. The most important terrain variables are elevation, slope, aspect, relief, and position relative to wind and water flow.
RECORD	para 58	Mountain terrain converts one regional climate into many local microclimates.
RECORD	para 59	Windward: uplift -> cooling -> clouds/precipitation -> wetter soils Leeward: sinking air -> warming/drying -> rain shadow
RECORD	para 61	montane forest dry woodland / shrub / grassland
RECORD	para 62	Figure 2. A simplified mountain transect. Rising air cools as it expands, encouraging cloud formation and precipitation on the windward side. Descending air on the leeward side is warmer and drier, creating a rain shadow. Elevation also lowers average temperature and changes snow duration, growing season, and vegetation height.
RECORD	para 63	Elevation: the vertical biome ladder
RECORD	para 65	Aspect: the same elevation can behave like two different climates
RECORD	para 67	Evidence: global mountain studies show biodiversity patterns associated with temperature, rainfall, relief, erosion, and soil heterogeneity, demonstrating that terrain and geological history interact with climate rather than acting as isolated controls.
RECORD	para 68	Reference: Antonelli et al. (2018), Nature Geoscience. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41561-018-0236-z
RECORD	Earth biomes, sub-biomes, and terrain - reference guide 5	Earth biomes, sub-biomes, and terrain - reference guide 5
RECORD	para 70	4. WATER MOVES DOWNHILL - AND BIOMES FOLLOW THE
RECORD	para 73	wet valley / floodplain
RECORD	para 74	Terrain redistributes water: ridge, slope, hollow, terrace, and floodplain can host different plant communities.
RECORD	para 75	Upper slopes: faster drainage, thinner soils Valleys: water + sediment accumulation Aspect: changes solar exposure and drying
RECORD	para 76	Figure 3. Cross-sectional terrain model. Runoff and subsurface flow concentrate in depressions and valley bottoms. That water movement changes soil saturation, nutrient transport, and disturbance frequency, producing a fine-scale mosaic of vegetation.
RECORD	para 78	Reference: Noe (2013), "Interactions among hydrogeomorphology, vegetation, and nutrient biogeochemistry in floodplain ecosystems." DOI: https://doi.org/10.1016/B978-0-12-374739-6.00338-9
RECORD	Drainage can create "hidden sub-biomes"	Drainage can create "hidden sub-biomes"
RECORD	Earth biomes, sub-biomes, and terrain - reference guide 6	Earth biomes, sub-biomes, and terrain - reference guide 6
RECORD	para 82	5. GEOLOGY AND SOIL: THE TERRAIN UNDER THE TERRAIN
RECORD	A useful state-factor model	A useful state-factor model
RECORD	Factor Landscape consequence Possible vegetation response	Factor Landscape consequence Possible vegetation response
RECORD	Parent material Controls mineral supply, texture, base status, and	Parent material Controls mineral supply, texture, base status, and weathering pathways. Species adapted to different pH, nutrient, or texture conditions.
RECORD	para 87	Slope / relief Changes erosion, soil depth, runoff, and stability. Deep-soil woodland in stable hollows; short vegetation on exposed shallow soils.
ALLOW	para 88	Soil texture Changes infiltration and water storage. Sandy sites may drain rapidly; clay-rich sites may hold water but drain slowly.
RECORD	Organic layer Buffers temperature and moisture, stores carbon and	Organic layer Buffers temperature and moisture, stores carbon and nutrients. Can support mosses, shrubs, conifers, or wetland plants under cold/wet conditions.
RECORD	para 90	Permafrost / hardpan Creates shallow barriers to drainage and root penetration. Can promote surface wetness despite low precipitation; supports specialized tundra or shrub communities.
RECORD	para 91	A global soil analysis found that climate, parent material, vegetation, and topography together explain much more variation in ecosystem properties than soil age alone. Another global soil-climate-biome analysis found strong relationships between soil properties, temperature, precipitation, and biome, reinforcing the idea that soil is part of the climate-biome-terrain coupling rather than a passive layer beneath plants.
RECORD	para 92	References: Delvaux et al. (2010), Nature Communications / global cross-biome soil chronosequences; Shen et al. (2019), Biogeosciences, global soil-climate-biome diagram.
RECORD	para 93	Links: https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-020-18451-3 | https://doi.org/10.5194/bg-16-2857-2019
RECORD	Earth biomes, sub-biomes, and terrain - reference guide 7	Earth biomes, sub-biomes, and terrain - reference guide 7
RECORD	para 95	6. TERRAIN EXPRESSION OF BIOMES
ALLOW	para 97	Lowland basins and wet slopes can support tall, layered forests. On steep tropical mountains, elevation, cloud immersion, and thin/young soils can produce montane forests and cloud forests. Valley bottoms may contain swamp or floodplain forests, while exposed ridges can be shorter and more wind-stressed.
RECORD	Savanna and tropical dry systems	Savanna and tropical dry systems
RECORD	para 99	Small changes in water table, soil depth, fire frequency, and drainage can change tree cover. Deep, well-drained soils may carry taller woody vegetation where rainfall permits, while shallow rocky soils or frequently burned areas can remain grass-dominated.
ALLOW	para 101	Mountain windward slopes can be wetter than nearby lowlands. Valley floors may be frost-prone because cold dense air pools at night, altering species composition. Limestone, sandstone, volcanic ash, and glacial deposits can each create different soil conditions within the same climatic forest zone.
RECORD	Grassland and shrub-steppe	Grassland and shrub-steppe
RECORD	para 103	Broad flat terrain favors widespread grasses where water limitation or fire prevents closed forest. Hills and mountains create a patchwork of shallow soils, snow traps, riparian strips, and sheltered pockets that can support shrubs or trees inside an otherwise grass-dominated regional biome.
RECORD	para 105	Deserts are highly structured by landform. Fans, playas, dunes, rocky pavements, canyon floors, and ephemeral stream channels differ in water storage and disturbance. A single desert can therefore contain many sharply contrasting "micro-biomes" separated by only a few kilometers.
RECORD	Boreal and tundra	Boreal and tundra
RECORD	para 107	Flat, cold lowlands can be surprisingly wet because evaporation is low and drainage is impeded by permafrost or shallow frozen layers. Raised ground, ridges, wind-exposed slopes, and south-facing aspects can be drier and warmer, creating a mosaic from wet sedge to shrub tundra to open woodland.
DEFINE	para 109	A biome label is a regional summary. Terrain is one reason the same broad biome can contain forests, wetlands, shrublands, grasslands, bare rock, and many transitional communities side by side.
RECORD	Earth biomes, sub-biomes, and terrain - reference guide 8	Earth biomes, sub-biomes, and terrain - reference guide 8
RECORD	para 111	7. MOUNTAINS: COMPRESSED BIOMES + HIGH TOPOGRAPHIC
RECORD	para 113	Mountains are unusually useful for understanding the biome-terrain relationship because elevation changes temperature quickly while relief simultaneously changes rainfall, exposure, snow distribution, and erosion. A single mountain slope can cross several vegetation belts in a relatively short horizontal distance.
RECORD	Zone Typical physical conditions Common ecological expression	Zone Typical physical conditions Common ecological expression
RECORD	Foothill / montane	Foothill / montane lowland Warmer, deeper soils, more developed drainage networks. Forest, woodland, or montane grassland depending on regional climate.
RECORD	para 116	Montane Cooler, often wetter; slopes can be steep and erosion strong. Montane forest, conifer forest, cloud forest, or shrubland.
RECORD	para 117	Subalpine Shorter growing season, high snow influence, strong winds. Open conifer woodland, tall shrub, meadow.
RECORD	para 118	Alpine Above treeline; cold, windy, short growing season. Alpine meadow, fellfield, dwarf shrub, cushion plant communities.
RECORD	Nival / permanent	Nival / permanent snow-ice Extremely short or absent growing season; exposed ground sparse. Bare rock, snow, ice, sparse pioneer organisms.
RECORD	para 120	Importantly, there is no universal elevation at which a particular biome "starts." Treeline and other ecological boundaries depend on latitude, maritime influence, moisture, wind, disturbance, and species history. Mountain research also shows that geology can matter directly by controlling relief and soil heterogeneity.
RECORD	para 121	Reference: Körner et al. (2011), "A definition of mountains and their bioclimatic belts for global comparisons of biodiversity data." https://doi.org/10.1007/s00035-011-0094-4
RECORD	para 122	Reference: Ott et al. (2020), "How Lithology Impacts Global Topography, Vegetation, and Animal Biodiversity." https://doi.org/10.1029/2020GL088649
RECORD	Earth biomes, sub-biomes, and terrain - reference guide 9	Earth biomes, sub-biomes, and terrain - reference guide 9
RECORD	para 124	8. COASTS, WETLANDS, AND OTHER WATER-CONTROLLED
RECORD	para 126	Some of the strongest ecological boundaries are not caused by broad climate at all. They are caused by persistent water geometry: coastlines, river corridors, deltas, lakes, springs, and wetlands. Here the terrain is inseparable from hydrology.
RECORD	Landform Hydrologic condition Ecological result	Landform Hydrologic condition Ecological result
RECORD	Floodplain Periodic inundation, sediment deposition, groundwater	Floodplain Periodic inundation, sediment deposition, groundwater connection. Riparian woodland, wet meadow, floodplain forest, swamp, or marsh.
RECORD	Delta / estuary Freshwater + tides + salinity gradients + sediment	Delta / estuary Freshwater + tides + salinity gradients + sediment accumulation. Marsh, mangrove, mudflat, tidal forest, brackish habitats.
RECORD	Peatland / bog Persistently waterlogged soil, slow decomposition, low	Peatland / bog Persistently waterlogged soil, slow decomposition, low oxygen. Sphagnum-rich bog, fen, wet shrubland, stunted woodland.
RECORD	para 131	Salt flat / saline depression Evaporation concentrates salts; episodic flooding. Halophytic shrubland, salt marsh, salt pan, sparse vegetation.
RECORD	para 132	Coastal dune Wind-driven sand movement; drainage often rapid. Dune grassland, beach shrubland, coastal heath, pioneer vegetation.
RECORD	para 133	In a floodplain, the relevant terrain is not just "flat land." What matters is elevation relative to the river, connection to the channel, frequency and depth of flooding, and how sediment is deposited. Hydrologic connectivity creates gradients from frequently flooded bars to rarely flooded terraces, each with different soils and plant communities.
RECORD	para 134	For a broad global framing, WWF terrestrial ecoregions explicitly separate flooded grasslands and savannas, mangroves, freshwater systems, and montane ecosystems from other major terrestrial classes. WWF Terrestrial Ecoregions of the World.
RECORD	Earth biomes, sub-biomes, and terrain - reference guide 10	Earth biomes, sub-biomes, and terrain - reference guide 10
DEFINE	para 136	9. BIOMES ARE NOT JUST PRODUCTS OF TERRAIN - THEY
RECORD	ALSO CHANGE TERRAIN	ALSO CHANGE TERRAIN
RECORD	para 138	Once vegetation becomes established, it starts modifying the physical environment. Roots stabilize soil. Canopy changes wind and rainfall interception. Litter alters infiltration and evaporation. Beavers, grasses, mangroves, and riparian forests can reshape channels and sediment patterns. Fire changes vegetation structure and erosion. The result is a set of feedback loops.
RECORD	Vegetation process Physical change Potential ecological feedback	Vegetation process Physical change Potential ecological feedback
RECORD	para 140	Root growth Improves soil aggregation and stabilizes slopes. More soil retention -> more moisture/nutrients -> continued plant growth.
RECORD	para 141	Canopy interception Changes rainfall reaching soil and surface evaporation. Understory becomes a different microclimate than exposed ground.
RECORD	Organic matter accumulation Raises water-holding capacity and changes	Organic matter accumulation Raises water-holding capacity and changes decomposition. Longer moisture persistence can support denser vegetation.
RECORD	Fire Removes biomass, exposes soil, changes runoff and	Fire Removes biomass, exposes soil, changes runoff and nutrient pulses. Repeated fire can maintain grassland/savanna where forest could otherwise expand.
RECORD	para 144	River vegetation Changes roughness, sediment trapping, bank strength, and channel form. New landforms create new habitat for plants and animals.
RECORD	para 145	River corridors are a particularly clear example of two-way coupling. Reviews of fluvial biogeomorphology show that vegetation responds to channel form and flow while also changing sediment transport, bank stability, and the development of vegetated landforms. The ecosystem therefore becomes a moving physical system, not a static patch of plants placed on top of terrain.
RECORD	para 146	Reference: Corenblit et al. (2007), "Reciprocal interactions and adjustments between fluvial landforms and vegetation dynamics in river corridors." https://doi.org/10.1016/j.earscirev.2007.05.004
RECORD	Earth biomes, sub-biomes, and terrain - reference guide 11	Earth biomes, sub-biomes, and terrain - reference guide 11
RECORD	para 148	10. A REAL-WORLD METHOD: PREDICT THE LOCAL BIOME
RECORD	para 150	For worldbuilding, simulation, environmental modeling, or simply interpreting a landscape, you can estimate the likely vegetation pattern by moving through the following sequence.
RECORD	para 151	1. Establish the regional climate
RECORD	para 152	Estimate latitude, seasonal temperature, annual precipitation, precipitation seasonality, prevailing winds, and distance from ocean.
RECORD	para 153	2. Locate the terrain
RECORD	para 154	Measure elevation, slope, aspect, relief, and position relative to mountain barriers or major water bodies.
RECORD	para 155	3. Predict local moisture
DEFINE	para 156	Ask where precipitation is concentrated, where runoff collects, how quickly water drains, and whether snow or groundwater stores it.
RECORD	para 157	4. Infer the soil environment
RECORD	para 158	Use parent rock, sediment type, erosion/deposition, organic matter, texture, depth, pH, and water table as filters.
RECORD	para 160	Fire, grazing, floods, landslides, storms, avalanches, drought, and human land use can maintain an alternative vegetation state.
RECORD	para 161	6. Select the vegetation structure
RECORD	para 162	Forest, woodland, shrubland, grassland, wetland, desert scrub, alpine vegetation, or sparse/bare ground.
RECORD	para 163	7. Name the regional unit
DEFINE	para 164	Only after understanding the physical setting should you assign a biome/ecoregion/subtype label. The label is the summary, not the mechanism.
RECORD	Earth biomes, sub-biomes, and terrain - reference guide 12	Earth biomes, sub-biomes, and terrain - reference guide 12
