RECORD	para 179	Durán, J. & Delgado-Baquerizo, M. (2020). Vegetation structure determines the spatial variability of soil biodiversity across biomes. Scientific Reports 10, 21500. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-020-78483-z
RECORD	para 180	USGS. Bailey Ecoregions of North America - Atlas of Relations Between Climatic Parameters and Distributions of Important Trees and Shrubs. https://pubs.usgs.gov/pp/p1650-e/graphical_bailey.html
RECORD	para 181	World Wildlife Fund. Terrestrial Ecoregions of the World - global ecoregion framework and map resources. https://www.worldwildlife.org/publications/terrestrial-ecoregions-of-the-world
RECORD	para 183	Topography remains an active research topic because its ecological effect is scale-dependent. A 2025 global alpine-zone study, for example, found that vegetation changes covary with elevation and slope aspect, reinforcing that even within one biome class, terrain can modify long-term vegetation response. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jag.2025.104727
RECORD	para 185	A useful causal picture is: climate sets the envelope -> terrain redistributes energy and water -> geology/soil controls storage and access -> disturbance selects which vegetation persists -> vegetation feeds back on soil and landform -> the resulting recurring pattern is what we call a biome or sub-biome.
RECORD	EARTH'S UPPER ATMOSPHERE	EARTH'S UPPER ATMOSPHERE
RECORD	AND THE TRANSITION TO SPACE	AND THE TRANSITION TO SPACE
RECORD	para 3	A physics-first field guide to the stratosphere, mesosphere, thermosphere, ionosphere, exosphere, and the environment beyond the conventional edge of space.
RECORD	para 13	~500-10,000 km* What changes with height?
RECORD	Weather, clouds, most water vapor	Weather, clouds, most water vapor
RECORD	para 15	Ozone absorbs UV; temperature rises upward
RECORD	para 16	Coldest layer; meteors ablate
RECORD	para 17	Absorbs solar EUV/X-rays; aurora, ionization
RECORD	para 18	Rare ballistic atoms; gradual escape to space
DEFINE	para 19	*Bands are schematic, not altitude-proportional; the edge fades gradually.
RECORD	para 20	Core idea: the atmosphere does not end at a hard ceiling. Air gets thinner, collisions become less frequent, the dominant physical description changes, and some particles eventually escape Earth. The familiar layer names describe different patterns within one continuous system.
DEFINE	para 21	Designed for atmospheric visualization, simulation, and deeper scientific reading. Altitude ranges are approximate and vary with conditions. Clickable source links are collected at the end.
RECORD	EARTH'S UPPER ATMOSPHERE | SCIENCE REFERENCE 2	EARTH'S UPPER ATMOSPHERE | SCIENCE REFERENCE 2
RECORD	para 23	1. How to think about the atmosphere
RECORD	para 24	Atmospheric layers are not stacked shells with sealed borders. They are regions defined by different properties: how temperature changes with altitude, whether gases remain mixed, how much ionization exists, and whether ordinary fluid mechanics remains a good approximation. Several classifications overlap rather than align perfectly. [1][2][3]
RECORD	Three maps should be kept separate	Three maps should be kept separate
RECORD	Map What it classifies Main use	Map What it classifies Main use
RECORD	para 27	Thermal layers Troposphere, stratosphere, mesosphere, thermosphere, exosphere Explains temperature structure and broad atmospheric behavior.
WHEN	para 28	Composition / mixing Homosphere, turbopause/homopause, heterosphere Explains when turbulence keeps gases mixed and when molecular diffusion separates species by mass.
RECORD	para 29	Electrical state Ionosphere and its D, E, F regions; plasmasphere beyond Explains ionization, radio propagation, currents, and coupling to space weather.
DEFINE	Collision regime Continuum flow, transitional flow, free-molecular	Collision regime Continuum flow, transitional flow, free-molecular flow Determines whether fluid equations, kinetic models, or a coupling of both is appropriate.
RECORD	para 31	Modeling warning: a single altitude-only label is not enough to determine pressure, temperature, density, composition, ionization, or drag. Latitude, local solar time, season, geomagnetic activity, and recent solar input matter especially above the mesosphere.
RECORD	A useful causal chain	A useful causal chain
RECORD	para 33	Solar radiation and charged particles deposit energy -> chemistry and ionization change -> temperature and pressure gradients drive winds and waves -> density and composition change with altitude -> the collision environment transitions toward rarefied gas -> a tiny fraction of particles can escape. The lower atmosphere drives waves upward, while the upper atmosphere can affect satellites, radio links, and near-Earth space.
RECORD	EARTH'S UPPER ATMOSPHERE | SCIENCE REFERENCE 3	EARTH'S UPPER ATMOSPHERE | SCIENCE REFERENCE 3
RECORD	para 35	2. Altitude map: the major regions
RECORD	para 36	The values below are reference ranges, not universal boundaries. The tropopause and stratopause move; the mesopause altitude varies; the thermosphere expands and contracts with solar activity; and the exosphere is defined by collision physics rather than a visible surface. [1][4]
RECORD	Region Approximate altitude Temperature trend / key process	Region Approximate altitude Temperature trend / key process
RECORD	Troposphere Surface to about 8 km at high	Troposphere Surface to about 8 km at high latitudes; up to 16-18 km in tropics; global mean ~12 km Usually cools upward; weather, clouds, most water vapor and aerosols.
RECORD	para 39	Stratosphere Roughly 12-50 km Warms upward through much of the layer because ozone absorbs ultraviolet radiation.
DEFINE	para 40	Mesosphere Roughly 50-85 km Cools upward; the mesopause is often around -90 C or colder in some conditions; meteor ablation and noctilucent clouds.
RECORD	Thermosphere About 85-500/700 km, with	Thermosphere About 85-500/700 km, with substantial variability Kinetic temperature rises strongly as sparse gas absorbs EUV/X-rays and particle energy; auroral energy deposition and satellite drag.
RECORD	Exosphere Begins roughly 500-1,000 km and	Exosphere Begins roughly 500-1,000 km and thins outward over thousands of km Collisions become rare; light atoms, especially hydrogen, populate extended distributions; gradual transition to space.
RECORD	Ionosphere (overlapping	Ionosphere (overlapping region) Commonly ~60 km to several hundred km or more Charged particles coexist with neutrals; D/E/F regions control radio-wave absorption and propagation.
RECORD	para 44	Karman line convention 100 km by FAI convention; other operational definitions use ~80 km or different thresholds A convention for aerospace, not a physical surface or end of gravity/air.
RECORD	Schematic temperature structure (not to scale above 100 km)	Schematic temperature structure (not to scale above 100 km)
RECORD	para 57	-100 C 0 C 300 C 600 C Actual profiles vary with latitude, season, local time, and solar/geomagnetic activity.
RECORD	para 58	Temperature profile is schematic. Thermospheric temperatures can reach many hundreds to over 1,000 K, and sometimes higher under strong solar forcing, but temperature is not the same thing as heat content or the ability to warm a person or object. [1][5][6]
RECORD	EARTH'S UPPER ATMOSPHERE | SCIENCE REFERENCE 4	EARTH'S UPPER ATMOSPHERE | SCIENCE REFERENCE 4
RECORD	para 60	3. Pressure, density, and why the upper atmosphere expands
RECORD	para 61	Gravity continuously pulls atmospheric mass downward. In approximate hydrostatic balance, the pressure change with height supports the weight of the air above. The ideal-gas law links pressure, temperature, and number density, but temperature alone does not determine density: pressure and molecular composition matter too.
RECORD	Two compact equations	Two compact equations Hydrostatic balance: dp/dz = -rho g
RECORD	para 63	Ideal gas: p = n k T (or p = rho R_specific T)
RECORD	Relative number density (log scale)	Relative number density (log scale)
RECORD	Dense lower air	Dense lower air
RECORD	Thin upper air	Thin upper air
RECORD	para 70	Concept only: real density profiles change with composition, temperature, and solar forcing.
DEFINE	para 71	Why is the atmosphere still present at hundreds of kilometers?
ALLOW	para 73	Important distinction: the thermosphere may have a very high kinetic temperature because individual particles move quickly, while its density is so low that it contains very little thermal energy per unit volume. A thermometer, a spacecraft surface, and a human body do not exchange heat with such a gas the way they do with dense hot air at sea level.
RECORD	EARTH'S UPPER ATMOSPHERE | SCIENCE REFERENCE 5	EARTH'S UPPER ATMOSPHERE | SCIENCE REFERENCE 5
RECORD	para 75	4. Stratosphere: ozone, stable layering, and wave gateway
DEFINE	Ozone is not a solid shell	Ozone is not a solid shell
RECORD	Why the stratosphere matters to the upper atmosphere	Why the stratosphere matters to the upper atmosphere
RECORD	Useful variables for a model	Useful variables for a model
RECORD	para 82	• Ozone number density and UV absorption profile • Temperature and stability (often expressed through the buoyancy frequency) • Mean zonal wind and vertical wind shear • Vertical wave momentum flux and wave breaking • Seasonal and latitude-dependent circulation
RECORD	para 83	Do not represent the ozone layer as a hard altitude band that completely blocks all UV. Absorption depends on wavelength, optical path, solar angle, ozone amount, and other absorbers. The ozone distribution is smooth and variable.
RECORD	EARTH'S UPPER ATMOSPHERE | SCIENCE REFERENCE 6	EARTH'S UPPER ATMOSPHERE | SCIENCE REFERENCE 6
RECORD	para 85	5. Mesosphere: cold, meteors, and rare high clouds
RECORD	para 86	The mesosphere occupies roughly 50-85 km. In the standard temperature profile, temperature decreases upward through the layer and reaches a minimum near the mesopause. This is commonly the coldest region of the atmosphere, with values near or below -85 to -90 C in many descriptions, but the exact profile varies with season, latitude, waves, and dynamics. [1][8][9]
RECORD	Meteors and metal chemistry	Meteors and metal chemistry
RECORD	Noctilucent / polar mesospheric clouds	Noctilucent / polar mesospheric clouds
RECORD	para 91	Dynamics: waves become more influential aloft
RECORD	para 93	Simulation note: noctilucent clouds need a specialized ice microphysics model at about 80 km, not a normal tropospheric cloud recipe simply moved upward. Water availability, very low temperature, meteoric nuclei, particle growth, and solar illumination all matter.
RECORD	EARTH'S UPPER ATMOSPHERE | SCIENCE REFERENCE 7	EARTH'S UPPER ATMOSPHERE | SCIENCE REFERENCE 7
RECORD	para 95	6. Thermosphere: high kinetic temperature, low density
WHEN	para 96	Above the mesopause, the temperature trend reverses and thermospheric kinetic temperature rises with altitude. Extreme-ultraviolet and X-ray solar photons are absorbed by oxygen and nitrogen species, causing excitation, dissociation, and ionization. Energetic particles from the magnetosphere can also deposit energy, especially in auroral zones. The thermosphere expands when heated and contracts when energy input declines. [1][5][6]
DEFINE	Heating is not the same as ordinary hot air	Heating is not the same as ordinary hot air
RECORD	Major thermospheric energy pathways	Major thermospheric energy pathways
RECORD	Input / pathway Physical effect Modeling implication	Input / pathway Physical effect Modeling implication
RECORD	Solar EUV and X-rays Heat, dissociation, and ion production at	Solar EUV and X-rays Heat, dissociation, and ion production at altitude-dependent absorption depths Use wavelength-dependent absorption / parameterized solar forcing, not a constant heater.
RECORD	para 102	Auroral particle precipitation Excites and ionizes upper-atmospheric species; locally heats gas Varies with latitude, magnetic activity, and time.
RECORD	Joule heating Electric currents and ion-neutral friction convert	Joule heating Electric currents and ion-neutral friction convert energy into heat Couples thermospheric neutral winds to ionospheric electrodynamics.
RECORD	Infrared cooling CO2, NO, and other species radiate energy to space	Infrared cooling CO2, NO, and other species radiate energy to space at relevant bands Cooling changes with composition, density, and temperature.
DEFINE	para 105	Conduction / circulation Redistributes heat vertically and horizontally High-altitude temperature is a global dynamical result, not only local sunlight.
RECORD	Why satellite drag changes	Why satellite drag changes
RECORD	EARTH'S UPPER ATMOSPHERE | SCIENCE REFERENCE 8	EARTH'S UPPER ATMOSPHERE | SCIENCE REFERENCE 8
RECORD	para 109	7. The ionosphere: atmosphere as a weak plasma
RECORD	The ionosphere overlaps altitude-defined layers	The ionosphere overlaps altitude-defined layers
RECORD	F region / topside	F region / topside
RECORD	D region (~60-90 km)	D region (~60-90 km) Daytime absorption of HF radio; often weakens greatly at night.
RECORD	E region (~90-150 km)	E region (~90-150 km) Ion production and collisions; sporadic-E sheets can occur.
RECORD	F region (~150 km and above)	F region (~150 km and above)
RECORD	Higher electron density supports long-distance HF	Higher electron density supports long-distance HF skywave propagation; splits/recombines with conditions.
DEFINE	These are functional ionospheric regions, not separate	These are functional ionospheric regions, not separate neutral-atmosphere layers. Their heights vary.
RECORD	D, E, and F regions	D, E, and F regions
RECORD	para 128	D region (~60-90 km): relatively weak ionization but strong collisional effects; often important for daytime absorption of high-frequency radio waves. It weakens greatly at night as the ionization source disappears and recombination dominates.
RECORD	para 129	E region (~90-150 km): a region of regular daytime ionization and variable thin structures such as sporadic-E layers. Collisions remain frequent enough to make ion-neutral interactions important.
RECORD	para 130	F region (~150 km and upward): often contains the peak electron density important for long-distance HF skywave communication. Depending on conditions, it separates into F1 and F2 structures by day and recombines or changes at night.
RECORD	Not a literal mirror	Not a literal mirror
WHEN	para 133	Use an ionosphere model when a simulation needs radio behavior, plasma density, or auroral electrodynamics. A neutral-air temperature profile alone does not specify electron density, ion composition, collision frequency, or magnetic-field coupling.
RECORD	EARTH'S UPPER ATMOSPHERE | SCIENCE REFERENCE 9	EARTH'S UPPER ATMOSPHERE | SCIENCE REFERENCE 9
RECORD	para 135	8. Exosphere: where collisions stop controlling every step
RECORD	Composition separates with height	Composition separates with height
RECORD	Escape mechanisms in plain language	Escape mechanisms in plain language
RECORD	Mechanism What happens Key caveat	Mechanism What happens Key caveat
RECORD	Jeans escape The high-speed tail of a particle velocity distribution includes	Jeans escape The high-speed tail of a particle velocity distribution includes particles that exceed escape speed near the exobase. Depends on species mass, exobase temperature, gravity, and distribution assumptions.
REQUIRE	Photochemical escape A chemical reaction or photochemical process creates a part	Photochemical escape A chemical reaction or photochemical process creates a particle with enough energy to escape. Must track the relevant chemistry and energy partition.
RECORD	Ion outflow / pickup Charged particles are accelerated or transported by electri	Ion outflow / pickup Charged particles are accelerated or transported by electric and magnetic fields, sometimes escaping along open field lines or into the solar-wind environment. Requires plasma and magnetic-field physics.
RECORD	Sputtering / energetic	Sputtering / energetic impacts Energetic particles transfer momentum and eject atmospheric particles. Efficiency depends on particle populations and upper-atmospheric conditions.
DEFINE	para 147	For modern Earth, loss is a slow species-specific process, not the atmosphere boiling off into space. Escape matters over geological time and varies enormously among planets and evolutionary conditions. [14]
RECORD	EARTH'S UPPER ATMOSPHERE | SCIENCE REFERENCE 10	EARTH'S UPPER ATMOSPHERE | SCIENCE REFERENCE 10
RECORD	para 149	9. Where does the atmosphere end?
DEFINE	para 150	No physical surface divides atmosphere from space. There are several useful thresholds because different scientific and operational problems need different definitions.
RECORD	Threshold Meaning Useful for	Threshold Meaning Useful for
RECORD	para 152	100 km Karman line Common FAI convention for the boundary of space. Aerospace records and public communication.
RECORD	para 153	~80 km threshold Used by some US organizations for particular astronautics purposes. Operational categories; not the same definition as FAI.
RECORD	Turbopause / homopause Approximate height above which molecular diffusion	Turbopause / homopause Approximate height above which molecular diffusion increasingly separates constituents instead of turbulent mixing keeping them uniform. Composition models.
RECORD	Exobase Region where mean free path becomes comparable to a	Exobase Region where mean free path becomes comparable to a characteristic scale height. Transition from continuum / collisional to kinetic descriptions.
RECORD	para 156	Orbital drag threshold No single height: drag depends on vehicle, speed, density, solar conditions, and required accuracy. Orbit prediction and mission design.
RECORD	para 157	Geocoronal extent A diffuse hydrogen distribution reaches far from Earth. UV observations and escape physics.
