RECORD	para 163	10. Equations and model choices for simulation
DEFINE	para 164	The following are useful scaffolding equations. They are not a complete upper-atmosphere model; their assumptions should remain explicit.
RECORD	para 165	1) Hydrostatic approximation dp/dz = -rho g
DEFINE	para 166	Good as a background approximation when vertical accelerations are small compared with gravity and pressure-gradient support. It does not resolve waves, rapid outflows, or kinetic exospheric trajectories by itself.
RECORD	para 167	2) Ideal gas relation p = n k T
WHEN	para 168	Here p is pressure, n is number density, k is Boltzmann constant, and T is kinetic temperature. If composition varies, mean molecular mass changes; do not assume a single fixed gas constant all the way from the surface to the exosphere.
RECORD	H = k T / (m g)	H = k T / (m g)
RECORD	para 171	For one species in an isothermal hydrostatic atmosphere, H estimates the vertical distance over which its density falls by e. Warmer gases and lighter particles have larger scale heights. In real Earth conditions, g also decreases slightly with altitude and T changes with z.
RECORD	para 172	4) Mean free path and the Knudsen number
DEFINE	para 173	lambda is the mean free path; Kn = lambda / L
WHEN	para 174	L is the characteristic length scale for the modeled phenomenon. When Kn is much smaller than one, continuum fluid equations are usually appropriate; when it approaches or exceeds one, rarefaction corrections or kinetic methods become necessary. There is no universal fixed altitude for a chosen Kn because L, density, temperature, and gas composition all matter.
RECORD	para 175	5) A practical modeling ladder
WHEN	Goal Reasonable model family Missing if used alone	Goal Reasonable model family Missing if used alone
RECORD	Layered educational	Layered educational visualization Empirical mean temperature and density curves by altitude Local weather, composition dynamics, solar-cycle response.
RECORD	Neutral winds / waves to	Neutral winds / waves to ~100 km Compressible dynamics with radiative/chemical terms and wave forcing Detailed ionization and collisionless particle trajectories.
DEFINE	Thermosphere density and	Thermosphere density and drag Empirical upper-atmosphere models or coupled thermosphere general circulation model Fine composition and space-weather events if drivers/data are inadequate.
DEFINE	para 180	Radio propagation Electron density profile plus refractive / collisional model A neutral-atmosphere model is not sufficient.
RECORD	Exosphere / escape Species-resolved kinetic distribution and relevant	Exosphere / escape Species-resolved kinetic distribution and relevant chemistry/plasma forcing Continuum fluid laws fail as the sole description.
RECORD	EARTH'S UPPER ATMOSPHERE | SCIENCE REFERENCE 12	EARTH'S UPPER ATMOSPHERE | SCIENCE REFERENCE 12
RECORD	para 183	11. How the layers communicate with each other
DEFINE	para 184	The upper atmosphere is a coupled system. An input in one altitude band can change conditions far away because energy, momentum, chemistry, and charged particles are transported vertically and horizontally.
RECORD	Upward coupling from weather	Upward coupling from weather
RECORD	para 186	Convection and flow over mountains generate gravity waves; weather systems generate planetary-scale wave patterns; daily solar heating drives atmospheric tides. These motions can propagate upward, change amplitude, break, and deposit momentum and heat. The effects can include mesospheric temperature changes, shifting wind jets, and changes in thermospheric density.
RECORD	Downward / cross-system coupling	Downward / cross-system coupling
RECORD	para 188	Sudden stratospheric warmings and planetary waves affect the circulation that connects to the mesosphere. Solar and geomagnetic energy deposited in the thermosphere-ionosphere alters winds, composition, electron density, and satellite drag. Electric fields and currents couple the ionosphere to the magnetosphere. Neutral wind and ion motion are linked by collisions, although collision frequency declines with altitude.
RECORD	A useful causal diagram	A useful causal diagram
RECORD	Driver First-order response Possible downstream signature	Driver First-order response Possible downstream signature
RECORD	para 191	Solar EUV changes Ionization and heating change in upper atmosphere Electron density, thermospheric expansion, orbit drag change.
RECORD	para 192	Geomagnetic storm Energetic particles and electric fields inject energy Auroras, Joule heating, composition changes, satellite drag disturbances.
RECORD	para 193	Tropospheric convection / terrain Gravity waves launch upward Middle-atmosphere wind and temperature perturbations.
RECORD	Seasonal circulation Winds redistribute minor species and waves filter	Seasonal circulation Winds redistribute minor species and waves filter differently Changes in mesopause temperatures and polar mesospheric clouds.
RECORD	para 195	Composition changes Radiative cooling/heating and chemistry change Feedbacks in thermospheric temperature and density.
RECORD	Avoid one-way thinking	Avoid one-way thinking
RECORD	para 197	The layers are convenient for teaching, but computational domains often couple several at once. For a credible model, specify boundary conditions for radiation, waves, composition, and electrodynamics rather than imposing independent profiles that cannot exchange energy or momentum.
RECORD	EARTH'S UPPER ATMOSPHERE | SCIENCE REFERENCE 13	EARTH'S UPPER ATMOSPHERE | SCIENCE REFERENCE 13
RECORD	para 199	12. How scientists measure it
RECORD	Method Best at measuring Limitation / interpretation	Method Best at measuring Limitation / interpretation
RECORD	para 201	Radiosondes and balloons Troposphere and lower stratosphere temperature, pressure, humidity, winds Most balloons stop far below the mesosphere; they do not sample the thermosphere.
RECORD	para 202	Lidar and radar Winds, temperature proxies, aerosols, meteors, mesospheric structures, ionospheric echoes depending on instrument Each system samples particular species, geometry, and altitude range.
DEFINE	para 203	Sounding rockets In-situ particles, density, composition, electric fields, and short-lived upper-atmospheric processes Brief snapshots; coverage is sparse in space and time.
RECORD	para 204	Satellites / accelerometers Thermospheric density inferred from drag; composition or temperature from dedicated sensors Retrievals depend on instrument calibration, orbit, and physical models.
ALLOW	para 205	GNSS radio occultation / TEC Integrated refractivity or total electron content; ionosphere affects radio paths Integrated measurements may need inversion/model assumptions to recover vertical structure.
DEFINE	para 206	Airglow and auroral imaging Emissions from excited atoms and molecules; precipitation and dynamics Brightness is not a direct single-variable thermometer; chemistry and line-of-sight matter.
RECORD	UV remote sensing Ozone, geocorona, upper-atmospheric composition and	UV remote sensing Ozone, geocorona, upper-atmospheric composition and emission profiles Requires spectroscopy, radiative transfer, and careful calibration.
DEFINE	Measured quantities are not interchangeable	Measured quantities are not interchangeable
ALLOW	para 209	Temperature, number density, mass density, electron density, composition, optical emission, and drag are different observables. A model may infer one from another only with assumptions about instrument response and the governing physics. For example, an aurora image reports emitted light integrated through a column; it does not independently provide the full 3D temperature, density, and particle precipitation field.
RECORD	Reference data and model families	Reference data and model families
WHEN	para 211	For real research, use mission datasets and documented empirical models appropriate to the requested altitude and date, and preserve solar/geomagnetic drivers such as F10.7 solar radio flux and geomagnetic indices when supplied by the dataset or model. Never present a climatological profile as a live forecast of the current upper atmosphere.
RECORD	EARTH'S UPPER ATMOSPHERE | SCIENCE REFERENCE 14	EARTH'S UPPER ATMOSPHERE | SCIENCE REFERENCE 14
