RECORD	para 227	14. Reading an upper-atmosphere profile without fooling yourself
RECORD	para 228	Suppose a simulation asks for temperature, density, and composition at 150 km. A common failure is to take a single textbook layer diagram and assign one constant value across the thermosphere. That loses several important controls.
RECORD	A better sequence	A better sequence
RECORD	Check Question to ask	Check Question to ask
DEFINE	para 233	Units Are altitude, temperature, pressure, and density units consistent? Is temperature kelvin or Celsius?
DEFINE	para 234	Layer limits Are named boundaries being treated as approximate ranges rather than physical walls?
RECORD	para 235	Solar forcing Does the model assume average conditions, solar minimum, solar maximum, or an observed storm?
DEFINE	para 236	Composition Is mean molecular mass allowed to change with altitude? Are species mixing ratios applicable in this regime?
DEFINE	para 237	Temperature meaning Is temperature kinetic temperature, and does the model separately represent radiation heat loads?
RECORD	para 238	Space transition Does the model need fluid flow, plasma dynamics, or collisionless particle trajectories?
RECORD	para 239	Evidence Can each data profile be traced to its source, epoch, coordinate system, and known limitations?
ALLOW	para 240	Practical principle: choose model complexity from the mechanism being simulated. A sky gradient may need only an atmospheric optical model; satellite drag needs thermospheric mass density; radio transmission needs electron density and propagation physics; atmospheric escape needs kinetic and/or plasma processes.
RECORD	EARTH'S UPPER ATMOSPHERE | SCIENCE REFERENCE 16	EARTH'S UPPER ATMOSPHERE | SCIENCE REFERENCE 16
RECORD	para 244	Ablation Loss of material from an incoming particle as it heats, melts, vaporizes, or chemically reacts during atmospheric entry.
RECORD	para 245	Airglow Emission of light from atmospheric atoms/molecules excited by sunlight, chemistry, or particle energy.
RECORD	para 246	Exobase Approximate level where the mean free path becomes comparable to a characteristic atmospheric scale height; above it, collisions become too infrequent for ordinary local-fluid assumptions.
RECORD	para 247	Exosphere Outermost conventional layer where particle trajectories and rare collisions become central to the physical description.
RECORD	para 248	Geocorona Extended cloud of hydrogen around Earth that scatters solar ultraviolet light.
RECORD	para 249	Homosphere / heterosphere Lower region with relatively well-mixed major gases versus higher region where molecular diffusion causes stronger species separation.
RECORD	para 250	Ionosphere Region with enough free electrons and ions to influence radio signals and electrodynamics; overlaps neutral-atmosphere layers.
RECORD	para 251	Joule heating Conversion of electrical energy into thermal energy through currents and ion-neutral friction.
RECORD	para 252	Kinetic temperature Measure of the statistical translational kinetic energy of particles; does not by itself indicate heat content or heat-transfer rate.
RECORD	para 253	Scale height Characteristic vertical distance for exponential decline in a simple atmosphere; depends on temperature, gravity, and particle mass.
WHEN	para 254	Thermospheric expansion Increase in scale height and density at a fixed high altitude when energy input warms the upper atmosphere.
RECORD	para 255	Turbopause / homopause Approximate transition above which molecular diffusion becomes more important than turbulent mixing for atmospheric composition.
RECORD	para 256	Knudsen number Ratio of mean free path to a characteristic length scale; helps determine whether a continuum-fluid approximation applies.
RECORD	A note on altitude ranges	A note on altitude ranges
ALLOW	para 258	All altitude ranges in this document are rounded educational references. A named boundary may be defined by a temperature minimum, composition transition, ionization profile, or convention. Those are different criteria, so their values do not line up precisely and can move with conditions.
RECORD	EARTH'S UPPER ATMOSPHERE | SCIENCE REFERENCE 17	EARTH'S UPPER ATMOSPHERE | SCIENCE REFERENCE 17
