WHEN	Core idea	Core idea Water moves when there is a usable driving force and a connected path. In a large pipe, that driving force is often a pressure or elevation difference. In a dry porous material, surface tension and wetting can create capillary pressure that pulls water into tiny pores even when the applied pressure is small.
DEFINE	para 14	Designed as a practical physics reference rather than a specialist textbook. Equations are simplified models; real materials require measured or fitted parameters.
RECORD	Fluidics, Pressure, and Water Ingress - real-world porous-material model 2	Fluidics, Pressure, and Water Ingress - real-world porous-material model 2
RECORD	Contents and model roadmap	Contents and model roadmap
RECORD	para 17	1. The physical picture - Pressure, elevation, viscosity, resistance, and what "fluidics" means inside a material.
RECORD	para 18	2. Pressure in the real world - Hydrostatic pressure, gauge vs absolute pressure, hydraulic head, and pressure gradients.
RECORD	para 19	3. How water enters a material - Connected pores, cracks, wetting, capillarity, sorption, vapor, and forced entry.
RECORD	para 20	4. Capillary pressure - Young-Laplace, contact angle, pore radius, capillary entry pressure, and capillary rise.
RECORD	para 21	5. Flow after entry - Darcy law, permeability, hydraulic conductivity, and why pore connectivity matters.
RECORD	para 22	6. Unsaturated materials - Water content, saturation, matric suction, Richards-type modeling, and hysteresis.
RECORD	para 23	7. Real material examples - Concrete/brick, wood, soil, paper/fabric, and cracked materials.
RECORD	para 24	8. Worked numerical model - Four simple calculations you can reproduce by hand or in code.
RECORD	para 25	9. A practical simulation recipe - State variables, boundary conditions, timestep updates, calibration, and failure modes.
RECORD	para 26	10. Scientific references - Primary papers, reviews, and deeper reading with clickable DOI or article links.
RECORD	One equation to keep in your head	One equation to keep in your head
RECORD	para 28	Hydraulic head: H = p/(rho g) + z
RECORD	para 29	This lets pressure and elevation be treated as parts of one driving potential. For saturated porous flow, a common macroscopic model is q = -K grad(H). For a dry or partially saturated material, an additional capillary or matric-pressure description is needed.
RECORD	para 31	pressure + gravity viscous losses set by pipe geometry
RECORD	para 33	pressure + gravity + capillary forces pore network matters
RECORD	para 35	large connected path very high effective permeability
RECORD	para 36	The same outside water pressure can produce very different ingress rates because geometry and connectivity change the resistance.
RECORD	Fluidics, Pressure, and Water Ingress - real-world porous-material model 3	Fluidics, Pressure, and Water Ingress - real-world porous-material model 3
RECORD	para 38	1. The physical picture
WHEN	para 39	"Fluidics" is the control and movement of fluids. At everyday scale it includes water in pipes, pumps, tanks, hoses, valves, and channels. When the fluid meets a porous material, the same conservation laws still apply, but the path becomes a huge network of microscopic channels.
RECORD	Think of a material as a hidden plumbing network	Think of a material as a hidden plumbing network
RECORD	para 41	A sponge, brick, soil bed, paper sheet, wood cell structure, or concrete paste can be represented as a connected set of voids. Some voids are large; some are tiny. Some connect all the way through; some terminate. The flow rate is therefore controlled by both driving force and hydraulic resistance.
RECORD	Quantity Meaning Typical role	Quantity Meaning Typical role
RECORD	para 43	p fluid pressure pushes fluid from high pressure toward low pressure
RECORD	para 44	z elevation gravity changes pressure with height
RECORD	para 45	rho density sets hydrostatic pressure and inertia
RECORD	para 46	mu dynamic viscosity sets viscous resistance
RECORD	para 47	k intrinsic permeability how easily the material transmits fluid
RECORD	para 48	n porosity how much void space exists
RECORD	S saturation fraction of accessible pore volume filled by water	S saturation fraction of accessible pore volume filled by water
RECORD	para 50	theta contact angle describes wetting of a solid by the liquid
RECORD	para 51	r effective pore radius sets capillary pressure scale
RECORD	para 52	Important: porosity alone does not tell you how fast water moves. Two materials can have similar void volume but very different permeability because their pores differ in size, tortuosity, connectivity, and throat geometry.
RECORD	Fluidics, Pressure, and Water Ingress - real-world porous-material model 4	Fluidics, Pressure, and Water Ingress - real-world porous-material model 4
RECORD	para 54	2. Pressure in the real world
DEFINE	para 55	Pressure is force per area. In a still column of water, pressure increases with depth because the water above has weight. Relative to a free surface exposed to atmosphere, gauge pressure is approximately:
RECORD	para 56	p_g = rho g h
DEFINE	para 57	For water near room temperature, rho is about 1000 kg/m^3. A 1 m water column therefore corresponds to about 9.8 kPa of gauge pressure. Ten meters corresponds to about 98 kPa.
RECORD	Hydrostatic pressure p(z) = p_surface + rho g (z_surface - z)	Hydrostatic pressure p(z) = p_surface + rho g (z_surface - z)
RECORD	para 59	deeper points have larger liquid pressure in a static column
RECORD	Hydraulic head H = p/(rho g) + z	Hydraulic head H = p/(rho g) + z
RECORD	para 61	pressure head + elevation head; useful for groundwater and porous flow
DEFINE	Pressure gradient is what matters for flow	Pressure gradient is what matters for flow
WHEN	para 63	A reservoir can have high pressure and still produce little flow if the downstream boundary has nearly the same pressure, or if the material resistance is enormous. Flow responds to the spatial gradient in the fluid potential, not to a single pressure number in isolation.
RECORD	para 64	For a one-dimensional saturated material, a useful model is:
RECORD	para 65	q = -(k/mu) (dp/dx - rho g)
DEFINE	para 66	where q is Darcy flux (volumetric flow per cross-sectional area). If the material is horizontal, the gravity term drops out. In vertical flow, gravity either helps or opposes the pressure-driven component.
RECORD	Gauge vs absolute pressure	Gauge vs absolute pressure
RECORD	para 68	Gauge pressure uses the local atmosphere as the reference. Absolute pressure includes atmospheric pressure. For water infiltration problems, using gauge pressure is often convenient as long as every boundary is expressed using the same reference.
RECORD	Fluidics, Pressure, and Water Ingress - real-world porous-material model 5	Fluidics, Pressure, and Water Ingress - real-world porous-material model 5
RECORD	para 70	3. How water actually enters a material
RECORD	para 71	Pore-scale picture: why water can enter a dry material
RECORD	para 72	wetting liquid capillary entry
RECORD	para 73	pressure jump across curved meniscus
RECORD	Pc = 2 gamma cos(theta) / r	Pc = 2 gamma cos(theta) / r
RECORD	para 75	smaller pore -> larger capillary pressure
RECORD	para 76	better wetting -> stronger spontaneous entry non-wetting case
ALLOW	para 78	external pressure may be needed
RECORD	para 79	to force invasion.
DEFINE	para 80	There are several distinct routes. They can happen simultaneously.
RECORD	Mechanism What physically happens Main controls	Mechanism What physically happens Main controls
DEFINE	para 82	Capillary imbibition A wetting liquid is pulled into small connected pores by curved liquid-air interfaces.contact angle, surface tension, pore radius, viscosity, pore network
RECORD	para 83	Pressure-driven permeation An imposed pressure difference pushes water through connected pores or cracks.pressure gradient, permeability, viscosity, thickness
RECORD	para 84	Gravity-driven infiltration Water moves downward because elevation contributes to hydraulic head.height, saturation, hydraulic conductivity
RECORD	para 85	Surface wetting + spreading A droplet spreads over a surface and finds pore openings. surface energy, roughness, contact angle
RECORD	para 86	Sorption / molecular uptake Water molecules bind or diffuse into hydrophilic solid phases or nanoscale spaces.chemical affinity, humidity, temperature, diffusivity
ALLOW	para 87	Vapor transport Water vapor diffuses through a material and may adsorb, desorb, or condense inside pores.vapor-pressure gradient, permeability, temperature
RECORD	para 88	Cracks / joints / defects Large connected paths bypass much of the fine-pore resistance.crack width, connectivity, wetting, pressure
WHEN	para 89	The practical rule: water enters when the combination of pressure, gravity, capillary suction, and wetting forces can move the liquid across the material surface and into an interconnected pore path. The rate is then limited by hydraulic resistance, air displacement, changing saturation, and geometry.
RECORD	Fluidics, Pressure, and Water Ingress - real-world porous-material model 6	Fluidics, Pressure, and Water Ingress - real-world porous-material model 6
RECORD	para 91	4. Capillary pressure: the "hidden pump" inside small pores
RECORD	para 92	A curved water-air interface carries a pressure jump. For an idealized cylindrical pore, the Young-Laplace relation becomes:
RECORD	Pc = p_air - p_water = 2 gamma cos(theta) / r	Pc = p_air - p_water = 2 gamma cos(theta) / r
RECORD	para 94	Here gamma is surface tension, theta is the contact angle measured through the liquid, and r is an effective pore radius. For a wetting liquid with theta < 90 degrees, the liquid pressure can be lower than the air pressure while the liquid is still pulled into the pore. This is the physical basis of capillary suction.
WHEN	para 96	If capillary suction balances the weight of a vertical liquid column, an idealized capillary rise is:
RECORD	para 97	h_cap = 2 gamma cos(theta) / (rho g r)
DEFINE	para 98	This reveals an unintuitive fact: a smaller pore can generate a larger pressure difference. That is why tiny pores can pull water upward or laterally through porous solids.
RECORD	Entry pressure and non-wetting liquids	Entry pressure and non-wetting liquids
RECORD	para 100	For a non-wetting liquid (theta > 90 degrees), spontaneous entry is unfavorable in an ideal pore. An externally imposed pressure can overcome the capillary barrier. In real porous materials, the actual threshold depends on pore-throat geometry, surface heterogeneity, roughness, trapped gas, and wetting hysteresis.
RECORD	Capillary pressure scale Pc ~ 2 gamma |cos(theta)| / r	Capillary pressure scale Pc ~ 2 gamma |cos(theta)| / r
RECORD	para 102	useful estimate for the pressure needed to overcome a pore-throat barrier
RECORD	para 103	Example: a 10 micrometer pore
RECORD	para 104	Taking gamma = 0.072 N/m, theta = 0 degrees, and r = 10 micrometers gives Pc about 14.4 kPa. That is equivalent to roughly 1.47 m of water head. So a tiny wetting pore can develop a capillary pressure comparable to a substantial water column.
RECORD	Fluidics, Pressure, and Water Ingress - real-world porous-material model 7	Fluidics, Pressure, and Water Ingress - real-world porous-material model 7
DEFINE	para 106	5. Flow after water is inside: Darcy's law
RECORD	para 107	Once a connected region of water exists, a porous material can often be modeled macroscopically as a hydraulic resistor. For saturated, single-phase flow: q = -(k/mu) (grad p - rho g)
RECORD	para 108	In 1D horizontal form: q = -(k/mu) dp/dx.
DEFINE	para 109	The intrinsic permeability k has units of square meters and is a property of the material-fluid geometry. Hydraulic conductivity K is often used in hydrology and folds in fluid density, gravity, and viscosity:
RECORD	K = k rho g / mu	K = k rho g / mu
DEFINE	para 111	Then q = -K grad(H), where H is hydraulic head.
RECORD	Why a crack changes everything	Why a crack changes everything
ALLOW	para 113	Permeability is highly sensitive to the size and connectivity of flow paths. A millimeter-scale connected crack can carry water much more readily than a dense fine-pore matrix because the hydraulic resistance of a channel falls steeply as its characteristic size increases. A material may therefore be "waterproof" through its intact matrix while leaking badly through one connected defect.
RECORD	para 114	For a flat slab with area A and thickness L, the saturated estimate is:
RECORD	Q = (k A / mu) (Delta p / L)	Q = (k A / mu) (Delta p / L)
DEFINE	para 116	This is a first-order real-world engineering model, not a universal law for every porous medium. It assumes steady, single-phase, saturated flow with effective constant properties.
RECORD	Fluidics, Pressure, and Water Ingress - real-world porous-material model 8	Fluidics, Pressure, and Water Ingress - real-world porous-material model 8
RECORD	para 125	6. Unsaturated materials: pressure becomes a state variable
RECORD	para 126	A dry material is not simply a saturated material with less water. As water content falls, air occupies more of the pore network, water films get thinner, and the set of pores carrying liquid changes. Consequently, the effective hydraulic conductivity can drop by orders of magnitude.
RECORD	para 127	A common macroscopic state description uses water saturation S, water pressure p_w, and a retention relation connecting saturation to capillary pressure. The broad structure is:
RECORD	para 128	Pc = p_air - p_water; S = S(Pc)
RECORD	para 129	and then an unsaturated conductivity K(S) is supplied by a fitted constitutive relation. The well-known van Genuchten model provides a closed-form water-retention relation and can be combined with conductivity models such as Mualem's.
RECORD	Wetting front in a material	Wetting front in a material
RECORD	para 131	wet front x(t)
RECORD	I(t) = S sqrt(t) + A0	I(t) = S sqrt(t) + A0
RECORD	para 133	common early-time form for water absorption
RECORD	para 134	S = sorptivity; A0 = initial offset
DEFINE	para 135	A sharp visible front is an approximation. Real materials have dispersed pore sizes, dead ends, cracks, and air entrapment.
RECORD	Richards-type model	Richards-type model
RECORD	para 137	A standard continuum model for partially saturated flow combines water conservation with a constitutive relation for pressure, saturation, and conductivity. The exact form depends on chosen variables, but conceptually it is:
RECORD	para 138	storage change = divergence of [conductivity x hydraulic-head gradient]
DEFINE	para 139	This is the natural next step after Darcy: Darcy describes the flow law, while the unsaturated model adds the fact that saturation and conductivity change as water content changes.
RECORD	para 141	The path by which a material becomes wet can differ from the path by which it dries. Pore-throat geometry and trapped non-wetting fluid can cause different pressure-saturation relationships during drainage and imbibition. Do not assume one curve works equally well in both directions.
RECORD	Fluidics, Pressure, and Water Ingress - real-world porous-material model 9	Fluidics, Pressure, and Water Ingress - real-world porous-material model 9
RECORD	para 143	7. Real materials: what the model predicts
RECORD	Concrete and brick	Concrete and brick
RECORD	para 145	These materials contain interconnected capillary pores at several scales. Dry concrete or brick can absorb water from a wet face even without a large external pressure because capillary forces drive early uptake. Sorptivity is commonly used to quantify the rate of capillary absorption. Initial moisture condition, pore structure, compaction, and mix composition all affect the result.
RECORD	para 147	Wood is anisotropic. Water can move through cell lumina and pits as capillary water and can also diffuse into cell walls. Liquid transport is often much faster along the grain than across it because the dominant connected pathways are oriented longitudinally. Thus a single isotropic permeability number can be a poor model for wood.
ALLOW	para 149	Soil is a classic unsaturated porous medium. Rain or irrigation first encounters a surface that may be dry. Water infiltrates under a combination of gravity and capillary forces, while air is displaced. Hydraulic conductivity depends strongly on water content. Fine-textured soils can hold strong matric suction but may have low conductivity when very dry.
RECORD	Paper, cloth, and fibrous materials	Paper, cloth, and fibrous materials
RECORD	para 151	Fibers form irregular pore networks. At low saturation, liquid can travel in thin films and bridges between fibers; at higher saturation, larger connected flow paths develop. The classical Lucas-Washburn picture captures the square-root-of-time scaling in idealized pores, while real fibrous structures can show more complicated transport.
RECORD	Cracks, joints, fasteners, and interfaces	Cracks, joints, fasteners, and interfaces
ALLOW	para 153	A defect can dominate total leakage. The matrix may have low k, but a connected crack, joint, or interface can provide a much lower-resistance path. For practical modeling, treat these as separate hydraulic domains rather than averaging them blindly into one bulk material property.
RECORD	Fluidics, Pressure, and Water Ingress - real-world porous-material model 10	Fluidics, Pressure, and Water Ingress - real-world porous-material model 10
RECORD	para 155	8. Worked numerical model
DEFINE	para 156	These examples are deliberately simple. Their purpose is to show how the parameters map onto physical behavior.
RECORD	Example 1 - water pressure at depth	Example 1 - water pressure at depth
RECORD	para 158	At h = 1.0 m, using rho = 1000 kg/m^3 and g = 9.81 m/s^2:
RECORD	para 159	p_g = rho g h = 1000 x 9.81 x 1.0 = 9.81 kPa
RECORD	para 160	So a one-meter water column adds about 9.8 kPa of gauge pressure at its base.
RECORD	Example 2 - capillary suction in a 10 micrometer pore	Example 2 - capillary suction in a 10 micrometer pore
RECORD	para 162	For gamma = 0.072 N/m, theta = 0 degrees, r = 10e-6 m:
RECORD	para 163	Pc = 2 gamma/r = 14,400 Pa = 14.4 kPa
RECORD	para 164	Equivalent water head = Pc/(rho g) about 1.47 m.
RECORD	Example 3 - pressure-driven flow through a slab	Example 3 - pressure-driven flow through a slab
RECORD	para 166	Suppose a slab has k = 1e-16 m^2, A = 0.01 m^2, L = 0.10 m, Delta p = 10 kPa, and water viscosity mu = 1e-3 Pa s. Then:
RECORD	para 167	Q = (k A / mu)(Delta p/L) = 1.0e-10 m^3/s = 0.36 mL/hour
DEFINE	para 168	That tiny flow is why a low-permeability intact matrix can appear almost dry despite a large water reservoir pressing on one side.
RECORD	Example 4 - spontaneous capillary penetration	Example 4 - spontaneous capillary penetration
RECORD	para 170	For an ideal 10 micrometer cylindrical pore with water, theta = 0 degrees, and t = 1 s, the Lucas-Washburn estimate gives:
RECORD	L^2 = gamma r cos(theta) t / (2 mu) -> L about 19 mm	L^2 = gamma r cos(theta) t / (2 mu) -> L about 19 mm
RECORD	para 172	Real porous materials usually deviate because pores vary in size, paths branch, air can become trapped, and the effective flow geometry changes while the material wets.
RECORD	Fluidics, Pressure, and Water Ingress - real-world porous-material model 11	Fluidics, Pressure, and Water Ingress - real-world porous-material model 11
RECORD	para 174	9. A practical simulation recipe
RECORD	para 175	A useful fluid-ingress model can be built from a small set of state variables. Start simple, then add complexity only when the data demand it.
RECORD	Variable Meaning Use in a timestep	Variable Meaning Use in a timestep
RECORD	para 178	p_w water pressure driving force
RECORD	para 179	z elevation gravity contribution
DEFINE	S water saturation controls which pores are water-filled	S water saturation controls which pores are water-filled
RECORD	para 181	k permeability resistance to saturated flow
RECORD	K(S) effective hydraulic conductivity resistance under partial saturation	K(S) effective hydraulic conductivity resistance under partial saturation
RECORD	Pc(S) capillary pressure links pore occupancy to pressure	Pc(S) capillary pressure links pore occupancy to pressure
RECORD	para 184	r_eff effective pore/throat scale entry-pressure estimate
RECORD	para 185	theta wetting/contact behavior capillary sign and magnitude
RECORD	para 186	x_f wetting-front position simple sharp-front approximation
RECORD	T temperature changes viscosity and surface tension	T temperature changes viscosity and surface tension
RECORD	A simple timestep	A simple timestep
RECORD	para 191	for each timestep: boundary = get_boundary_conditions(t) for each material cell:
RECORD	Pc = retention_curve(S)	Pc = retention_curve(S)
RECORD	Keff = conductivity_curve(S)	Keff = conductivity_curve(S)
RECORD	H = p/(rho*g) + z	H = p/(rho*g) + z flux = -Keff * grad(H)
RECORD	S = update_by_mass_balance(S, flux, dt)	S = update_by_mass_balance(S, flux, dt) apply_sources_sinks_and_drainage()
RECORD	para 197	Measure or estimate parameters in this order: geometry -> density/viscosity -> permeability or hydraulic conductivity -> water-retention curve -> wetting/contact behavior -> defect pathways. This prevents one "mystery permeability" value from being forced to compensate for incorrect geometry or missing capillary physics.
WHEN	When the simple model fails	When the simple model fails
WHEN	para 199	Upgrade to a pore-network model, multiphase formulation, fracture domain, or coupled heat/vapor model when you see strong hysteresis, trapped air, phase change, preferential flow, swelling/shrinkage, chemical reactions, or flow that is visibly concentrated in a small number of channels.
RECORD	Fluidics, Pressure, and Water Ingress - real-world porous-material model 12	Fluidics, Pressure, and Water Ingress - real-world porous-material model 12
