RECORD	Parameter checklist for a real-world model	Parameter checklist for a real-world model
RECORD	para 241	• Fluid: density rho, viscosity mu, surface tension gamma.
RECORD	para 242	• Boundary: water pressure or water level, outlet pressure, temperature, exposure duration.
RECORD	para 243	• Material: porosity n, permeability k or hydraulic conductivity K, effective pore-size scale, tortuosity/connectivity.
RECORD	para 244	• Wetting: contact angle or a fitted capillary-pressure relation.
DEFINE	para 245	• State: initial saturation / moisture content and whether the material is drying, wetting, or cycling.
RECORD	para 246	• Defects: cracks, joints, edges, interfaces, holes, fasteners, and preferential pathways.
RECORD	para 247	Bottom line: a material does not have one universal "water pressure". Its response is the result of pressure and gravity acting on a pore network whose hydraulic resistance changes with saturation, while capillary forces can actively pull water into wetting pores. A good model therefore keeps pressure, geometry, saturation, and wetting as separate variables.
RECORD	FOG, MIST, FOAM	FOG, MIST, FOAM
RECORD	AND AEROSOL DISPERSION	AND AEROSOL DISPERSION
RECORD	How water moves between surfaces, air, and visible suspended droplets	How water moves between surfaces, air, and visible suspended droplets
RECORD	para 4	Example: warm moist air crosses a colder lake or coastal current
RECORD	para 5	Surface cooling lowers saturation vapor pressure; air reaches saturation and droplets grow on aerosol particles.
RECORD	para 6	warm, moist air advects cold water
WHEN	Water vapor can also be added	Water vapor can also be added when cold air passes over warmer water.
RECORD	A physical guide to near-surface clouds and water-atmosphere exchange	A physical guide to near-surface clouds and water-atmosphere exchange
RECORD	para 10	This guide connects the thermodynamics of fog to the fluid mechanics of spray and foam. It explains why fog forms, why it can cling to the ground or water, how wind and mixing disperse it, and how breaking waves can turn liquid water into airborne aerosol particles. Emphasis is placed on mechanism rather than visual label alone.
RECORD	para 11	Core distinction: fog is a suspension of tiny liquid droplets (or, in ice fog, ice particles) in air. Foam is a collection of gas bubbles enclosed by liquid films. Spray is liquid droplets launched into air. These can interact, but they are not the same physical state.
RECORD	para 12	Includes ground fog, lake and sea fog, steam fog, cold-water advection fog, sea foam and bubble-burst aerosols, waterfalls and surf mist, visibility physics, dissipation, field diagnosis, a compact modeling framework, and research links.
DEFINE	Scientific reference guide | Standard-font edition | Research links are clickabl	Scientific reference guide | Standard-font edition | Research links are clickable
RECORD	FOG, MIST, FOAM & AEROSOL DISPERSION	FOG, MIST, FOAM & AEROSOL DISPERSION 2
RECORD	para 15	1. The system at a glance
RECORD	para 16	Visible fog and mist are outcomes of two related but different pathways: phase change (water vapor condenses into droplets) and mechanical atomization (liquid water is broken into airborne droplets). A third pathway, bubble bursting, couples foam to aerosol generation. All three are shaped by humidity, temperature, turbulence, and droplet-size distributions. [1-5]
RECORD	Topic What it answers	Topic What it answers
WHEN	para 18	Water vapor to fog When does invisible vapor become visible liquid?
RECORD	para 19	Ground and valley fog Why does fog pool in low places and hug the surface?
RECORD	para 20	Water-body interactions Why does a lake or ocean make fog in one situation and steam fog in another?
RECORD	para 21	Foam and spray How do waves and bubbles eject liquid droplets into the atmosphere?
RECORD	para 22	Dispersion and dissipation What removes fog: warming, mixing, wind, deposition, or evaporation?
