RECORD	para 25	1.1 Terminology that prevents category errors
RECORD	Term Physical meaning	Term Physical meaning
RECORD	para 27	Water vapor Individual water molecules in the gas phase; invisible by itself at ordinary atmospheric concentrations.
DEFINE	para 28	Aerosol Solid or liquid particles suspended in a gas. It is a broad category, not a synonym for smoke.
RECORD	para 29	Fog Near-surface cloud droplets or ice particles that reduce horizontal visibility to less than about 1 km under the common meteorological convention. [1,2]
RECORD	para 30	Mist A less visibility-reducing suspension of droplets; exact operational thresholds vary among services and contexts.
RECORD	para 31	Spray Liquid droplets mechanically produced by waves, splashing, jets, waterfalls, fountains, or other breakup of liquid.
RECORD	para 32	Foam Gas bubbles dispersed through a liquid. Surface-active compounds can stabilize the thin liquid films between bubbles.
RECORD	para 33	Haze Visibility reduction dominated by dry or weakly hydrated aerosol particles, often with pollution, dust, smoke, or salt.
DEFINE	para 34	Cloud Suspended droplets and/or ice crystals aloft; fog is often described as a cloud with its base at the surface.
ALLOW	para 35	Important: you cannot infer the formation mechanism from a pale, white appearance alone. A white plume over water may be condensed fog droplets, mechanically generated spray, or both. A “steam fog” plume is usually condensed water droplets, not hot gaseous steam.
RECORD	FOG, MIST, FOAM & AEROSOL DISPERSION	FOG, MIST, FOAM & AEROSOL DISPERSION 3
RECORD	para 37	2. How invisible vapor becomes visible fog
RECORD	para 39	2.1 Relative humidity and dew point
ALLOW	para 41	Field reading: if air temperature is only 0-2 C above dew point, fog may be close, but it is not guaranteed. Wind, vertical mixing, aerosols, surface temperature, and whether the air is actually cooling or moistening determine the outcome.
RECORD	para 42	2.2 Why aerosols matter
RECORD	para 44	2.3 Condensation versus atomization
DEFINE	Pathway How droplets are made Typical clue	Pathway How droplets are made Typical clue
RECORD	Condensation Water vapor transfers to liquid on aerosol nuclei as air reaches	Condensation Water vapor transfers to liquid on aerosol nuclei as air reaches saturation. Cloud/fog boundary follows air temperature, dew point, and mixing.
ALLOW	para 47	Atomization Liquid is stretched, splashed, or torn into droplets by turbulence, jets, impact, or wave breaking. Droplets can be present even when RH is below 100%; then they may evaporate.
RECORD	para 48	Bubble-burst aerosol A bubble rises through water and ruptures; film fragments and a recoiling cavity/jet eject droplets. Strongly associated with whitecaps, surf, and aerated water; chemistry reflects the source water.
RECORD	para 49	The two pathways can follow one another: spray droplets can partially evaporate, leaving salt or organic residues; those residual particles can later take up water and act as cloud-condensation nuclei. Conversely, fog droplets evaporate during drying and leave any nonvolatile material behind as aerosol. [4,7,8]
RECORD	FOG, MIST, FOAM & AEROSOL DISPERSION	FOG, MIST, FOAM & AEROSOL DISPERSION 4
RECORD	para 51	3. Ground fog, radiation fog, and valley fog
RECORD	Cold, sheltered basin	Cold, sheltered basin
RECORD	Clear sky + light wind -> ground loses heat -> shallow saturated layer	Clear sky + light wind -> ground loses heat -> shallow saturated layer
RECORD	para 55	near-surface fog
RECORD	Cold air drains downslope and collects in low terrain	Cold air drains downslope and collects in low terrain
WHEN	para 57	Figure 1. In a sheltered basin, cooled dense air can drain downslope and collect in low terrain. If the near-ground layer reaches saturation, fog can form as a shallow pool and deepen upward as cooling continues.
RECORD	para 58	3.1 A typical overnight sequence
RECORD	para 59	• Evening: the surface begins losing stored heat. Moist soil, wetlands, irrigation, lakes, and vegetation can keep the near-surface layer humid.
ALLOW	para 60	• Late night: the surface and adjacent air cool. If temperature reaches dew point, droplets form around nuclei. A light breeze may help mix moisture into a shallow layer; stronger wind can mix in warmer or drier air and prevent a dense layer.
ALLOW	para 61	• Predawn: continued cooling can increase liquid water content and deepen fog. In valleys, cold-air drainage may reinforce pooling; a temperature inversion can cap vertical mixing.
WHEN	para 62	• After sunrise: surface heating raises air temperature and saturation vapor pressure. Droplets evaporate if the air becomes subsaturated, though thick fog can persist where sunlight is weak or moisture supply remains strong.
RECORD	para 63	3.2 Why fog likes low places
ALLOW	para 64	Cold air is denser than warmer air at similar pressure and tends to flow downslope under gravity. Valleys can therefore accumulate a humid, cool layer while ridges remain clear. Topography also reduces ventilation and can shelter the surface from stronger winds. Yet not every valley fog is purely radiative: moisture advection, evaporation, precipitation, and slope lifting may contribute. [2,3]
DEFINE	para 65	3.3 Dew, frost, fog, and wet vegetation are different
WHEN	para 66	Dew is liquid water deposited directly onto a surface when that surface cools below the local dew point. Frost forms as ice deposition or freezing under appropriate conditions. Fog consists of airborne droplets or ice particles. A foggy landscape can collect additional liquid by interception on leaves, needles, fences, and buildings, but that surface water is not itself airborne fog.
ALLOW	para 67	Ground-fog diagnostic: inspect vertical depth, wind, cloud cover, surface wetness, valley position, and the temperature-dew-point spread. A thin fog layer that leaves treetops clear suggests a shallow near-surface process; fog attached to a slope may instead indicate upslope flow or advection.
RECORD	FOG, MIST, FOAM & AEROSOL DISPERSION	FOG, MIST, FOAM & AEROSOL DISPERSION 5
RECORD	para 69	4. Fog in relation to lakes, rivers, and oceans
RECORD	para 76	Figure 2. Two competing water-body effects: cold water cools advected air toward saturation; relatively warm water can supply vapor to colder air, producing an evaporation or “steam fog” plume.
RECORD	Process Required contrast Typical setting / appearance	Process Required contrast Typical setting / appearance
RECORD	Advection fog over cold	Advection fog over cold water Warm, moist air flows over colder water and cools to saturation. Cold coastal currents, springtime lakes, air moving over cold upwelled ocean water; broad low cloud-like bank.
RECORD	para 79	Steam / evaporation fog Cold air passes over water warmer than the air; evaporation moistens the lowest air until it saturates.
DEFINE	Autumn lakes, rivers, reservoirs, or open water during a cold-air	Autumn lakes, rivers, reservoirs, or open water during a cold-air outbreak; wisps often stream downwind. Despite the name, the visible plume is mostly droplets, not vapor.
DEFINE	Radiation fog over water Surface and adjacent air cool enough overnight	Radiation fog over water Surface and adjacent air cool enough overnight to reach saturation; wind is generally light. Calm lakes, bays, sheltered harbors, and wetland pools; often shallow and patchy.
DEFINE	Lake / sea fog moving	Lake / sea fog moving ashore Fog formed over water is transported inland by low-level wind. Coastal roads, estuaries, harbors, shorelines; can persist while cold water maintains the layer.
ALLOW	Precipitation / frontal fog Rain evaporates into cooler air, raising vapor	Precipitation / frontal fog Rain evaporates into cooler air, raising vapor content toward saturation; cooling may also contribute. Near fronts, persistent rain, and shallow cool air; fog can develop even without clear nighttime skies.
RECORD	Upslope fog Moist stable air rises along terrain and cools as	Upslope fog Moist stable air rises along terrain and cools as pressure decreases. Hills around lakes, coastal ranges, and mountain slopes; often merges with low stratus.
RECORD	para 85	4.1 The key temperature difference
RECORD	para 87	4.2 Why big lakes behave differently from small ponds
RECORD	FOG, MIST, FOAM & AEROSOL DISPERSION	FOG, MIST, FOAM & AEROSOL DISPERSION 6
RECORD	para 90	4.3 Bodies of water do not always make fog
RECORD	para 93	Wave breaking entrains air; bubbles rise and burst at the surface
DEFINE	para 94	Foam is gas bubbles in liquid. Bubble bursting can eject tiny liquid droplets into the air.
RECORD	para 95	airborne droplets / spray aerosol
ALLOW	Small droplets may evaporate, leaving salt	Small droplets may evaporate, leaving salt and organic residue; some larger droplets fall back.
RECORD	para 97	foam remains on water
RECORD	para 98	Figure 3. Breaking waves entrain air. Bubbles rise, expand or shrink, and burst at the surface. The rupture can launch airborne droplets that become sea-spray aerosol; much of the foam remains in the liquid phase. [8-10]
WHEN	para 99	5.1 What happens when a bubble bursts?
RECORD	para 100	• Bubble formation: breaking waves, waterfalls, turbulence, or splashing drive air into water. Dissolved gases and surfactants influence bubble behavior.
RECORD	para 101	• Bubble rise: buoyancy carries bubbles toward the surface; small bubbles can be transported or trapped by turbulent water.
RECORD	para 102	• Film-cap rupture: the thin bubble cap breaks into fragments, producing film drops. The exact number and size vary strongly with bubble size and surface chemistry.
RECORD	para 103	• Cavity collapse and jetting: after the cap breaks, the cavity recoils and can eject a jet that pinches into additional drops.
ALLOW	para 104	• Atmospheric fate: larger drops may fall back quickly; smaller droplets can be transported by turbulence, evaporate, or retain water and grow if ambient humidity is high.
RECORD	para 105	5.2 Why sea foam can be white or brown
RECORD	para 107	5.3 Spray versus humid air
RECORD	para 109	Modeling caution: do not generate sea fog solely from wind speed. Wind and wave state create spray and bubbles; fog requires a thermodynamic path to saturation. They should be separate but coupled processes.
RECORD	FOG, MIST, FOAM & AEROSOL DISPERSION	FOG, MIST, FOAM & AEROSOL DISPERSION 7
RECORD	para 111	6. Other aerosolized water features
RECORD	Source Main production route How it differs from fog	Source Main production route How it differs from fog
ALLOW	Waterfall / rapids Impact, turbulent breakup, and air entrainment eject	Waterfall / rapids Impact, turbulent breakup, and air entrainment eject droplets. Often localized spray plume; droplets can evaporate downwind. Cooling and saturation may create secondary fog nearby.
RECORD	Breaking surf / whitecaps Bubble bursting plus direct wave fragmentation and	Breaking surf / whitecaps Bubble bursting plus direct wave fragmentation and spume from wind stress. Broad droplet spectrum; large drops return quickly while fine droplets travel farther.
RECORD	Fountains / jets / irrigation A pressurized stream or nozzle breaks into	Fountains / jets / irrigation A pressurized stream or nozzle breaks into droplets; wind transports them. Mechanically produced; can occur in dry air and then evaporate.
RECORD	Rain splash Impact forms crowns, ligaments, and secondary	Rain splash Impact forms crowns, ligaments, and secondary droplets. A local aerosol source during precipitation; evaporation can modify the size and lifetime.
ALLOW	Wet soil / wetlands Water vapor flux plus surface cooling can raise	Wet soil / wetlands Water vapor flux plus surface cooling can raise humidity; vegetation intercepts droplets. May support condensation fog but does not automatically create airborne droplets.
RECORD	Industrial or urban water	Industrial or urban water spray Cooling towers, humidifiers, wash systems, and other sources eject droplets or vapor. Droplet size, dissolved chemistry, and biological content depend on source and controls.
RECORD	para 119	Geysers / hot springs Hot water jets and bubbles can create spray; cooling causes condensation under some conditions.
RECORD	A visible plume can mix mechanical droplets with condensed	A visible plume can mix mechanical droplets with condensed droplets; distinguish liquid spray from vapor.
RECORD	para 121	6.1 Evaporation does not always mean instant disappearance
RECORD	para 123	6.2 Aerosol source chemistry
RECORD	FOG, MIST, FOAM & AEROSOL DISPERSION	FOG, MIST, FOAM & AEROSOL DISPERSION 8
RECORD	para 126	7. Fog dispersion and dissipation
RECORD	para 127	Fog is maintained by a balance of cooling or moisture supply, condensation, turbulent transport, radiation, and droplet loss. Dissipation usually does not mean the droplets “fall out” like rain. More often, the air becomes subsaturated and droplets evaporate, or mixing replaces a foggy parcel with warmer or drier air. [3,5,6]
WHEN	Mechanism What it does When it tends to matter	Mechanism What it does When it tends to matter
RECORD	Solar heating / sensible	Solar heating / sensible heat Warms the surface and lower air; saturation vapor pressure rises and droplets evaporate. Morning radiation fog over land, sunlit shallow fog layers.
RECORD	Entrainment of dry air Turbulence mixes unsaturated air into fog, evaporating	Entrainment of dry air Turbulence mixes unsaturated air into fog, evaporating droplets and reducing liquid water. At fog top, edges, or where shear increases.
ALLOW	Advection Wind transports fog into a new environment that may be	Advection Wind transports fog into a new environment that may be warmer, drier, or differently ventilated. Sea fog moving inland; a valley fog bank crossing terrain.
RECORD	Mechanical mixing Wind or convective turbulence deepens and dilutes the	Mechanical mixing Wind or convective turbulence deepens and dilutes the layer; outcome depends on moisture and temperature of mixed air.
RECORD	Transitions from calm dawn to breezy daytime	Transitions from calm dawn to breezy daytime conditions.
RECORD	para 134	Changing surface fluxes Cooling or vapor supply weakens; the fog can no longer maintain saturation. After winds change or a water-air temperature contrast diminishes.
RECORD	Droplet deposition Droplets impact foliage, structures, or terrain and remove	Droplet deposition Droplets impact foliage, structures, or terrain and remove liquid from the air. Vegetation-rich slopes, cloud forests, and persistent fog zones.
WHEN	para 136	Coalescence / drizzle Droplets collide and combine; if large enough, they may fall as drizzle. Deep, liquid-rich fog and low stratus; can thin fog while wetting surfaces.
RECORD	Freezing / ice processes Supercooled droplets freeze on contact or ice particles	Freezing / ice processes Supercooled droplets freeze on contact or ice particles form in very cold air. Freezing fog or ice fog; hazards and optics differ from liquid fog.
RECORD	para 138	7.1 Why wind can either clear or thicken fog
DEFINE	para 140	7.2 Fog is a moving field, not a solid object
RECORD	para 142	Practical chain: air transport + surface heat and moisture fluxes -> local temperature and vapor -> droplet activation/growth -> liquid water content and droplet sizes -> light extinction/visibility -> evaporation, deposition, or precipitation.
RECORD	FOG, MIST, FOAM & AEROSOL DISPERSION	FOG, MIST, FOAM & AEROSOL DISPERSION 9
RECORD	para 144	8. Droplet size, optics, and visibility
RECORD	para 146	8.1 Visibility and extinction
DEFINE	para 147	In a uniform, approximately homogeneous fog, a useful meteorological approximation is the Koschmieder relation:
RECORD	para 148	Visual range: V ~= 3.912 / beta V is the visibility distance in meters for a 2% contrast threshold, and beta is the atmospheric extinction coefficient in inverse meters. This approximation is useful for intuition and rendering, but real scenes require care with illumination, background contrast, nonuniform density, and multiple scattering.
WHEN	para 149	As droplet number or effective size changes, extinction changes. At visible wavelengths, fog droplets are commonly in a size regime where Mie scattering is important; they are not simply tiny Rayleigh scatterers. For physically consistent models, use a size distribution (number concentration by diameter), not only one average radius, when fine accuracy is needed.
RECORD	para 150	8.2 A qualitative droplet-size map
RECORD	Population Typical behavior Visual / transport consequence	Population Typical behavior Visual / transport consequence
RECORD	para 152	Very small aerosol particles Often invisible individually; can be hygroscopic nuclei. Scatter weakly per particle but can be numerous; can grow at high RH.
DEFINE	Fog and cloud droplets Micron-scale liquid drops, with broad distributions	Fog and cloud droplets Micron-scale liquid drops, with broad distributions that depend on formation history and dynamics. Strong visible scattering collectively; remain suspended when air motion is present.
RECORD	para 154	Fine spray droplets Produced by fragmentation or bubble bursting; some overlap fog sizes. Can be carried downwind and can evaporate to residue.
RECORD	para 155	Large spray drops Produced by strong wave splash, jets, or impact. Higher inertia and settling speed; more likely to fall or strike nearby surfaces.
RECORD	para 156	The boundaries between these categories overlap. Particle origin is not always recoverable from size alone: the same diameter could be a condensed fog droplet or a mechanically generated spray drop. Composition, location, humidity history, and dynamics can help distinguish them.
RECORD	para 157	8.3 White fog, gray fog, and glowing halos
RECORD	FOG, MIST, FOAM & AEROSOL DISPERSION	FOG, MIST, FOAM & AEROSOL DISPERSION 10
RECORD	para 160	9. Fog water, deposition, and environmental effects
WHEN	para 161	Fog is a multiphase chemical environment rather than pure distilled water suspended in air. Droplets can scavenge soluble gases and particles, host aqueous reactions, and deposit material on vegetation, buildings, and soil. When the fog evaporates, some nonvolatile components remain as aerosol. The chemistry depends on air pollution, marine salts, organic matter, and local biological emissions. [4,7]
RECORD	para 162	9.1 Fog deposition and cloud forests
RECORD	para 163	In persistent fog regions, leaves, needles, and branches intercept droplets. The resulting fog drip can add water to ecosystems, particularly where rainfall is limited or seasonal. Deposition depends on droplet size, wind, vegetation roughness, canopy structure, and whether droplets evaporate after collision. Fog frequency alone does not quantify water input; liquid water content, wind, exposure, and collection efficiency also matter.
RECORD	para 164	9.2 Coastal atmosphere and marine aerosol
RECORD	para 166	9.3 Freezing fog and ice fog
RECORD	para 168	Environmental distinction: fog can be a source of water and dissolved substances through deposition, but it can also transport pollutants and salts. Its net ecological effect depends on chemistry, frequency, liquid water content, and the receiving surface.
RECORD	FOG, MIST, FOAM & AEROSOL DISPERSION	FOG, MIST, FOAM & AEROSOL DISPERSION 11
RECORD	para 170	10. A compact physically informed model
RECORD	para 171	A useful model need not resolve every microscopic droplet. It should, however, preserve the separation between thermodynamic fog and mechanical spray, and it should evolve moisture, temperature, transport, and visible extinction consistently. The following structure is a hierarchy from a minimum viable simulation to a higher-fidelity model.
RECORD	Layer State variables / processes Minimum useful behavior	Layer State variables / processes Minimum useful behavior
RECORD	Atmosphere Air temperature T, vapor mixing ratio qv or relative	Atmosphere Air temperature T, vapor mixing ratio qv or relative humidity, pressure, wind, turbulent mixing. Advect fields; compute saturation vapor pressure and saturation deficit.
RECORD	para 174	Surface boundary Surface temperature, water temperature, moisture flux, roughness, terrain exposure. Cool or moisten near-surface air; vary lake/sea and land independently.
DEFINE	Condensation fog Droplet number N, liquid water content LWC, effective	Condensation fog Droplet number N, liquid water content LWC, effective radius or size bins. When saturation is reached, activate nuclei and transfer vapor to liquid; evaporate when subsaturated.
RECORD	para 176	Spray / bubble aerosol Source flux from waves, waterfalls, jets, bubbles; drop-size distribution and source chemistry. Spawn droplets from mechanical breakup independent of saturation; transport, evaporate, deposit.
RECORD	para 177	Foam surface state Bubble coverage, characteristic bubble size, drainage/rupture rate, surface-active material proxy. Keep foam on the liquid surface; generate aerosol only through an explicit burst/spray mechanism.
RECORD	Radiative optics Extinction, scattering albedo, phase function, spectral	Radiative optics Extinction, scattering albedo, phase function, spectral dependence. Compute view-dependent contrast loss and light beams from droplet/aerosol fields.
RECORD	para 179	Sinks / phase change Evaporation, deposition to land/water/vegetation, freezing, drizzle/coalescence. Conserve water approximately between vapor, droplets, surface storage, and precipitation.
RECORD	para 180	10.1 Simplified saturation logic
RECORD	para 181	• Compute saturation vapor pressure from temperature and compare it with the actual vapor pressure.
WHEN	para 182	• If RH exceeds 100% in a numerical step, relax supersaturation by condensing vapor into droplets rather than allowing uncontrolled excess moisture. In a simple model, use a timescale; in a microphysical model, use droplet growth and aerosol activation.
WHEN	para 183	• If RH falls below 100%, evaporate liquid droplets subject to the available liquid water and a size-dependent evaporation rate.
RECORD	para 184	• Advect fog-water and spray-droplet fields with wind and mix them with a turbulence term. Add source terms for water-body fog, waves, falls, jets, and spray impacts.
DEFINE	para 185	• Convert the evolving droplet distribution to optical extinction. Do not map visibility directly from RH alone; high RH can occur without visible fog if liquid droplets are absent.
RECORD	para 186	10.2 A conceptual fog-water budget
RECORD	para 187	Change in airborne liquid water = condensation growth + injected spray - evaporation - deposition - precipitation loss + net transport.
RECORD	para 188	Change in vapor = surface evaporation and vapor advection - condensation - mixing losses or gains.
RECORD	para 189	The same water can move between vapor, suspended droplets, surface water, and aerosol residue. Tracking only a “fog density” slider skips the mechanism that should create and remove it.
RECORD	FOG, MIST, FOAM & AEROSOL DISPERSION	FOG, MIST, FOAM & AEROSOL DISPERSION 12
RECORD	Tier What it resolves Best suited for	Tier What it resolves Best suited for
RECORD	para 193	Tier 1: artistically controlled Fog density and height, wind drift, fade with warming. A stylized scene with physically sensible controls.
RECORD	para 194	Tier 2: thermodynamic Temperature, humidity, dew point, surface water temperature, advection and mixing. Ground fog, lake fog, low cloud bases, morning dissipation.
RECORD	para 195	Tier 3: microphysical Aerosol activation, droplet size distribution, LWC, collision/coalescence, detailed extinction. Research-grade appearance and size-dependent optics.
RECORD	para 196	Tier 4: coupled fluid/source model Wave breaking, bubbles, foam lifetime, spray production, aerosol evaporation and deposition. Surf, waterfalls, rivers, fountains, and sea-spray interaction.
RECORD	para 197	11. Field scenarios: infer the mechanism
RECORD	Observation Likely mechanisms Useful checks	Observation Likely mechanisms Useful checks
WHEN	A white plume streams downwind	A white plume streams downwind from a lake on a cold morning. Steam/evaporation fog, possibly mixed with water spray if wind is rough. Water warmer than air? Very cold surface layer? Does plume follow wind and thin as it mixes?
RECORD	A broad fog bank drifts inland from	A broad fog bank drifts inland from a cold coast. Advection / sea fog. Moist air crossing colder water; low stratus-like top; wind carries it ashore.
DEFINE	A thin fog layer sits in a valley at	A thin fog layer sits in a valley at dawn while ridges are clear. Radiation and valley fog, cold-air drainage. Clear night, light wind, wet ground, inversion, dew-point spread.
ALLOW	The air above a waterfall looks	The air above a waterfall looks smoky, even in dry weather. Mechanical spray from droplet breakup; local cooling/condensation may add fog. Visible close to impact, droplets wet nearby surfaces, plume decays downwind.
RECORD	Brown foam accumulates at a	Brown foam accumulates at a shoreline after strong surf. Gas bubbles stabilized by dissolved/suspended organic matter or other surface-active material. Foam is on the liquid surface; color alone cannot identify chemistry or hazard.
RECORD	Sea spray looks like fog above	Sea spray looks like fog above whitecaps but vanishes quickly. Spray droplets evaporating in unsaturated air, with residual aerosol possible. Humidity and temperature; droplet size; salt residue; wave and wind conditions.
RECORD	Fog forms during steady rain below	Fog forms during steady rain below a warm front. Precipitation-induced fog: evaporation moistens cooler low-level air. Ongoing rain, shallow cool air, high RH; not dependent on nighttime radiative cooling.
RECORD	A visible plume appears but	A visible plume appears but humidity is well below saturation. Mechanical spray, dust, smoke, or another aerosol is more plausible than condensation fog. Check source, droplet deposition, particle composition, and vapor conditions.
RECORD	para 207	11.1 Quick diagnostic order
RECORD	para 208	• First, locate the source: surface cooling, warm water, rain, waves, a waterfall, or a jet?
RECORD	para 209	• Second, classify the pathway: condensation, mechanical breakup, bubble bursting, or a mixture?
RECORD	para 210	• Third, inspect the air-water contrast: air temperature, water temperature, dew point, RH, wind, and stability.
RECORD	para 211	• Fourth, infer transport and lifetime: droplet size, turbulent mixing, downwind dilution, evaporation, surface collection, and precipitation.
RECORD	para 212	• Finally, infer the optics: droplet number and size, humidity, lighting, background contrast, and any non-water aerosol residue.
DEFINE	para 213	The visual category is the last step, not the first. Starting from mechanism is more reliable than choosing “fog” because the plume happens to look white.
RECORD	FOG, MIST, FOAM & AEROSOL DISPERSION	FOG, MIST, FOAM & AEROSOL DISPERSION 13
