DEFINE	para 236	• Fog is an airborne droplet field created by saturation and condensation; spray is mechanically broken liquid; foam is gas bubbles contained by liquid films.
DEFINE	para 237	• Ground fog is commonly connected to radiative cooling and terrain pooling, but the label describes where it sits rather than proving its cause.
RECORD	para 238	• Cold water can cool moist air into advection fog. Warm water can moisten cold air into steam or evaporation fog. Both can occur near the same shoreline under changing conditions.
ALLOW	para 239	• Breaking waves and bubbles make sea-spray aerosols; foam does not simply turn into a fog bank, though bubble bursting ejects droplets that may later evaporate or participate in aerosol and cloud processes.
RECORD	para 240	• Fog disperses through evaporation, mixing, advection, changing surface fluxes, deposition, and sometimes drizzle or freezing. Wind can clear, create, or transport fog depending on the air mass and source boundary.
RECORD	para 241	• For physical simulation, track temperature, vapor, aerosol nuclei, droplet water, droplet size, sources, transport, and optics separately enough that each process has a plausible cause.
RECORD	para 242	Scope note: This is an educational synthesis, not a substitute for aviation, marine, or road-visibility forecasts. Actual atmospheric fog and sea-spray production depend on multiscale turbulence and droplet microphysics; several bubble-bursting and aerosol-production details remain active research topics.
